        "title": "Tianwan 8 completes key commissioning tests",
        "link": "https://www.world-nuclear-news.org/articles/tianwan-8-completes-key-commissioning-tests",
        "source": "World Nuclear News",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-18T20:19:42.488176+00:00",
        "image": "https://world-nuclear-news.org/images/articles/Tianwan8hottests(CNNC)_36151.jpg",
        "full_content": "Such tests are carried out to confirm whether components and systems important to safety are properly installed and ready to operate in a cold condition. The main purpose of cold functional tests is to verify the leak-tightness of the primary circuit and components - such as pressure vessels, pipelines and valves of both the nuclear and conventional islands - and to clean the main circulation pipes. The tests mark the first time the reactor systems are operated together with the auxiliary systems.\nCNNC said completion of the cold tests \"marks the full transition of Tianwan Nuclear Power Unit 8 from the installation phase to the commissioning phase, laying a solid foundation for subsequent hot functional tests and fuel loading\".\nHot functional tests involve increasing the temperature of the reactor coolant system and carrying out comprehensive tests to ensure that coolant circuits and safety systems are operating as they should. Carried out before the loading of nuclear fuel, such testing simulates the thermal working conditions of the power plant and verifies that nuclear island and conventional equipment and systems meet design requirements.\nTianwan units 7 and 8 (Image: CNNC)\nIn addition to hot tests, Tianwan 8 will subsequently undergo containment pressure testing, fuel loading, criticality testing, grid connection, and post-grid connection power platform testing.\nThe background\nIn June 2018, Russia and China signed agreements for the construction of two VVER-1200 reactors as units 7 and 8 of the Tianwan plant. Construction of unit 7 began in May 2021, with that of unit 8 starting in February 2022. The units are scheduled to be put into commercial operation in 2026 and 2027, respectively.\nThe Tianwan nuclear power plant is owned and operated by Jiangsu Nuclear Power Company, a joint venture between CNNC (50%), China Power Investment Corporation (30%) and Jiangsu Guoxin Group (20%).\nThe first four units at the Tianwan site - which began commercial operation between June 2007 and December 2018 - are Gidropress VVER units supplied by Russia. Units 5 and 6 both feature Chinese ACPR1000 reactors.\nIn January, CNNC announced that hot tests had been completed at unit 7 on 30 December.",
        "content": "Such tests are carried out to confirm whether components and systems important to safety are properly installed and ready to operate in a cold condition. The main purpose of cold functional tests is to verify the leak-tightness of the primary circuit and components - such as pressure vessels, pipelines and valves of both the nuclear and conventional islands - and to clean the main circulation pipe...",
