        "full_content": "An international conference held online Tuesday agreed to expand the catch quota for bluefin tuna weighing at least 30 kilograms in the Central and Western Pacific by about 25%.\nAt a meeting held in the city of Nagasaki last month, members of the international council on Pacific bluefin tuna resource management were close to an agreement on a plan to expand the quota that had been based on Japan’s proposal.\nThe plan was not adopted, however, as Mexico abruptly raised objections.\nLater, Japan held bilateral talks with the Latin American country and eventually obtained its support.\nPacific bluefin tuna catches have been robust in the areas where Japanese fishing boats operate. Japanese fishers have called for an expansion in the quota as they have had to scale down their operations or release some of their catches to abide by the cap.\nThe agreement allows an increase in the annual quota in the Central and Western Pacific to 14,836 tons in fiscal 2027 and fiscal 2028 from the current 11,869 tons.\nMeanwhile, the quota for fish weighing less than 30 kilograms will be cut by 6% to 4,823 tons.\nThe catch quota in the Eastern Pacific, near Mexico, will be expanded to 7,740 tons from 7,581 tons.",
