        "title": "Trump’s Exercise Cut Risks Turning South Korea-US Alliance Readiness Into a Bargaining Chip",
        "link": "https://thediplomat.com/2026/08/trumps-exercise-cut-risks-turning-south-korea-us-alliance-readiness-into-a-bargaining-chip/",
        "source": "Asia Defense – The Diplomat",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-18T19:44:08.474996+00:00",
        "image": "https://thediplomat.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/08/sizes/td-story-s-2/thediplomat_2026-08-18-022522.jpg",
        "full_content": "Hours before South Korea and the United States began Ulchi Freedom Shield on August 17, U.S. President Donald Trump ordered the Pentagon to “substantially reduce” the exercise. He called the drills “costly” and an “inappropriate and hostile” signal to North Korea, emphasized his “very good relationship” with North Korean leader Kim Jong Un, and linked his displeasure to Seoul’s refusal to join U.S. action against Iran. He noted in a post on Truth Social that the exercises were being scaled back, not scrapped entirely, only because “it is too late to cancel.”\nThe exercise nevertheless started as scheduled, while the Pentagon offered no public explanation of what would be cut. This ambiguity makes the immediate military consequences difficult to measure. It also reveals a more serious alliance problem. A recurring combined exercise, developed through months of bilateral planning against a nuclear-armed adversary, was recast overnight as an instrument of presidential diplomacy and leverage over an ally.\nOne shortened exercise – if indeed Ulchi Freedom Shield is scaled back – will not undo the South Korea-U.S. combined defense posture. Repeated uncertainty over whether future exercises will proceed, however, could weaken readiness, disrupt the transition of wartime operational control, and reduce confidence in alliance consultation. If Washington wants to use military restraint to reopen diplomacy with Pyongyang, it must do so in concert with Seoul and in return for meaningful North Korean restraint.\nThe Last-Minute Reduction\nUlchi Freedom Shield is an 11-day combined exercise involving about 18,000 South Korean personnel. It integrates computer-assisted command-post training with field exercises and this year incorporates lessons from recent wars, including drone operations, GPS disruption, and cyberattacks. Even before Trump’s order, the allies had planned 14 battalion-level or larger field exercises, down from 17 in 2025. The overall number of South Korean participants remained roughly unchanged.\nBecause the order – if a post on Truth Social can be called that – arrived only hours before the exercise began, canceling or redesigning the command-post phase would create more disruption than savings. The likelier near-term options are narrower U.S. participation, fewer supporting assets, or reductions in field events that have not yet occurred. Until detailed guidance emerges, it’s difficult to evaluate the actual impact.\nHowever, the value of Ulchi Freedom Shield cannot be counted only in troops or field events. It rehearses the machinery that would allow two national militaries to operate as one force in a crisis: command relationships, intelligence flows, reinforcement, logistics, missile defense, and civil-military coordination. Smaller training can mitigate gaps, but it cannot fully reproduce theater-level decision-making under pressure.\nUlchi Freedom Shield 2026 also has an unusually important institutional purpose. The allies planned to use it to assess agreed capabilities and systems supporting the conditions-based transition of wartime operational control. Seoul hoped that a successful assessment would permit certification of the future Combined Forces Command’s full operational capability and support discussion of a transition timeline later this year. A major last-minute change could therefore affect more than readiness; it could complicate an alliance project that Washington itself has long encouraged.\nThis exposes a contradiction in current U.S. policy. The 2026 U.S. National Defense Strategy calls for South Korea to take primary responsibility for deterring North Korea with “critical but more limited” U.S. support. That shift requires stronger combined planning and credible validation of South Korean command capacity. Exercises are one of the main mechanisms for transferring and testing greater responsibility. Curtailing them, especially without prior discussion, makes burden-sharing harder to implement, even as Washington demands more of it.\nTrump’s reference to Iran is even more damaging. He ended his post with a comment criticizing Seoul’s reluctance to join the Israeli-U.S. strikes on Iran. Trump himself called the Iran factor “somewhat unrelated (?)” (sic) to the decision to scale back exercises, only adding to the confusion.\nThe U.S.-ROK Mutual Defense Treaty is centered on “common danger” in “the Pacific area.” Any South Korean contribution to operations involving Iran must also account for domestic legal procedures, available forces, and immediate requirements on the peninsula.\nWashington can ask Seoul to contribute to wider security challenges, and South Korea should seriously consider requests involving shared interests such as maritime security and the protection of sea lanes. But conditioning joint exercises – a core mechanism of peninsula defense – on participation in an unrelated military campaign changes calls into question alliance solidarity.\nSeoul is increasing defense spending, expanding conventional capabilities, and accepting a larger role in deterring North Korea – all in line with Trump’s stated preferences. A demand for allies to take greater responsibility for their own defense is sustainable when paired with reliable U.S. support and predictable consultation. If U.S. participation can be reduced suddenly because Seoul declined an out-of-area request, South Korean policymakers will reasonably question whether alliance decisions are still governed by common strategy.\nThe Risk of Seoul Being Sidelined\nTrump has done this before. After the 2018 Singapore summit with Kim Jong Un, Trump surprised both South Korean and U.S. military officials by announcing the suspension of major exercises. The pause created space for diplomacy with North Korea, but it also produced a measurable degradation in combined-force readiness. Three Trump-Kim meetings ultimately had no lasting impact on curtailing North Korea’s nuclear weapons or ballistic missile development.\nCertainly military restraint can support diplomacy when it is coordinated, limited in time, and exchanged for reciprocal measures. The 2018 experience, however, shows the danger of granting a visible concession before North Korea agrees to make any concessions of its own.\nTrump may hope that a reduction in Ulchi Freedom Shield will help revive direct dialogue with Kim. South Korean President Lee Jae-myung also favors renewed engagement, and the presidential office responded cautiously by expressing hope that the “friendly” Kim-Trump relationship “will lead to meaningful US-North Korea dialogue.” A coordinated reduction could therefore support both governments’ diplomatic objectives.\nThe current strategic setting, however, is less favorable than it was in 2018. North Korea has deepened military cooperation with Russia. North Korean troops have gained battlefield experience in the Russia-Ukraine war, while Pyongyang continues to improve its missiles and has conducted two ballistic missile launches since August 6. Just before Ulchi Freedom Shield, North Korea threatened to strengthen its nuclear deterrent in response to the exercise. Trump just rewarded this behavior, even calling North Korea’s behavior in general “unthreatening and respectful.”\nMeanwhile, Pyongyang has recast inter-Korean relations through its “two hostile states” policy and consistently rejects all outreach from Seoul. Under these conditions, a U.S. concession made without prior allied agreement can reinforce Pyongyang’s preference for dealing directly with Washington while excluding Seoul. This is the dynamic South Koreans often call “Korea passing.” It is not an inevitable result of North Korea-U.S. diplomacy, but the risk grows when Washington demonstrates that it can alter combined military activity first and consult Seoul afterward.\nRestoring Joint Control of Alliance Decisions\nSeoul should avoid both a public confrontation with Trump and passive acceptance of an undefined order. The immediate priority is a joint political and military review that clarifies which activities are affected and protects the essential command-post functions, the operational control assessment, and training involving critical U.S. enablers. If some field events or U.S. deployments are reduced, the allies should identify compensatory training rather than allow temporary cuts to become an unplanned new baseline.\nThe two governments also need rules for future exercise adjustments. Any change intended to advance diplomacy should be jointly decided, time-limited, reversible, and linked to observable North Korean action. Exercise policy should remain separate from trade disputes and requests for South Korean participation in operations outside the peninsula. Washington may seek wider contributions from Seoul, but those discussions belong in their own strategic and legal framework.\nFor its part, South Korea should continue strengthening independent intelligence, command-and-control, missile defense, precision-strike, and sustainment capabilities. Greater self-reliance will make the alliance more resilient to changes in U.S. force posture. It cannot substitute for combined training, however, because deterrence depends on an adversary believing that U.S. and South Korean forces can fight together, not merely that each possesses capable weapons.\nThe alliance can absorb one late and partially implemented order. It will struggle if combined readiness becomes a bargaining chip that can be exchanged for diplomatic atmospherics or used to punish disagreement on unrelated issues. Trump’s decision could still become the opening to a coordinated diplomatic initiative. Without reciprocal North Korean steps and genuine consultation with Seoul, however, it is more likely to expose a widening gap between the alliance’s formal commitments and the way its most consequential decisions are made.",
        "content": "Hours before South Korea and the United States began Ulchi Freedom Shield on August 17, U.S. President Donald Trump ordered the Pentagon to “substantially reduce” the exercise. He called the drills “costly” and an “inappropriate and hostile” signal to North Korea, emphasized his “very good relationship” with North Korean leader Kim Jong Un, and linked his displeasure to Seoul’s refusal to join U.S...",
