        "image": "https://thediplomat.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/08/sizes/td-story-s-2/thediplomat_2026-08-18-140247.jpg",
        "full_content": "The daughter of a prominent Cambodian opposition figure has been appointed as special envoy to Prime Minister Hun Manet, after a string of high-level meetings late last week.\nThe appointment of Kem Monovithya, which was formalized by a royal decree yesterday, was granted at the request of Hun Manet and conferred on her a rank equivalent to minister.\nThe appointment was made after Monovithya held several meetings late last week, including one with Hun Manet and what CambojaNews described as a three-hour meeting with Manet’s father and predecessor Hun Sen, which also included her father Kem Sokha.\nOn Friday, Hun Manet posted on Facebook a photo of himself standing with Monovithya, describing her as his “sister.” He wrote, “As a new generation of politicians alike, there are some things we can collaborate on for the betterment of our country, especially on the international stage.”\nThe decree comes not long after Kem Sokha, the former leader of the now-banned Cambodia National Rescue Party (CNRP), received a royal pardon that quashed a 27-year prison sentence for treason.\nSokha had been in custody since his midnight arrest in 2017, after which he was charged with “conspiracy with a foreign power” under Article 443 of Cambodia’s criminal code. At the time of his arrest, Sokha was the acting president of the CNRP, which had performed well at national elections in 2013 and local polls earlier in 2017, eating into the majority of Hun Sen’s Cambodian People’s Party (CPP) and raising hopes of a genuine political opening.\nHun Manet’s remark about the “international stage,” and the fact that Monovithya is being appointed as a “special envoy,” would suggest that her role will be geared toward the international arena.\nThis would make sense. The Cambodian government has recently come under close foreign scrutiny for the giant online scamming centers that have metastasized over the past five years, some of which have been linked to prominent CPP-aligned tycoons and political figures. While the government is currently in the midst of what it describes as a far-reaching crackdown on scam centers, this has done little to reverse the serious impacts that this has had on the tourism sector and the investment climate more generally.\nThen there is the ongoing border dispute with Thailand, which erupted into fighting on two occasions last year and remains far from any kind of resolution. A ceasefire in late December has kept the peace, but the situation at the border remains tense due to the Thai military’s occupation of territories at various points along the land border.\nWhile the Cambodian government periodically issues statements protesting the Thai occupation of Cambodian territory, the issue has failed to gain much regional or global traction. In this context, it is plausible that Monovithya, who was publicly supportive of the government’s stance during last year’s clashes, will help rally international support for Phnom Penh’s position on the border.\nAs with Sokha’s pardon, however, one should not overinterpret what this means for the domestic political context. While Reuters described Monovithya’s appointment as “a rare concession to the political opposition,” there is little in the recent past to suggest that this presages a genuine political opening. Indeed, the use of royal pardons to quash politically motivated court rulings was a regular feature of Hun Sen’s more than 38 years in power, as a means of managing domestic political opposition and external opinion. This has continued under since his son took over the leadership in 2023.\n“Without concrete legal action and reopening of democratic space,” the Cambodian analyst Seng Vanly told CambojaNews, “these encounters remain high-profile political gestures rather than the groundwork for a broad political settlement.”\nThe CPP maintains an “open door” policy to most of its current and former opponents. However, the criteria for entry are that these opponents accept the reigning political dispensation, and divest themselves of independent political ambitions. The logical conclusion is that this apparent reconciliation marks not the beginning of a political thaw but an end to the Kem family as a source of meaningful political opposition in Cambodia. Sokha, 73, said just before receiving his pardon that he now wished “to leave politics behind and spend the rest of his life with his family.”\nIn some ways this is no surprise. Many are the opponents of the CPP who have decided that they would rather try to achieve their goals from within the system than be frozen out permanently, as is the case with Sokha’s former CNRP ally (one longtime rival) Sam Rainsy, who has spent the last decade in exile.\nPolitics remains an unpredictable business in Cambodia, as elsewhere, but one iron law pertains: namely, that any political liberalization will be enacted solely on the CPP’s terms, and in support of the party’s own political goals.",
