        "title": "How to Decide When an AI Tool Is Worth Keeping",
        "link": "https://www.nngroup.com/articles/prove-framework/?utm_source=rss&utm_medium=feed&utm_campaign=rss-syndication",
        "source": "NN/g latest articles and announcements",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-18T19:44:08.474101+00:00",
        "image": "https://media.nngroup.com/media/articles/opengraph_images/How_to_Decide_When_an_AI_Tool_Is_Worth_Keeping_1.png",
        "full_content": "Tech workers face constant pressure to adopt AI tools, with little guidance on how to tell whether a specific tool improves a specific type of work. This article introduces PROVE, a lightweight evaluation framework we teach at NN/G, and applies it to a real workflow.\nWhy It's Difficult to Evaluate AI Tools\nProduct teams hear from leadership that they need to move faster, use the tools the company has purchased, and keep up with every release, all while spending less money on AI and learning new tools without taking time away from their work. Under that kind of pressure, evaluating a new tool fails in two main ways:\n- We try a tool once, get a plausible result on a demo-friendly use case, and call it productive.\n- We try it on the wrong task, hit a frustrating limitation, and write it off completely.\nNeither failure addresses the real question: compared with how you already do this work, does the tool produce an outcome worth adopting?\nPROVE stands for Problem Alignment, Risk, Output Quality, Velocity, and Experience. The goal is to evaluate one tool against one task and end with a recommendation you can explain to a manager, teammate, client, or yourself. PROVE is not meant for organization-wide tool recommendations or for characterizing monetary costs for prolonged use. Instead, PROVE is a framework for a person deciding whether a tool is promising enough to keep using on a specific task. It is designed to alleviate the pressure to rapidly evaluate and adopt new AI tools for work.\nGemini Notebooks Example\nTo show how the framework works, I'll walk through an evaluation I ran on Google's Gemini Notebooks (formerly known as NotebookLM). Every week, I curate two to four articles worth sharing with colleagues and write a short editorial digest for our team's Slack channel; ideally, it's a paragraph of commentary on each piece, with links. I wanted to know whether Gemini Notebooks could draft that digest for me.\nProblem Alignment: Start with the Task, Not the Tool\nIt's tempting to open a new AI tool and start poking around. Exploration can be fun, but it won't tell you whether the tool is useful. Before you spend money, change a workflow, or ask colleagues to adopt anything, define the task first: what would this tool help you do?\nStart with a recurring task that takes meaningful time or effort, then ask whether the tool's capabilities match that task. A tool that looks impressive in a demo may have no useful role in your workflow, while a modest tool may remove a bottleneck you hit every week. AI doesn't exist in a vacuum; it competes against your current process: your current quality, speed, and tolerance for friction.\nGemini Notebooks Evaluation\nFor my needs, the digest task passed this check easily. It recurs weekly, so any improvement compounds. It doesn't demand publication-quality writing, since the audience is an internal Slack channel. And Gemini Notebooks' core mechanic (upload sources, ask it to synthesize across them) maps directly onto the work: I've already read and selected the articles; I need help turning them into a digest. I also already had access to it through our Google Workspace, so no procurement request was needed.\nRisk: Confirm You Can Use the Tool Responsibly\nBefore spending hours testing a tool, make sure it's appropriate for the data you plan to give it. Is the tool approved by your organization? What kinds of data or information will you put into it? Does the privacy policy permit that use? Are you handling personal information, confidential research data, unreleased strategy, or proprietary code?\nWhen someone is under pressure to adopt AI, creating a personal account or subscribing to an unapproved service can feel like an easy workaround. This practice (often called shadow AI) is widespread: UpGuard's research found that roughly 80% of employees admit to using AI tools their employer hasn't approved. ‘Widespread’ doesn't mean ‘safe.’ Using organizational data in an unapproved tool creates risks that can wipe out the time savings you were after.\nIf you can't use a tool responsibly for the task you have in mind, don't force it into the workflow. Use that finding to redirect the conversation: maybe the team needs a different approved tool, a limited use case with safe inputs, or a formal security review.\nGemini Notebooks Evaluation\nThe inputs to my AI digest workflow were published, public articles, so there's no participant data or client information. I still checked Gemini Notebooks' privacy documentation, which states that Workspace users' uploads and queries aren't reviewed by humans or used to train models. Low-sensitivity inputs, along with acceptable terms, meant the tool passed. If I were uploading research transcripts instead of public blog posts, this step would have ended the evaluation.\nOutput Quality: Benchmark Against Your Own Real Work\nOnce an AI tool meets a need and is safe to use, it's time to evaluate its output against a real benchmark. Pick an actual example of your own work and ask: is the tool's version better than, as good as, or good enough compared with what you already produce?\nQuality isn't one universal standard. A rough internal outline can be useful even if it doesn't sound like you; a client-facing recommendation has a higher bar. What matters is whether the output is good enough for this task and its stakes.\nGemini Notebooks Evaluation\nMy benchmark was a digest I had written and posted to Slack. I uploaded the same three sources: a 1983 white paper on the ironies of automation, a podcast episode, and a blog post about AI coding agents. Then I used Gemini Notebooks to produce a digest for the same audience. The output was accurate and complete: every claim traced to the right source, with no invented findings. But it read like a report, with numbered items and Summary/Significance labels, where my version read like a person talking to colleagues. The tool captured the content and missed the voice, so output quality wasn't perfect.\nVelocity: Measure Total Time, Not Generation Time\nA new AI tool can make one part of a task feel fast, but without a comparison point, it's hard to know whether the whole task is faster with AI or without. Compare the total time required with and without the tool: setup, prompting, reviewing, correcting errors, reformatting, and moving material between systems. A three-second generation can still create a thirty-minute editing job.\nThe inverse can also happen: Using a new AI tool may take longer than your usual approach but produce an outcome you couldn't have produced alone, and that trade can be worthwhile when the quality gain matters.\nGemini Notebooks Evaluation\nWriting the digest by hand takes me about 25 minutes. The Gemini Notebooks version took a little over 10 (including voice edits). Some of that time was learning curve, so I'd expect the second run to be even faster.\nExperience: Weigh the Friction that Recurs\nA tool can deliver high-quality output quickly but still fail in daily practice because it's difficult to use. Maybe it requires repetitive setup, forces you to shuttle content across browser tabs and file formats, or makes its results hard to review and trust. These small costs can compound over time, especially for frequent tasks.\nIn PROVE, we recommend separating one-time friction from recurring friction. It's reasonable for a new tool to require setup and practice, but it's less reasonable for it to lead to an awkward, error-prone workflow forever. The best AI tools fit the work well enough that people keep using them after the novelty wears off.\nGemini Notebooks Evaluation\nSetup friction was low, and the learning curve is mostly behind me; they're both one-time costs that amortize quickly for a weekly task. The recurring friction is workflow fragmentation: Gemini Notebooks adds a system to the middle of the task without removing one. When writing the update manually, the digest is three steps:\n- read the articles\n- write\n- post\nWith Gemini Notebooks, it becomes six:\n- read the articles\n- upload them to Gemini Notebooks\n- prompt the system one or more times\n- copy the draft out\n- reformat it for Slack\n- post\nI still select and read the articles myself, and I still edit and format in Slack; the tool sits between those two steps and costs me two extra handoffs every week. It's tolerable, but it's the reason this dimension of PROVE earned my lowest score.\nTurn the Evaluation into a Decision You Can Explain\nFor rating the dimensions of output quality, velocity, and experience, we recommend a simple five-point scale, where 3 means \"about the same as my current approach.\"\nMy Gemini Notebooks evaluation came out as follows.\n- Output quality: 4/5 –- Accurate and complete, but needs voice editing before posting\n- Velocity: 4/5 –- Roughly 60% faster on raw time; closer to even with a full voice edit\n- Experience: 3/5 –- Functional but not seamless; two extra handoffs every run\nScores reveal the pattern, but they don't replace judgment. A tool with strong quality but a weak experience may warrant a limited trial; a high total score may still be the wrong fit for a high-stakes task.\nWhether the evaluation is for yourself or a stakeholder, you should be able to answer four questions:\n- What did you evaluate? Name the tool and the specific task.\n- What did you find? State the most important quality, time, and workflow evidence.\n- What are you going to do? Keep using it, run a time-boxed trial, set it aside, or revisit it later.\n- What is the main caveat or next step? Name the condition that matters and what should happen next.\nHere's my Gemini Notebooks evaluation in that form:\nI evaluated Gemini Notebooks for drafting a weekly research digest for our team Slack channel. Compared with writing by hand, it produced accurate, complete drafts and cut drafting time from about 25 minutes to about 10, though the output needs voice editing before posting. I'm going to keep using Gemini Notebooks for this task through a one-month trial, because a first pass against a real benchmark showed enough quality and time gain to justify a longer test. The main caveat is that it writes like a report rather than a person, so the next step is a month of weekly use to confirm the time savings.\nThat structure gives your teammates enough information to understand the decision, and it keeps the claim proportional to the evidence: a single run on a single set of inputs is a screen, not a verdict. It also prevents a common overreach: an evaluation of one tool for one task is not evidence that the whole organization should use that tool for everything.\nUse Evidence in Both Directions\nPressure to use AI is real, but pressure is not evidence. If someone asks why you're not using a particular tool, you should be able to point to the task you tested, the tradeoffs you found, and the decision you reached: \"This tool didn't improve this work enough to justify its cost, risk, or friction.\" It also replaces a vague demand like \"use more AI\" with a better question: which parts of this workflow have we tested, and where did the evidence show improvement?\nSkepticism can become its own reflex, though. A framework should not become a way to protect every existing workflow from change. My Gemini Notebooks evaluation ended in a ‘yes,’ and the evidence made that ‘yes’ easy to defend. If a tool produces work that's good enough for the task, saves meaningful time, and fits how people work, it's likely worth keeping in your workflow, even if you feel personally resistant.\nTreat the outcome of a PROVE evaluation as a provisional decision, not a permanent one. Tools change, tasks change, and your own skills change. We recommend revisiting a tool after a few weeks of real use, especially when the tool updates or the workflow evolves. The eventual goal is a smaller set of tools you trust for work that demonstrably improves. We want to avoid a growing pile of AI that looks impressive but makes your work worse.",
        "content": "Tech workers face constant pressure to adopt AI tools, with little guidance on how to tell whether a specific tool improves a specific type of work. This article introduces PROVE, a lightweight evaluation framework we teach at NN/G, and applies it to a real workflow.\nWhy It's Difficult to Evaluate AI Tools\nProduct teams hear from leadership that they need to move faster, use the tools the company ...",
        "tags": [
            "General"
        ],
        "location": "Global",
        "human_time": "7m ago"
    },
    {
        "title": "Psychological Ownership: Own the Right Things",
        "link": "https://www.nngroup.com/articles/psychological-ownership/?utm_source=rss&utm_medium=feed&utm_campaign=rss-syndication",
        "source": "NN/g latest articles and announcements",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-18T19:44:08.474097+00:00",
        "image": "https://media.nngroup.com/media/articles/opengraph_images/Psychological_Ownership1.png",
        "full_content": "Feeling a sense of ownership over your work seems like a necessary quality for any passionate design professional. Many organizations promote ownership cultures and encourage employees to solve problems proactively. Designers who feel ownership are more motivated, take greater initiative, and find more job satisfaction.\nBut psychological ownership has drawbacks. Getting attached to the wrong things won’t fuel your dedication or performance; it’ll fuel your underperformance instead. Organizations often don’t talk about ownership with nuance, so let’s discuss how to notice when your sense of ownership has attached to something you can't (or shouldn't) control, and how to redirect it to a better target.\nWhat Is Psychological Ownership?\nReflect on your career. Have you ever experienced:\n- Relief when your colleagues leave all the project’s design work for you to decide?\n- Pride when presenting your design work or your research insights?\n- Defensiveness when receiving pushback from stakeholders?\n- Anger when you see your designs derailed or poorly executed?\nMoments like these are perfectly normal, and they don’t make you unprofessional if you’ve experienced them. But when left unexamined, they can make you susceptible to frustration, disillusionment, and eventually burnout. These are times where psychological ownership is at work.\nPsychological ownership is the attachment an employee may develop towards their output, role, projects, or organization, where they consider the target to belong to them, regardless of actual ownership.\nSources of Psychological Ownership\nResearch by Linn Van Dyne and Jon Pierce shows that psychological ownership develops from three sources:\n- Controlling something\n- Knowing it deeply\n- Investing your time and effort into it\nTypical design work readily supplies all 3 of the psychological-ownership sources mentioned above. You conduct research and create deliverables, you know the details behind the design better than anyone else on the team, and you pour your judgment and taste into it. It’s why designers can experience strong feelings of ownership so readily (and why low-fidelity approaches, which keeps artifacts disposable and cheap to change, are still worth including into your process).\nPerils of Psychological Ownership\nAccording to the research, psychological ownership can have real job benefits: higher motivation, enhanced stewardship and initiative, and greater satisfaction. It also can create real problems when it misfires: territorial defensiveness and poor collaboration, resistance to feedback and change, and reluctance to share information (which worsens the common-knowledge effect and harms team decision making).\nYou can’t redirect psychological ownership in a healthy way by merely:\n- Forcing yourself to feel differently about the job\n- Acquiescing to opinionated colleagues\n- Discarding your principles and work ethic (i.e., “I work for the income and not the outcome”)\nInstead, redirect the inputs that fuel ownership, like where you invest your time, what knowledge you develop, and what you choose to control. Picking more realistic and rewarding targets is how you can turn psychological ownership into a strength.\nWhen You’d Rather Work Alone\nHave you felt the quiet relief when requirements land in your lap and your teammates leave you to design? Ownership of your creative process and its resulting ideas can feel gratifying. But research by Markus Baer and Graham Brown suggests that strong ownership of an idea makes us discount alternatives and undervalue collaborative input. Early solo concepts are usually the most assumptive (and often not very good).\nAsk yourself:\n- Who besides me decides whether this design ships?\n- What knowledge do they have that would influence my decisions?\n- Am I protecting time to think or protecting my ideas from challenge?\nFocused solo time has legitimate uses and can be efficient for simple, low-risk tasks. But watch out for relief at the absence of other perspectives, because that's psychological ownership attaching to your authorship instead of the design's success.\nOwn a collaborative design process instead of exclusive authorship. Have a sketching session with your business or development colleagues before your concept hardens. Point out both the drawbacks and benefits of different approaches. Remember that good ideas can come from anyone, so be open to serving as a facilitator instead of contributing yourself. Identify positive aspects of others’ ideas and incorporate them into the solution whenever possible.\nWhen It’s “My” Prototype\nThere's nothing wrong with saying \"the prototype I put together\" if you did the work. You may even feel a sense of accomplishment for its clever workflow or elegant visual design. Beware when you present the work for review, though. That's when psychological ownership tempts you to frame the deliverable into an extension of yourself. Then it becomes something for you to protect rather than something the team decides on together.\nRemember that a prototype becomes real only through the team's combined efforts. It must be technically feasible, meet business objectives, and survive priorities beyond just optimizing for usability or quality.\nAsk yourself:\n- When I say \"my design,\" do I mean \"the work I contributed to address a need\" or \"my vision that I expect others to faithfully execute?\"\n- Would this design succeed if it can’t be built on time?\n- Would this design succeed if it doesn’t contribute positive business impact?\nOwn collective team goals, not your design goals alone. In a study of creative teams, Steven Gray and colleagues found that when the person who originated an idea actively sought their teammates' input, rather than guarding the idea as their own, the team was less prone to clash over ownership. So, during reviews and decision moments, deliberately reframe the work as the team's, and think beyond just user-experience concerns. Adjust your language to use more first-person-plural pronouns like \"we\" and \"our,\" and be careful with \"my.\" Notice how a designer can voice nondesign questions with colleagues or subtly nudge them to own more of the user experience themselves:\n- “How could we revise our design to handle the response delay from the API?”\n- “What is our contractual deadline with the customer for releasing this feature?”\n- “The prototype currently doesn’t explain the triggered error. How would our customers know where to get help?”\nLanguage that acknowledges shared ownership and attention that reaches past the interface to the team's business and technical goals prompts investment that promotes more effective ownership from others as well as yourself.\nWhen Feedback Feels Like an Attack\nA developer pushes back on your design in a meeting. Before you've consciously decided anything, you're reexplaining your rationale with more and more detail, certain that if they just understood your reasoning or respected your expertise, they'd agree with you. The meeting quickly escalates into an argument as nobody feels heard.\nSkepticism and opposition are challenges that all designers face in their career. When they happen unexpectedly, psychological ownership can make you feel threatened. Pause and consider:\n- Is my objection justified in user evidence or is it my preference?\n- Am I communicating understandably with my audience?\n- Do I accurately understand the feedback’s rationale and what it’s trying to achieve?\n- If a colleague had designed this exact experience, would I still be defending it this hard?\nSometimes strong ownership is what’s needed. For instance, when the change would harm users (like deceptive patterns), when accessibility would be cut for convenience, or when you have user evidence people are clearly ignoring. Don’t meekly yield for the sake of getting along. Thankfully, these are not common situations. When these feedback moments go poorly, they’re most often a symptom of the designer’s misplaced ownership in their artifacts or their perceived mandate as the “designer” that undermines their ability to listen, persuade, and educate.\nOwn guiding others to experience user insights instead of your authority over the design. Listen first and echo the concern back to confirm you understood it. Translate your reasoning into plain terms instead of design jargon meant to intimidate. When you and a colleague genuinely disagree and neither of you is sure, let the users settle it. Involve team members in discovery research, or put both approaches in front of real users and test them, instead of deciding by whoever argues the hardest. Admit when you don’t have the user evidence for your recommendation and gently help others realize there may be unknown consequences to their recommendation as well. Helping others directly witness tradeoffs' impact on users is a far more powerful form of influence than winning an argument. You stop trying to psychologically own the role and instead start owning the team's understanding of the users they are ostensibly trying to serve.\nWhen The Build Isn’t What You Designed\nYou sit down during the team’s sprint demo to review the most recently finished work. It soon dawns on you that the feature being demonstrated has substantially diverged from the designs you provided. The changes simplified the design, and many of the details you so carefully designed and documented were discarded. As you fume over why these developers don’t care about quality, you mourn the loss of what would have been a great portfolio case study.\nTwo things are happening here. First, you're claiming sole ownership of a collective effort, hoping for an impactful portfolio piece. Second, you're explaining the gap through the fundamental attribution error, a cognitive bias that assumes people's actions reflect their character (not caring about quality) rather than their situation, such as deadlines, constraints, competing priorities, or a crisis you don't know about. Unless you’re well connected with the team’s activity and its people, then you won’t have the full story in a meeting.\nAsk yourself:\n- What do I actually know about why the build diverged and what am I assuming?\n- Was there anything in my control during the sprint that I didn't utilize, such as hosting checkins or performing design QA?\nOwn monitoring impact and improving your process instead of your emotional reactions and inferences. It's okay to be angry over a disappointing result, but treat it as a cue to think constructively. Document the gaps as UX debt so they're visible and actionable. Follow up with the developers to learn what happened during the sprint. Reflect on what was yours to control, like your presence during the sprint or the designs you provided. Owning what you can control beats ruminating over a disappointing build you may be unable to change.\nWhat's Genuinely Yours to Own\nEverything so far has been about loosening your grip on poor ownership targets, which raises a fair question: what's left? More than you might think. Some things are yours outright, because no one else can take them away from you:\n- Your judgment: what you pay attention to, how you analyze it, and how you leverage your intuition\n- Your actions: how you interpret and respond to situations, feedback, and your reactive emotions\n- Your growth: the skills and craft you develop and continuously improve over a career\n- Your values: the principles that guide your choices, and your integrity when holding to them is difficult\n- Your boundaries: the limits you set on your time and effort, and your discipline maintaining them\n- Your relationships: the professional connections you foster with others, the mentors you seek out, and the communities you associate with\nPsychologically own these completely by taking personal responsibility for them, investing your time and effort in cultivating them, and mastering your ability to use them well. That’s far easier said than done. It can mean taking the initiative to:\n- Hone your judgment with a deliberate reflective practice like inkthinking, instead of repeating the same mistakes.\n- Take action to repair your relationship with a product manager after a heated disagreement, instead of quietly holding a grudge.\n- Invest in your growth by delivering a conference talk, even when public speaking scares you.\n- Hold to your values and push back on a deceptive or exploitative design, even when caving would be expedient.\n- Establish a boundary by setting your work hours with your team and then honoring it.\n- Nurture relationships by keeping in touch with the brilliant colleague who taught you everything you know about qualitative research.\nOther things, like a shipped design or whether your advice is heeded, are shared with the team and beyond your complete control. Own those more lightly.\nGrowing into the Right Kind of Ownership\nMisplaced psychological ownership is common, especially early in a career. Schools tend to teach idealized design processes without constraints, and often in a solo context where you can own everything in a project without consequence. But work life is a muddy mess of corporate politics, deadlines, technical realities, and business demands. Students are rarely given the advice they need to prepare for the transition.\nUnderstanding the right kinds of psychological ownership requires learning what to hold on to and what to let go. The exhausting way is attempting to own things that employers own or that teams own, which leads to frustration and seeking a different job hoping that “this time it’ll be different.” It probably won’t be.\nThe wiser way is to thoughtfully reflect on this article’s questions and become more self-aware. Honestly consider these questions:\n- What are the drawbacks and sacrifices of trying to own these things?\n- How successful have I really been at trying to own them?\n- Even if I think I’ve been successful, what will I do when my control inevitably ends because my company, team, or role change?\nIn addition to reflection, seek out mentorship, which can offer a seasoned outsider’s perspective on your choices. Good mentors can often candidly assess your approach in ways that your boss likely won’t.\nAnd design leaders, take heed: don't thoughtlessly echo corporate culture slogans of \"taking ownership\" with design staff, especially in a lower UX-maturity environment. Your most passionate junior people are the most vulnerable to attaching ownership to the wrong targets and stressing themselves out defending them. Coach them on these distinctions, because it could be the difference between an ownership culture that energizes your team and one that quietly breeds cynicism and costly attrition.\nConclusion\nDesigners are often told that they own the user experience, but that’s not the reality. Very little of it is under any one person's control in this industry, and thinking otherwise will inevitably cause stress.\nRedirect your psychological ownership fully toward what’s genuinely yours and lightly towards what’s shared. Own your judgment, your actions, your career growth, your values, your boundaries, and your relationships. You can’t switch the feeling off, but you can influence what it holds on to, so the same instinct that would have worn you down starts building you up instead. That choice is always yours to make.\nReferences\nLinn Van Dyne and Jon L. Pierce. 2004. Psychological ownership and feelings of possession: Three field studies predicting employee attitudes and organizational citizenship behavior. Journal of Organizational Behavior 25, 4 (April 2004), 439–459. DOI: https://dx.doi.org/10.1002/job.249\nLin Wang, Kenneth S. Law, Melody Jun Zhang, Yolanda Na Li, and Yongyi Liang. 2019. It’s mine! Psychological ownership of one’s job explains positive and negative workplace outcomes of job engagement. Journal of Applied Psychology 104, 2 (February 2019), 229–246. DOI: https://dx.doi.org/10.1037/apl0000337\nMarkus Baer and Graham Brown. 2012. Blind in one eye: How psychological ownership of ideas affects the types of suggestions people adopt. Organizational Behavior and Human Decision Processes 118, 1 (May 2012), 60–71. DOI: https://dx.doi.org/10.1016/j.obhdp.2012.01.003\nSteven M. Gray, Andrew P. Knight, Markus Baer. 2019. On the Emergence of Collective Psychological Ownership in New Creative Teams. Organization Science 31(1):141-164. DOI: https://doi.org/10.1287/orsc.2019.1307",
        "content": "Feeling a sense of ownership over your work seems like a necessary quality for any passionate design professional. Many organizations promote ownership cultures and encourage employees to solve problems proactively. Designers who feel ownership are more motivated, take greater initiative, and find more job satisfaction.\nBut psychological ownership has drawbacks. Getting attached to the wrong thing...",
