        "title": "The problem(s) with Hegseth and other Cabinet members hitting the campaign trail",
        "link": "https://www.ms.now/rachel-maddow-show/maddowblog/hegseth-kennedy-iowa-cabinet-secretaries-midterms",
        "source": "MS NOW",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-18T19:44:08.472348+00:00",
        "image": "https://www.ms.now/wp-content/uploads/2026/08/260818-pete-hegseth-secretary-of-defense-war-campaign-trail-campaigning-iowa-3rd-congressional-district-republican-gop-zach-nunn-des-moine-es.webp?crop=0px,21px,1334px,700px&w=1200&h=630",
        "full_content": "A few months ago, as part of Donald Trump’s relentless crusade against Republican Rep. Thomas Massie, Defense Secretary Pete Hegseth took the extraordinary step of hitting the campaign trail, appearing in Kentucky to condemn the GOP congressman ahead of his primary race.\nThis week, it happened again. The New York Times reported:\nDefense Secretary Pete Hegseth campaigned on Monday in Iowa for Representative Zach Nunn, stepping into the political arena in a way previous leaders of the Pentagon have avoided.\nMr. Hegseth appeared at a veteran-focused fund-raising event at the Iowa State Fair alongside Mr. Nunn, who is one of the most vulnerable House Republicans in this year’s midterms. He touted Mr. Nunn’s candidacy and was sharply critical of previous Democratic administrations.\nThere’s a lot to unpack here, so let’s consider this from a handful of different angles.\nThis isn’t what defense secretaries are supposed to do: When the Pentagon hit the campaign trail in March, a separate Times report explained, “The campaign appearance was an extraordinary breach of military decorum — even for Mr. Hegseth, who has stretched the boundaries of partisan politics in his job leading the Pentagon. Previous defense secretaries and other Pentagon officials typically stay far away from such activities to avoid even the appearance of partisanship.”\nHegseth, a relentless partisan, is blazing a new trail that does lasting harm to his office, its credibility and the Defense Department.\nDoesn’t Hegseth have other things to do right now? If the defense secretary invested less time in the midterms and more time in the failing war with Iran and the service members aboard the USS Lincoln, the nation would be far better off.\nThe broader problem isn’t limited to Hegseth: Health and Human Services Secretary Robert F. Kennedy Jr. has spent recent months making appearances with vulnerable congressional Republicans; Attorney General Todd Blanche spoke at a political event late last week; Hegseth has already campaigned in Kentucky and Iowa; and there are related plans for other Cabinet secretaries as Election Day approaches.",
