        "full_content": "Today’s installment of campaign-related news items from across the country.\n* Republican Sen. Susan Collins of Maine has long described herself as a proponent of reproductive rights, but after the senator’s votes to confirm Donald Trump’s Supreme Court nominees, who ultimately voted to overturn Roe v. Wade, some of her former allies have turned against her.\nThis week, for example, Planned Parenthood’s political action committee announced a new ad campaign targeting Collins in her home state. The $600,000 investment is the largest investment Planned Parenthood Votes has made in any state this election cycle.\nAround this time nine years ago, Planned Parenthood gave the Maine senator an award in recognition of her support for women’s health, but as Collins’ critics keep arguing, things have changed quite a bit since 2017.\n* Though the details are still taking shape, Harmeet Dhillon, the controversial assistant attorney general who leads the Justice Department’s Civil Rights Division, told Bloomberg News this week that the DOJ intends to deploy roughly 1,000 election monitors to local precincts this fall.",
