        "full_content": "Under federal law, only American citizens can vote in American elections. But that didn’t stop House Speaker Mike Johnson from unveiling legislation a couple of years ago to ban noncitizens from casting ballots that they already can’t cast.\nAsked at a press conference about the need for his pointless proposal, the Louisiana Republican replied, “We all know intuitively that a lot of illegals are voting in federal elections.”\nThe House speaker’s use of the word “intuitively” stood out, in part because Johnson so frequently relies on evidence-free hunches to guide his policy preferences, especially when it comes to election denialism, but also because his answer captured a familiar sentiment in GOP circles: Republicans don’t know that noncitizens are voting, but they have insisted for years that it feels true to them.\nIt’s like watching an entire political party embrace “truthiness,” a word made popular by Stephen Colbert’s old Comedy Central persona.\nLike Johnson, Texas Attorney General Ken Paxton has also long assumed that noncitizens are voting in the Lone Star State, and the scandal-plagued Republican even launched an effort to hold these ineligible voters accountable. It just hasn’t worked out especially well. NBC News reported:\nFor more than a year, Texas attorney general and GOP Senate nominee Ken Paxton has been probing what he says is the large number of noncitizens who illegally voted in his state. So far, three people have been prosecuted, according to an NBC News review of court documents, public records and statements.\nTo be sure, others have been investigated as part of this same effort, but according to NBC News’ report (which has not been independently verified by MS NOW), some of those who were targeted proved to be American citizens.",
