        "full_content": "There was a bizarre moment at a White House Cabinet meeting in May when Donald Trump was asked about Oman playing the role of a diplomatic interlocutor between the United States and Iran.\n“Oman will behave just like everyone else, or we’ll have to blow them up,” the president said.\nIt wasn’t altogether clear why he made such a threat. There was also some question as to whether Trump realized that Oman is a longtime U.S. ally. But shortly after the Cabinet meeting, Trump’s State Department nevertheless used social media to promote and amplify the president’s comments.\nIn the weeks that followed, Secretary of State Marco Rubio tried to ease tensions, declaring publicly that the U.S. relationship with Oman “is fine,” the American president’s threat to “blow them up” notwithstanding.\nThat was in June. Two months later, “fine” does not appear to be the best adjective to describe relations between the two countries.\nAs MS NOW’s Hayley Meissner noted, Trump began the week telling Fox News that he’s prepared to “bomb the s—” out of Oman if the country gets “in the way.” In response to Trump’s threat, a senior Middle East diplomat with direct knowledge of the Iran-Oman talks told MS NOW that Oman’s “primary objective” is working toward a “solution that leads to” the reopening of the strait.\nSoon after, at an unrelated White House event, a reporter asked Trump about his latest threat.",
