    {
        "title": "Franno’s last lap",
        "link": "https://www.jamaicaobserver.com/2026/08/16/frannos-last-lap/",
        "source": "Jamaica Observer",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-18T19:44:08.471822+00:00",
        "image": "https://www.jamaicaobserver.com/jamaicaobserver/news/wp-content/uploads/sites/4/2026/08/b3163880a56252043e883527864726a5-1024x683.jpg",
        "full_content": "Franno’s last lap\nWorld beaters, grateful nation bid legendary track and field coach goodbye\nHE spent a lifetime on the sidelines, stopwatch and clipboard in hand, watching athletes take their places on the track, but at the thanksgiving service for his life on Saturday, Vincent Stephen “Franno” Francis was in the spotlight. He made his final lap down a red-carpeted track, his cremains carried in a platinum urn as athletes he had trained and mentored formed a guard of honour — a fitting tribute for the legendary Jamaican track and field coach who produced world beaters.\nThe emotion was unmistakable as athletes fought back tears while ushering Francis out of the National Indoor Sports Centre, a stone’s throw from the National Stadium where many of them had delivered some of their greatest performances. This time, however, there was no race to prepare for, no stopwatch to start, and no final instructions to give, only the painful realisation that they were saying goodbye to the coach who had spent a lifetime helping them find their way to the finish line, and now they were returning the favour.\nFor approximately five minutes, sprint legends Shelly-Ann Fraser-Pryce, Shericka Jackson, Kishane Thompson, Elaine Thompson Herah, and many more who spent years being taught the art and science of sprinting stood still as family members, friends, Government officials, and colleagues looked on, reminiscing on the life of a world-class coach.\nA nod to his alma mater, Wolmer’s Boys’ School, the sports centre and many attendees were decked in maroon and gold for the four-hour-long service that was enveloped in an atmosphere that swung from grief to celebration.\nPot covers echoed during tributes about Francis’s excellent track record, tears flowed when travelling down memory lane, laughter filled the room at comical moments, and musical tributes and selections provided much-needed comfort.\nIn his tribute, delivered by Finance Minister Fayval Williams, Prime Minister Dr Andrew Holness praised Francis for demonstrating what was possible when natural talent was matched with world-class coaching, structure, and preparation. He noted that, through the formation of the Maximising Velocity and Power (MVP) Track Club, Francis helped create a pathway for Jamaican athletes to develop at home, at a time when many were leaving the island in search of training opportunities abroad.\n“Athletes under his guidance became Olympic and World champions, record holders and household names. Yet his influence cannot be measured by counting medals alone. He developed people, he established standards, and he challenged athletes to become more disciplined, more prepared, and more demanding of themselves,” wrote Holness.\n“He also helped prove that a Jamaican coaching programme could consistently produce performances of the highest international standard. In that sense, his impact reached well beyond MVP. He contributed to the evolution of the Jamaican coaching profession and to a stronger national system of athlete development,” he said.\nIt was for these reasons that Sports Minister Olivia Grange urged the congregation not to mourn, but to celebrate the man who possessed an extraordinary vision backed by purpose and confidence that what he was doing would bring the results that he envisioned.\n“Francis understood that talent was only the beginning. Talent had to be developed, potential had to be channelled, and dreams had to be matched by commitment. That philosophy, my brothers and sisters, helped to shape athletes and contributed significantly to Jamaica’s remarkable place in world athletics. Francis possessed technical mastery. He studied speed like a scientist. Above all, he had a strong belief in Jamaica and the potential of our people,” said Grange.\nShe added that to honour his legacy, the Government is in discussions to provide student-athletes at the University of Technology, Jamaica, the home of the MVP track club, a scholarship in his name.\nThe move was welcomed by president of the university Dr Kevin Brown, who thanked Francis for choosing the campus as his training ground and placing the institution on the map, one athlete at a time. He remembered Francis for his bluntness, discipline, and belief in the potential of Jamaica, joking that he never wasted words and advocated fiercely for his athletes.\nAn emotional Brigitte Foster-Hylton and Asafa Powell, two sprint legends who reaped the rewards of Francis’s brilliance, stood as living evidence of his legacy, though struggling to steady their voices as they fought back tears. Between the emotion, they recalled cherished memories, from dodging train conductors in Italy to travelling in Francis’s old car, thanking the coach who had taken a chance on them, believed in their potential, and pushed them towards global success.\n“Everything I became started with one man who saw something in a young man before anyone else did,” said Powell, Foster-Hylton finishing his speech as he broke down in tears.\nShe thanked Francis for every lesson, sacrifice, laugh, and opportunity, noting that he did not just change her life, he changed countless lives.\n“Rest well, Franno. Your whistle might be silent, but your legacy lives on,” she said through tears.\nEulogised by his brother, Paul, who also spoke through shaky breaths, the decorated coach was remembered as a friend, a loyalist, a protector of his athletes, a family man, and a coach.\nAdrian Laidlaw wiped away tears as he declared that delivering a tribute to his best friend was the hardest job he was ever tasked with. He said when Francis closed his eyes on July 4, 2026, a piece of Jamaica left with him, and his legacy will always be remembered.\nRecounting cherished memories of a man he described as both mentor and friend, MVP President Bruce James said Francis made countless sacrifices in his mission to put Jamaica firmly on the international athletics stage, remaining committed to the vision even in the face of doubt.\nJames recalled that Francis’s dedication to his athletes never wavered, even from his hospital bed, where he continued asking for updates on their progress — a final testament to a coach whose commitment to his craft and athletes endured to the very end.\n“History has proven you right, Franno. The World records, the Olympic records, the World champions, the Olympic champions, the world athletes of the year, the numerous athletes who won medals on the global stage achieved more than they ever thought possible and became better versions of themselves because they were a part of MVP,” said James in a written tribute.",
        "content": "Franno’s last lap\nWorld beaters, grateful nation bid legendary track and field coach goodbye\nHE spent a lifetime on the sidelines, stopwatch and clipboard in hand, watching athletes take their places on the track, but at the thanksgiving service for his life on Saturday, Vincent Stephen “Franno” Francis was in the spotlight. He made his final lap down a red-carpeted track, his cremains carried in ...",
