        "title": "The Wedding Guest List Dilemma",
        "link": "https://www.jamaicaobserver.com/2026/08/18/wedding-guest-list-dilemma/",
        "source": "Jamaica Observer",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-18T19:44:08.471778+00:00",
        "image": "https://www.jamaicaobserver.com/jamaicaobserver/news/wp-content/uploads/sites/4/2026/08/image_1-283-1024x594.jpg",
        "full_content": "The Wedding Guest List Dilemma\nCreating the wedding guest list sounds simple — until you actually have to do it. For today’s couples, weddings are becoming more personal, intentional and experience-driven. Many couples would rather host 80 people they genuinely love than invite 200 because tradition says they should. But weddings are also one of those rare occasions when family, culture, relationships and etiquette all collide. So how do you create a guest list that reflects your wedding while still respecting the traditions and people that matter? The answer isn’t necessarily to abandon tradition. It’s to modernise it.\nStart With The Wedding You Can Actually Afford\nBefore names go onto a spreadsheet, establish your approximate guest count. Every additional guest potentially means another meal, chair, place setting, invitation, favour and portion of the bar budget. Depending on your wedding, it may also affect the size of the venue, transportation requirements and even the number of tables and floral arrangements required.\nYour guest list and your budget therefore have to be developed together. A 100-person wedding isn’t simply a 75-person wedding with 25 extra chairs.\nCreate Your Non-Negotiable List First\nStart with the people you cannot imagine celebrating without. For most couples, this includes immediate family, grandparents, closest friends and people who have played meaningful roles in their lives. Then create additional categories: Extended family, family friends, colleagues, parents’ friends and social acquaintances. This makes it much easier to see where your numbers are going before invitations become emotional decisions.\nYes, Parents Should Have Some Input\nThis is where modern wedding planning sometimes clashes with traditional etiquette. The wedding belongs to the couple, but marriage also represents the joining of families. Parents may have relatives, lifelong friends or important family relationships they genuinely believe should be acknowledged. Listen to them.\nThat doesn’t mean handing over half your guest list. Give each family an agreed number of invitations or ask them to submit a priority list. This allows parents to feel included without allowing the guest count to grow uncontrollably. If parents are making a significant financial contribution to the wedding, their expectations should ideally be discussed before the guest list is finalised.\nRespect works both ways: Parents should understand the couple’s vision and limitations, and couples should recognise that some invitations carry family significance beyond their own personal relationships.\nThe Modern Rule: Relationship Over Obligation\nThere was once an expectation that if someone invited you to their wedding, you should invite them to yours. That’s no longer necessary. Perhaps their wedding was eight years ago and you haven’t spoken in five. Sometimes relationships change over time.\nI have a rule of thumb: Ask yourself these two questions 1. If I weren’t getting married, would this person still be an active part of my life? 2. Do you think they will still be a part of your life in the next five years? Those questions can eliminate quite a few names. Your wedding shouldn’t become a reunion of everyone you’ve ever known simply because you’re worried someone might be offended.\nBut Don’t Forget Basic Courtesy\nBeing modern doesn’t mean abandoning good manners however. If you regularly socialise with a group of six friends, inviting five and excluding one will probably create unnecessary hurt. The same applies to very close family units. You don’t have to invite everyone from every social circle, but consider how your choices will feel from the other side. There is a difference between setting boundaries and being unnecessarily inconsiderate.\nWhat About Plus-Ones?\nThis is one of the biggest guest-list questions. Generally, spouses, fiancés and established partners should be invited together — even if you haven’t personally met the partner. For single guests, however, you don’t necessarily have to offer everyone an unrestricted plus-one. If space and budget allow, it’s a lovely gesture, particularly for guests travelling alone or who won’t know many people at the wedding. Whatever rule you choose, try to apply it consistently.\nChildren or No Children?\nAn adults-only wedding is perfectly acceptable today, but communicate it clearly and graciously. You might choose to invite children only from immediate family or the wedding party. That’s also acceptable, provided the rule has some logic and isn’t being selectively applied to individual families. Give parents plenty of notice, particularly for a destination wedding where child-care arrangements may be more complicated.\nDon’t Forget the Destination Wedding Reality\nFor destination weddings, the guest list requires another level of consideration. Inviting someone doesn’t mean they will automatically attend. Guests may need to purchase flights, book accommodation, take time away from work and possibly arrange childcare. Send save-the-dates early and avoid assuming who can or cannot afford to attend. Let guests make that decision for themselves. And please don’t invite someone simply because you believe they won’t come. They just might!\nBe Careful With the B-List\nHaving a secondary list isn’t inherently wrong. Venues have capacities and couples have budgets.The problem comes when guests realise they were obviously an afterthought.\nIf you’re working with invitation rounds, send your first invitations early enough to receive responses before approaching the next group. Avoid sending a second wave suspiciously close to the wedding date unless circumstances genuinely changed. Nobody wants to feel like they were invited because somebody else cancelled.\nConsider the People Who Have Been Part of the Journey\nSometimes couples become so focused on immediate friends that they overlook people who have genuinely contributed to their lives. The aunt who helped raise you. Your parents’ best friends who have known you since childhood. The mentor who helped shape your career. The family friend who has been present for every major milestone.\nTradition can sometimes remind us of something valuable: a wedding isn’t only about who you socialise with right now. It can also honour the people who helped you become who you are.\nThat doesn’t mean inviting everyone your parents have ever met. It means looking at your relationships with a little perspective.\nAnd What About Co-workers?\nYou don’t have to invite the entire office. If colleagues have become genuine friends outside of work, invite them because of the friendship — not because you share an employer. If you’re inviting only a few people from a larger workplace, discretion is important. Wedding invitations don’t need to become lunchtime office announcements.\nThe Final Test\nBefore finalising your list, look at each name and ask: Do we genuinely want this person there? Is there an important family or cultural reason they should be included? Will excluding them create a significant and legitimate family issue? Are we inviting them because we want to — or purely because we feel guilty? Not every answer will be straightforward, and that’s okay.\nToday’s couples should absolutely feel empowered to create weddings that reflect who they are. Smaller weddings, adults-only celebrations, limited plus-ones and carefully curated guest lists are all perfectly reasonable.\nBut in our desire to make weddings more personal, we shouldn’t lose the graciousness that has always been at the heart of good wedding etiquette.\nYour wedding is your celebration, but it is also a moment shared with family and community.\nThe best guest lists find room for both.\nInvite with intention. Set boundaries with kindness. Honour the relationships that deserve honouring. And when tradition still has meaning, don’t be afraid to keep it.\nShikima Hinds\nManaging Director\nShikima Hinds Events Concierge\nTel. 876-925-4285 or 876-361-0910\nEmail: shikima@shikimahinds.com\nwww.shikimahinds.com",
