    {
        "title": "Trump wants America to vote like India. But will Indian model work?",
        "link": "https://economictimes.indiatimes.com/news/india/trump-wants-america-to-vote-like-india-but-will-indian-model-work/articleshow/133327139.cms",
        "source": "India-News-Economic Times",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-18T19:44:08.471735+00:00",
        "image": "https://img.etimg.com/thumb/msid-133327267,width-1200,height-630,imgsize-155208,overlay-economictimes/articleshow.jpg",
        "full_content": "In a Truth Social post on August 18, Trump highlighted India’s voter identification requirements and low reliance on mail voting as evidence that large democracies can run elections with tighter controls.\n“Earlier this month, India’s Chief Election Commissioner, Gyanesh Kumar, asked our Administration — How can you have Elections in the U.S. without a valid Photo ID? India’s last Election had 646,000,000 Voters! Less than 1% voted by mail, and every single Voter had to have a VALID Photo Identity Document. We need to pass THE SAVE AMERICA ACT, now! President DONALD J. TRUMP,” he wrote on Truth Social.\nALSO READ | Trump, Gor praise India's voter ID system to push SAVE America Act\nThe remarks came as Republicans intensify their push for the SAVE America Act, a proposal that would require documentary proof of US citizenship for voter registration and impose stricter identification requirements. The comparison is politically useful for Trump because it allows him to frame voter ID rules not as a uniquely Republican demand but as a practice already followed by another giant democracy.\nWhy Trump is pointing to India\nTrump’s argument rests on two facts. First, India requires voters to verify their identity before voting, typically through the Electoral Photo Identity Card (EPIC) or another approved government document. Second, India conducts elections overwhelmingly through in-person voting, with postal ballots limited to specific categories of voters such as military personnel, election staff, some senior citizens and persons with disabilities.\nFor Republicans, India offers a powerful example because it combines strict voter verification with massive participation. More than 640 million Indians voted in the 2024 Lok Sabha election, making it the largest election ever conducted. Trump's argument is that if a country with a vastly larger electorate can require identity verification at polling stations, the US should be able to do the same.\nDemocrats and Trump's other opponents argue that the comparison overlooks fundamental structural differences. India has a dedicated voter identity document issued through a national election system. The US has no national voter card, no national population register and no single authority overseeing elections. Instead, election administration is fragmented across states and counties.\nHow broken has been America’s voting system\nThe debate over voter identification cannot be understood without examining the history of voting rights in the US. After the Civil War, the 15th Amendment prohibited denying voting rights on the basis of race. Southern states responded by creating mechanisms that technically appeared race-neutral but were designed to suppress Black voters.\nOne of the most common tools was the literacy test. Prospective voters were required to read and interpret complex constitutional provisions. White election officials had broad discretion to decide who passed and who failed. In practice, educated Black citizens were often rejected while poorly educated white citizens were approved.\nAnother method was the poll tax, which required citizens to pay a fee before voting. For many poor Black Americans and poor whites, the tax effectively barred participation. Some states also used \"grandfather clauses\", allowing individuals to vote only if their grandfathers had been eligible voters before the Civil War, a requirement that excluded most Black citizens. Intimidation was also widespread. Black citizens seeking to register often faced threats, economic retaliation, arrests and violence. In some areas, employers fired workers who attempted to vote and local authorities actively obstructed registration efforts.\nThe Voting Rights Act of 1965 was designed specifically to dismantle these systems. It outlawed literacy tests, authorised federal examiners to register voters directly in jurisdictions with histories of discrimination and required certain states and localities to obtain federal approval before changing election rules. The impact was immediate. By the end of 1965, roughly 250,000 new Black voters had been registered, many through federal intervention. Within a year, Black voter registration rates rose dramatically across the South. The Act transformed voting rights enforcement by shifting significant authority from state officials to the federal government.\nThis history explains why voting regulations remain politically sensitive in the US. Many Democrats and civil rights organizations view new voting restrictions through the lens of past voter suppression campaigns, while Republicans view election security measures as necessary safeguards unrelated to race because they are a basic minimum for any democracy.\nThe turn that came in 2000 election\nThe modern election-integrity debate gained momentum after the 2000 presidential election between George W. Bush and Al Gore.\nThe election ultimately came down to Florida, where Bush's official victory margin was just 537 votes out of nearly six million cast. Election night descended into chaos. Television networks initially projected Florida for Gore, then for Bush, then declared it too close to call. Recounts followed and disputes emerged over punch-card ballots, \"hanging chads\" in ballot papers, voter roll errors and differing recount standards across counties. The controversy lasted more than a month and culminated in the Supreme Court's Bush v. Gore decision, which halted the statewide recount. Gore conceded the next day.\nThe Florida dispute exposed weaknesses in election administration. Questions arose about outdated voting technology, inconsistent recount procedures and the accuracy of voter rolls. Investigations also highlighted allegations that eligible voters had been wrongly removed from registration lists and that minority voters encountered disproportionate difficulties. Many thought that election procedures could influence outcomes in extremely close contests.\nIn the years that followed, states increasingly adopted voter identification requirements and upgraded voting systems. The debate shifted from ballot design and recount procedures to questions about voter verification.\nAmerica's voting system had already been mechanised long before the 2000 election. Mechanical lever machines, first introduced in the 1890s, became widespread during the 20th century, while punch-card and optical-scan systems emerged from the 1960s onward. The Florida recount exposed the weaknesses of punch-card voting in particular, helping trigger the Help America Vote Act of 2002, which accelerated the replacement of punch-card and lever systems with optical scanners and electronic machines.\nWhy 2020 intensified the dispute\nThe 2020 election elevated election security from a policy issue to a central political fault line.\nBecause of the Covid-19 pandemic, states dramatically expanded mail voting and absentee voting options. More than 65 million Americans voted by mail. Trump repeatedly argued that expanded mail voting created opportunities for fraud and challenged the results in several battleground states after losing to Joe Biden.\nCourts, state election officials, the Department of Justice and numerous investigations found no evidence of fraud on a scale capable of changing the outcome. Nevertheless, Republican voters increasingly expressed concerns about election security. The party responded by making voter identification, citizenship verification and mail-voting restrictions major legislative priorities. The SAVE Act emerged from that political environment.\nHow voter identification actually works in America\nThe US does not have a single nationwide voting system. States establish their own procedures and requirements. Before voting, citizens must generally register. Federal registration forms require applicants to provide identifying information such as a driver's licence number, a state-issued identification number or the last four digits of a Social Security number. States verify that information against government databases.\nWhat happens on Election Day depends on the state. Some states require government-issued photo identification. Others accept non-photo documents such as utility bills, bank statements or government correspondence showing a voter's name and address. Several states allow voters to cast ballots merely after signing an affidavit affirming their identity. Some states also permit provisional ballots that are counted only after a voter later provides identification.\nThe result is a patchwork of rules. A voter in one state may be required to present a driver's licence while a voter in another state may simply confirm personal information already contained in registration records. This decentralisation is one reason comparisons with India are difficult. India operates under a nationally administered election framework while the United States delegates election administration largely to states and local governments.\nWhat the SAVE Act would change\nThe Safeguard American Voter Eligibility Act, or SAVE Act, would fundamentally alter federal voter registration requirements.\nThe bill would require documentary proof of US citizenship before a person could register to vote in federal elections. Acceptable documents would generally include a passport, a birth certificate combined with supporting identification or naturalisation papers.\nThe legislation would also require states to maintain programmes aimed at identifying non-citizens on voter rolls and removing them where appropriate. Election officials who fail to comply could face legal consequences and private lawsuits.\nSupporters say the bill merely enforces a basic principle that only citizens should be able to vote in federal elections. Republicans argue that existing registration systems rely too heavily on self-attestation and that stronger verification would improve public confidence in election outcomes.\nWhy Republicans are pushing it\nRepublicans have made the SAVE Act a central legislative priority because it addresses several issues that resonate strongly with their voters. First, it responds to concerns about non-citizen voting. Although federal law already prohibits non-citizens from voting in federal elections, Republicans argue that registration systems should require documentary proof rather than relying primarily on declarations made under penalty of perjury.\nSecond, it aligns with broader Republican efforts to standardize election security measures nationwide. Many Republican lawmakers argue that identification requirements should not vary dramatically from state to state.\nThird, the bill serves a political purpose. Election integrity remains one of the Republican Party's most effective mobilizing issues, particularly among voters who remain sceptical of existing election procedures after 2020.\nWhy the SAVE Act is stuck\nThe bill's biggest obstacle is the Senate. Although Republicans support the legislation overwhelmingly, Democrats have opposed it almost unanimously. Senate Democrats argue that documented cases of non-citizen voting are not common and that the bill would create significant barriers for eligible voters who lack readily available citizenship documents.\nCritics point out that millions of Americans do not possess passports. Others may struggle to obtain original birth certificates, particularly if they were born decades ago or have experienced name changes through marriage. Voting rights groups also warn that local election offices could face major administrative burdens verifying documents for millions of voters. Senate Democrats have therefore characterized the legislation as a solution in search of a problem.\nThe Senate's procedural rules create an additional challenge. Most major legislation requires 60 votes to overcome a filibuster. Unless Republicans can attract substantial Democratic support -- they have 53 votes -- the bill faces difficult odds despite strong backing from Trump and Republican leadership.\nCan the Bill pass before November?\nThat depends largely on the balance of power in Congress and the political environment heading into the November 3 midterm elections.\nTrump has made the legislation a signature issue ahead of the elections, portraying it as essential to protecting election integrity. Republicans are campaigning on tighter voter verification rules and hope the issue energises their base. Democrats are using the same legislation to argue that Republicans are attempting to make voting more difficult. As a result, the SAVE Act has become both a legislative proposal and an electoral messaging tool.\nIf Republicans expand their Senate majority after November, the bill's prospects could improve substantially. If the current partisan divide remains largely unchanged, passage will remain difficult because the legislation lacks bipartisan support.\nHow India has become a model for the US\nTrump's reference to India was not primarily about India. It was about strengthening a domestic American argument.\nBy invoking the world's largest democracy which is praised for its huge electoral machinery, Trump sought to rebut claims that strict voter identification rules are inherently restrictive or undemocratic. India allows him to point to a country that combines massive voter participation with mandatory identity verification and limited mail voting.\nBut the comparison has limits. India's electoral system is built around a centralized election authority, a dedicated voter identity framework and a different constitutional structure. America's system is decentralised by design and shaped by a long history of conflicts over voting rights, racial discrimination and federal versus state authority.\nThat's why the debate over voter ID in the US remains so contentious. It is not merely a technical discussion about identity documents but a dispute over how to balance election security against voter access, an issue that has shaped American democracy for more than half a century.",
        "content": "In a Truth Social post on August 18, Trump highlighted India’s voter identification requirements and low reliance on mail voting as evidence that large democracies can run elections with tighter controls.\n“Earlier this month, India’s Chief Election Commissioner, Gyanesh Kumar, asked our Administration — How can you have Elections in the U.S. without a valid Photo ID? India’s last Election had 646,...",
