        "title": "Government to crack down on super switching schemes",
        "link": "https://www.abc.net.au/news/2026-08-19/telemarketers-the-target-of-government-crackdown-super-switching/107045422",
        "source": "Just In",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-18T19:44:08.471475+00:00",
        "image": "https://live-production.wcms.abc-cdn.net.au/b3ca2c1c848bbf03d89a35abea481442?impolicy=wcms_watermark_news&cropH=2810&cropW=4995&xPos=0&yPos=61&width=862&height=485&imformat=generic",
        "full_content": "Unlicensed telemarketers target of government crackdown on super switching schemes\nIn short:\nTelemarketers that cold call people trying to convince them to switch super providers will need to be licensed.\nIn this story:\nUnlicensed telemarketers that cold call and make unsolicited approaches to convince people to switch their super will be banned under long-awaited reforms.\nAssistant Treasurer Daniel Mulino will appear at the National Press Club today and announce a suite of reforms to better protect Australians from falling victim to operators that rob them of their retirement savings.\nThe reforms follow the collapse of the Shield and First Guardian funds that saw 12,000 people lose more than $1 billion in retirement savings, and which exposed deep flaws in the regulation of Australia's almost $4.5 trillion retirement savings pool.\nCurrently, people might go on Facebook and click an ad that looks innocent like \"Find Your Lost Super\" or \"Check Your Super\", but then gives permission for a telemarketer, also known as a \"lead generator\", to contact them and suggest they connect with a financial adviser to switch their super.\nMr Mulino said, under the changes, in order to be able to advertise, these lead generators would need to be licensed.\nIt stops short of the outright ban on advertising that some consumer groups have been calling for.\nThe assistant treasurer said there would be \"exceptions\" to protect advocacy, educational and employment communications.\n\"These reforms are designed to disrupt some of the most damaging business models operating in the system today,\"\n\"They target the point at which consumers are first exposed to harm and reduce the ability of bad actors to gain access to consumers in the first place.\"\nMr Mulino said he also wanted to \"strengthen the anti‑hawking regime to enhance consent requirements, limit the existing financial advice exemption to existing client relationships and strengthen penalties for breaches\".\nThat would require licensees \"to take reasonable steps to ensure lead generation activities comply with relevant regulatory and legal requirements\".\nSuper telemarketers lure investors in\nMichael Johnson is one of thousands of investors who lost his retirement savings, along with his wife, Caroline, when First Guardian collapsed in April 2025.\nThey each had separate superannuation accounts with retail funds, but in October 2020 moved about $500,000 of their super from APRA-regulated funds into one joint self-managed superannuation fund (SMSF).\nMr Johnson said he decided to switch his super after seeing an advertisement on Facebook, and then being contacted by a lead generator, who eventually put them onto financial advisers at United Global Capital (UGC).\n\"It was just like other ads you see for changing your health insurance or your car insurance, and it said, 'Do a health check on your super,'\"\nThat call from the telemarketer quickly led to him connecting with financial advisory firm UGC, which convinced him and his wife to move all their super and invest it wholly in the First Guardian Master Fund.\nUGC entered liquidation in August 2024 and ASIC cancelled the advisory firm's Australian Financial Services Licence (AFSL) in July 2024.\nThe primary adviser from UGC involved was contacted for comment but did not respond.\nMr Johnson said the financial advisers \"were very convincing\", but that had the government regulated lead generators before he and his wife switched their super, \"we wouldn't have gone in at all\".\n\"Part of how this worked was a remarkably large number of people signing up at the same time,\" Mr Johnson said.\n\"The operators … constructed a scheme that was based on getting lots of people in quickly, grabbing that money and then going home and then disappearing with it.\"\nMr Johnson said at the time he invested, First Guardian was described to the couple as a \"diversified\" investment vehicle.\nHe didn't realise anything was wrong until it was too late.\n\"It was devastating,\" Mr Johnson told ABC News.\n\"We're approaching retirement. Caroline was looking at reducing her work hours. We wanted to be able to retire and keep our current lifestyle.\"\nMr Johnson said the couple now did not know how they could financially manage.\n\"It looks like we will be needing to have a government pension and the whole point of having compulsory superannuation is that you don't need [to do] that,\" he said.\n\"It's impacted our mental health. That impacts our physical health. It's made life seem much harder.\"\nCall for social media companies to take down 'harmful' ads\nSuper Consumers CEO Xavier O'Halloran supports the ban on unlicensed cold calling, but wants to see obligations on social media platforms to take down harmful ads.\nHe said that through lead generators, consumers' details then got sold on to a financial advisor \"where you're put into really a train towards selling you financial advice, often quite expensive, over $10,000 in some cases, and getting you to switch into certain products\".\n\"We still want to see greater protections and obligations on the social media platforms to ensure that these ads are taken down if there are still harmful ones being put up,\"\n\"We don't want to see claims made that can't be substantiated. We don't want to see people's details collected then on-sold to someone else without their knowledge.\n\"We don't want … what is meant to be a basic health check on their superannuation, then suddenly sold into very expensive financial advice that sees them switched into really inappropriate products and have their entire retirement savings wiped out.\"\nCSLR changes could impact payouts\nToday, Mr Mulino is also expected to announce changes to the Compensation Scheme of Last Resort (CSLR), which covers victims of financial misconduct who have not been compensated for their losses — up to $150,000.\nHe is expected to widen the number of sectors that pay the levy to fund the scheme, which will include big super funds.\nThe \"but for\" test, which considers whether a claimant would have been in a better financial position had they received appropriate advice and earned interest, is expected to be removed.\nThe \"but for\" test has faced criticism from some in the advice industry for using a definition that's too broad, but consumer groups argue that removing it punishes victims of misconduct.\nStronger obligations on super platforms to check the investments they offer and act when things go wrong are also anticipated, along with stronger powers for ASIC to make platforms pay if they fail to protect their customers.\nMr Mulino is expected to unveil financial advice reforms to make financial advice more affordable and accessible to a broader range of consumers.\nMichael Johnson said he and his wife Caroline had made a complaint to the Australian Financial Complaints Authority (AFCA) to recover their super lost in the First Guardian collapse, but that the body was still investigating their claim.\nIf their claim fails, or if AFCA cannot recover the money from UGC, the couple will have to rely on the CSLR.\nThe CSLR will treat the couple's claims as one claim, and the $150,000 cap falls well short of their total losses.\nMr Johnson said if their retirement money had stayed in performing superannuation funds, he estimated that, together, they would have accumulated about $1.3 million in retirement savings by now.\nSuper Consumers' Mr O'Halloran was concerned removing the \"but for\" test would punish consumers and urged victims of the First Guardian and Shield collapses to claim before it is removed.\n\"For a typical person that was invested in Shield and First Guardian — if they don't get their complaint in by July 1 of 2027 — they could miss out on any investment earnings that they would have otherwise received in compensation,\" he said.\nMr O'Halloran said people who lost their life savings in the Shield and First Guardian collapses lost an average of $100,000, and Super Consumers' modelling found the government's proposed change could cost them another $26,500 in lost investment earnings.\n\"We don't think victims should be punished for the collapse of these financial schemes. They should be properly compensated and we [the government and regulators] should go after the actual criminals.\"\nMary Delahunty, chief executive of the Association of Superannuation Funds of Australia (ASFA), welcomed the changes to ensure unlicensed lead generation operators were banned.\nBut she was unhappy that super funds were being tapped to pay for growing claims under the CSLR.\n\"It is not okay that superannuation funds have been co-opted into paying for a scheme that the majority of their members cannot claim from,\"\n\"What that amounts to is like a tax on every Australian to pay for a scheme that, yes, needs to exist, but not in its unsustainable form.\n\"So this is an ongoing point that we need to work with the government on.\"\nSarah Abood, CEO of the Financial Advice Association Australia (FAAA), welcomed the minister's expected change to broaden the scope of financial advice consumers can access.\n\"Financial advice has never been more important, but too many Australians simply cannot afford it,\"\n\"Australians need a financial advice system that is affordable, accessible and trustworthy.\"\nWatch The Business, Mondays to Thursdays 8:44pm on ABC News Channel, after the Late News on ABC TV, and anytime on ABC iview",
        "content": "Unlicensed telemarketers target of government crackdown on super switching schemes\nIn short:\nTelemarketers that cold call people trying to convince them to switch super providers will need to be licensed.\nIn this story:\nUnlicensed telemarketers that cold call and make unsolicited approaches to convince people to switch their super will be banned under long-awaited reforms.\nAssistant Treasurer Dani...",
        "tags": [
            "General"
        ],
        "location": "Global",
        "human_time": "7m ago"
    },
