        "title": "Temporary visa rules leave Iranian refugee 'trapped in nightmare'",
        "link": "https://www.abc.net.au/news/2026-08-19/australian-immigration-laws-temportary-visas-refugee-limbo/106998224",
        "source": "Just In",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-18T19:44:08.471453+00:00",
        "image": "https://live-production.wcms.abc-cdn.net.au/3ff02f9ffb664175e8f4a03e79ba0583?impolicy=wcms_watermark_news&cropH=675&cropW=1200&xPos=0&yPos=269&width=862&height=485&imformat=generic",
        "full_content": "Iranian refugee fears temporary visa could lead to separation from family\nMahsa Mazaheri wakes up every day feeling like she is \"trapped in a nightmare\" and fearing she will be separated from her husband and two daughters at any time.\nFor the past eight years the Iranian refugee has had to reapply for a temporary bridging visa to stay in Australia, which expires every six months.\n\"I'm scared. I have to remind myself, 'Don't forget to check the date. Don't forget,'\" Ms Mazaheri told the ABC.\nMs Mazaheri and her mother came to Australia from Iran by boat in 2013 and were sent to offshore detention centres on Naoero (formerly Nauru) and Christmas Island.\nTheir time in those facilities felt like \"time in jail\", but the pair were medically evacuated to mainland Australia after her mother's health worsened in 2014.\nThey have been in visa limbo since.\nUnder Australia's immigration policies for people who arrived by boat, Ms Mazaheri and her mother have been denied any pathway to a permanent visa in Australia.\nThey are part of a cohort of more than 700 survivors of offshore detention living in Australia with no permanency, according to the Human Rights Law Centre.\nDespite their uncertain visa status, many have started families, had children, and built lives they say they cannot leave behind.\nMs Mazaheri is a small business owner who pays taxes, but she cannot leave the country and make any long-term plans due to restrictions on her temporary visa.\nLives built around 'insecure visas'\nHuman Rights Law Centre associate legal director Laura John said that under the Australian government's migration laws, people previously held in offshore detention who were now living in Australia are categorised as \"transitory persons\".\n\"Despite living in Australia for over a decade, having an Australian citizen partner or children, working in critical industries and being part of the fabric of the Australian community, the 700 people previously held in offshore detention who have built homes in Australia are still left on short-term, insecure visas,\" she said.\nMs Mazaheri said reapplying for her visa every three or six months with no guarantee of it being granted had been emotionally taxing.\n\"I'm not sure if they give me the visa or they tell me I have to go to a different country,\" she said.\n\"We don't know what's going to happen the next day.\"\nMs John, who has been working closely with Ms Mazaheri and her family, said Australia's offshore processing policy had \"caused immeasurable harm to thousands of people and their families\".\nMs John said it was time the government abandoned \"cruel and senseless policies from the past\" and let people stay with their families and communities.\nA Department of Home Affairs spokesperson said \"transitory persons temporarily residing in Australia under regional processing arrangements do not have a permanent settlement pathway in Australia\".\n\"Transitory persons are encouraged to consider their options to depart Australia, either to a third country or return to their home country,\" it said in a statement.\n\"A range of support is available to transitory persons to connect with third country migration options or to return home voluntarily.\"\n'I just want permanent safety'\nMs Mazaheri has set up a life with her husband, who she met in Australia, and their two daughters, who are four and three years of age.\n\"We have the kids, we have a family, and I just want permanent safety,\" she said.\nMs Mazaheri's husband arrived by boat from Iran a few days after her, but was not transferred to Naoero because the government's policy \"was not applied consistently\", according to Ms John.\nMs Mazaheri's husband was allowed to apply for refugee protection onshore and was granted Australian citizenship earlier this year.\nTheir two daughters were added to their father's application and both are now Australian citizens.\nIt was a bittersweet outcome for Ms Mazaheri.\n\"I was really happy for them, but in my heart I was really upset,\" she said.\nMs Mazaheri said the situation reminded her of the possibility of being taken from her daughters due to her temporary status.\n\"In Iran I had a really hard situation with the government, but here it's harder for me,\" she said.\n\"Every morning when I wake up, I'm thinking about my kids: 'Am I going to stay with them? Maybe they're [the Australian government] are going to surprise me. If they tell me to go to a different country, what should I do?'\n\"I don't have any future now.\"\nCalls for resolution of status\nThe bridging visa Ms Mazaheri and her mother have been granted includes conditions such as limitations on work and study, and they are subject to a conduct of behaviour policy, according to the Department of Immigration.\nThe website states that Bridging Visa E (BVE) has two categories: subclasses 051 and 050.\nBoth BVE categories are described as temporary visas that \"let you stay lawfully in Australia\" while you wait for an immigration decision or for your protection visa to be processed.\nPeople on a BVEs have to reapply for Medicare benefits every time their visas expire.\nMs Mazaheri said that she had gone without Medicare cover many times while waiting for a pending application, which could sometimes take up to three months.\nThat has meant that her mother, whom Ms Mazaheri cares for full-time, has gone without medical treatment due to the high out-of-pocket fees.\n\"My daughter, she asks, 'Oh, mummy, can we go on holiday back to see grandma?' But it's too hard to sit down and explain to a four-year-old, 'Oh, mummy can't because I don't have a visa,'\" Ms Mazaheri said.\nMs Mazaheri's is among a large number of individual cases that have been submitted to Immigration Minister Tony Burke, according to Ms John.\n\"If the minister decided not to grant a bridging visa, a person could be ripped from their family, taken into immigration detention and sent back to Nauru [Naoero] or potentially deported to their country of origin,\" she said.\n\"Our migration laws prevent people in this group from applying for any permanent visa, including a partner visa, unless Minister Burke personally intervenes in their case. Without a permanent visa, people are not eligible for Australian citizenship.\"\nMs John said the roughly 700 refugees had very few protections to prevent deportation back to the country they fled from.\n\"Even to a place like Iran,\" she said.\n\"Minister Burke already has the power to grant permanent safety to Mahsa and the other 700 people who are trapped in limbo.\n\"It is time for people to be given the protection they first sought over 13 years ago. Granting people permanent safety will simply reflect the practical reality that Australia is now their home.\"\nThe Human Rights Law Centre, Asylum Seeker Resource Centre and advocacy group GetUp are petitioning Mr Burke to grant permanent residency to the refugees in limbo.",
        "content": "Iranian refugee fears temporary visa could lead to separation from family\nMahsa Mazaheri wakes up every day feeling like she is \"trapped in a nightmare\" and fearing she will be separated from her husband and two daughters at any time.\nFor the past eight years the Iranian refugee has had to reapply for a temporary bridging visa to stay in Australia, which expires every six months.\n\"I'm scared. I ha...",
