        "title": "On the Agenda: What's happening in Denmark next week?",
        "link": "https://www.thelocal.dk/20260815/on-the-agenda-whats-happening-in-denmark-next-week-4",
        "source": "The Local Denmark",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-18T19:44:08.471359+00:00",
        "image": "https://assets.thelocal.com/cdn-cgi/rs:fit:1200/quality:75/plain/https://apiwp.thelocal.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/08/watermarks-logo-07frb2024-1200px.jpg@webp",
        "full_content": "Monday, August 17th\nBlack Hole Week, hosted by the Center of Gravity, starts on Monday, August 17th. The event kicks off with a micro-opera: a lyrical performance dedicated to black holes. Black Hole Week includes lots of other great educational events, including Science & Cocktails, where you can learn about black holes while enjoying a cocktail, a talk about where black holes come from at The Round Tower, and more.\nTuesday, August 18th\nCourt case between Dansk Folkeparti and Radikale Venstre\nThe chairman of the Danish People's Party (Dansk Folkeaparti), Morten Messerschmidt, and the political leader of the Social Liberal Party (Radical Venstre), Martin Lidegaard, are expected to meet in the Court in Frederiksberg on August 18th. This is a civil lawsuit in which Messerschmidt has sued Lidegaard for defamation, due to his statements that Dansk Folkeparti want to deport citizens based on their skin colour.\nWednesday, August 19th\nThe Danish Democrats’ Summer Meeting\nThe political summer meetings are in full swing, and this week the Danish Democrats (Danmarksdemokraterne), Denmark’s right-wing populist party, are hosting their summer meeting on August 19th and 20th in Nyborg on the island of Funen.\nThursday, August 20th\nThursday sees the start of a large variety of cultural events, one of them being Kulturmødet Mors, a cultural event which takes place in Mors in North Jutland. The event celebrates art and culture through debates, performances, workshops, exhibitions and more. More than 32,000 visitors are expected to attend the event which start on August 20th and lasts until August 22nd.\nLouisiana, one of Denmark’s most famous modern art museums, kicks off its much anticipated event, Louisiana Literature, which starts on Thursday, August 20th. This annual event celebrates modern literature through interviews, dialogues, readings, performances and musical interpretations. The event takes place all weekend and features many Danish authors, but also international authors such as Douglas Stuart, Katie Kitamura, David Szalay, and more.\nOne more cultural event takes place, this one nestled between the Black Diamond library and the Danish parliament, Christiansborg, in a secret garden. The event takes place every Thursday evening until mid-September. This week’s event features musical performances, artistic interpretations of poetry and talks on the topic of “trust”.\nLiberal Alliance’s Summer Meeting\nOn Thursday, August 20th, one of Denmark’s centre-right political parties, Liberal Alliance, hosts its annual summer meeting, which this year takes place at Egelund Castle in Fredensborg in North Zealand.\nFriday, August 21st\nCopenhagen Cooking and Food Festival\nCopenhagen Cooking is an annual food festival which takes place in the heart of Copenhagen. Every year at the end of August, the country’s best chefs, restaurants, producers, cultural institutions, and foodies gather for a 10-day celebration of Copenhagen’s diverse food scene. This year’s programme includes everything from outdoor, communal dinners, a day dedicated to the Danish smørrebrød, and an event revolving around Spanish jamón (ham). If you’re a foodie, this is an event you won’t want to miss.\nA handful of Fridays throughout the summer, streets across Copenhagen are closed, as roller-skaters zoom through the city. The routes change each time, and are roughly 20km long. Between 500 and 1000 people take part in each event. The trip starts and finishes at Next House, close to Copenhagen’s Central Station, begins at 7.30pm, and takes roughly two hours.\nSaturday, August 22nd\nOn Saturday and Sunday, Esbjerg’s annual music festival takes place in the city’s harbour on the west coast of Denmark. This year’s lineup features big international names such as Pet Shop Boys and Kelis (known for her song Milkshake), and big Danish names such as Rasmus Seebach, Dizzy Mizz Lizzy, Suspekt, and more.\nOpening of Hovedstadens Letbane\nThe Greater Copenhagen light rail (Hovedstadens Letbane) opens the last stretch of its route August 22nd, allowing passengers to travel the entire route from Ishøj to Lundtofte north of Lyngby. The opening will be marked by a public festival at Gladsaxe Town Hall with music and activities. The full opening of the light rail system includes 17 new stations, connecting Rødovre and Lyngby, adding to the existing 12 stations between Ishøj and Rødovre.\nWith the season for Octoberfest approaching, Reffen are kicking things off with a party dedicated to good beer. The event invites micro-breweries to their pop-up bars, as well as talks, tastings, and beer bingo.\nSunday, August 23rd\nOn Sunday, August 23rd, you can go for a walk around the stone embankment around Copenhagen's new (and controvertial) peninsula, Lynetteholm and get a full sense of how large the peninsula will be. The full walk is 7.5km long, but a shorter 4km option is also available. The event requires registration, and includes free coffee, snacks, talks and more.\nREAD ALSO: Party demands new environmental scrutiny of Copenhagen artificial island project\nOn Sunday August 23rd, DSB (Danish State Railways) invites everyone to what they call Cykelsøndag or “Bike Sunday”. The S-trains, which run from central Copenhagen through Zealand to many of the capital’s suburban towns, will be free, and bike-related events have been planned, such as free bike repairs in Ishøj, bike rides and pancakes in Hareskoven, mountain biking in Rude Skov, and more.",
        "content": "Monday, August 17th\nBlack Hole Week, hosted by the Center of Gravity, starts on Monday, August 17th. The event kicks off with a micro-opera: a lyrical performance dedicated to black holes. Black Hole Week includes lots of other great educational events, including Science & Cocktails, where you can learn about black holes while enjoying a cocktail, a talk about where black holes come from at The Ro...",
