        "title": "Today in Denmark: A roundup of the latest news on Monday",
        "link": "https://www.thelocal.dk/20260817/today-in-denmark-a-roundup-of-the-latest-news-on-monday-57",
        "source": "The Local Denmark",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-18T19:44:08.471352+00:00",
        "image": "https://assets.thelocal.com/cdn-cgi/rs:fit:1200/quality:75/plain/https://apiwp.thelocal.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/08/watermarks-logo-20260815-163253-L-1920x1279web.jpg@webp",
        "full_content": "Copenhagen celebrates Pride peacefully without arrests\nThousands of people attended the Copenhagen Pride festival on Saturday, including Denmark's Prime Minister Mette Frederiksen, without police having to make any arrests.\n\"There have been no assaults, and we have had no arrests in connection with Copenhagen Pride, so it's gone well and without any major issues,\" Mads Østermark from the Copenhagen Police said after the event. \"There can always be some minor issues when there are a lot of people on the streets and when people are drinking alcohol.\"\nThe parade on Saturday was the culmination of a week of celebrations as Copenhagen Pride marked its 30th anniversary.\n\"In a world where rights are being rolled back and democracy is under pressure, we must fight for the basic democratic values, and of course that also means that two men can be together and that a rainbow family can have good conditions,\" Frederiksen told TV2 during the parade.\nThe Liberal Party was the only party from Denmark's right-wing opposition which took part in this year's event, a decision Jakob Engel-Schmidt, tax minister from the Moderate Party, told TV2 was \"a bit sad\".\nDanish vocabulary: en smule trist – a bit sad\nRed-Green Alliance calls for ban on US company Palantir\nDenmark's far-Left Red-Green Alliance has called for a ban on the use of software from the controversial US company Palantir by Danish police, intelligence services and other public agencies.\nAhead of the party's group meeting on Monday, the party's leader, Pelle Dragsted, said his party aimed to demand in upcoming budget negotiations that money be put aside for helping these agencies that use Palantir's products replace it with Danish or European software.\n\"We are concerned about whether Palantir can be used by the United States to gain access to sensitive information about Denmark and Greenland. This is about digital sovereignty and being able to stand on our own two feet,\" he said.\n\"We must not be vulnerable in relation to Trump and his government, which, as we know, uses every vulnerability and dependency as a means of pressure.\"\nDragsted also said that the government must push ahead with its agreement to lower VAT on food, threatening to trigger a government crisis if the agreement is not fulfilled.\n\"This is not a threat, it is a fact,\" Dragsted told the Børsen newspaper.\nThe proposal has been met with harsh criticism from economists, who warn, among other things, that it is an expensive and inefficient way to help citizens.\nDanish vocabulary: et forbud – a ban\nMan flown for treatment after stabbing in Horsens\nA man was flown to hospital for treatment by helicopter on Sunday after he was stabbed in a violent assault in central Horsens. Police cordoned off the area after the attack and blocked the nearby roads. Police officers were out on patrol on Sankt Helene Vej and Sønderbrogade, the two most affected areas.\nThe cordon was lifted at 1.30am on Monday morning. Police have concluded that the stabbing was an \"isolated incident\" and have told residents there is no risk that the situation \"may develop further\".\nDanish vocabulary: knivstikkeri – stabbing\nDenmark gets unified emergency psychiatric hotline\nDenmark is launching a nationwide psychiatric emergency hotline, which anyone in Denmark can access by ringing the 112 emergency number or contacting their local regional emergency doctor, or lægevagten.\nThe scheme was first announced in 2023 as part of a psychiatric agreement between all the parties in the Danish parliament, but it has taken until now to be rolled out across the country.\nIf a citizen or their relative calls the emergency medical service with a mental health issue, the service can then trigger a psychiatric referral to a consultant with psychiatric skills.\nThe new function is staffed by nurses and social and health assistants with psychiatric experience in close collaboration with the regions' psychiatric specialists.\nThe scheme was developed after three people were killed at the Field's shopping centre in the summer of 2022 by a man who had spent years in and out of psychiatric treatment.\nDanish vocabulary: sygeplejersker – nurses",
        "content": "Copenhagen celebrates Pride peacefully without arrests\nThousands of people attended the Copenhagen Pride festival on Saturday, including Denmark's Prime Minister Mette Frederiksen, without police having to make any arrests.\n\"There have been no assaults, and we have had no arrests in connection with Copenhagen Pride, so it's gone well and without any major issues,\" Mads Østermark from the Copenhage...",
