        "title": "Denmark launches unified emergency psychiatric hotline",
        "link": "https://www.thelocal.dk/20260817/denmark-launches-unified-emergency-psychiatric-hotline",
        "source": "The Local Denmark",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-18T19:44:08.471348+00:00",
        "image": "https://assets.thelocal.com/cdn-cgi/rs:fit:1200/quality:75/plain/https://apiwp.thelocal.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/08/watermarks-logo-20251108-013647-L-1920x1280web.jpg@webp",
        "full_content": "The scheme was first announced in 2023 as part of a psychiatric agreement between all the parties in the Danish parliament, but it has taken until now to be rolled out across the country.\nEssentially, the new scheme means that anyone calling 112, whether for a physical or mental health issue, can receive emergency assistance. In the case of an emergency mental health issue, a call will trigger a referral to a consultant with psychiatric skills.\nThe new function is staffed by nurses and social and health assistants with psychiatric experience in close collaboration with the regions' psychiatric specialists.\nCallers who need to go to hospital can still be picked up in an ambulance and treated by paramedics if needed, before being brought to emergency psychiatric evaluation.\nOne of the aims of the new scheme is to provide help before a situation worsens or develops to the extent that the patient requires inpatient treatment.\nThe scheme was developed after three people were killed at the Field's shopping centre in the summer of 2022 by a man who had spent years in and out of psychiatric treatment. The incident sparked a debate about psychiatric treatment in Denmark, including a request for a national emergency line for psychiatric issues.\nMia Kristina Hansen, spokesperson for national mental health association Sind, said a year after the attack that she was disappointed that the new emergency line was not yet in place.\n\"If the perpetrator had called and they had said that someone needed to drive out to him and help him, I think that would have made a huge difference,\" she said.\nIda Auken, health and church minister, welcomed the new scheme, saying that it is part of a new \"historic\" improvement in psychiatric treatment in Denmark.\n\"For a lot of people with mental health issues, the psychiatric emergency line will be a quick and easy way to access treatment,\" she said in a press statement.\nThe new scheme will be monitored closely for further evaluation.",
        "content": "The scheme was first announced in 2023 as part of a psychiatric agreement between all the parties in the Danish parliament, but it has taken until now to be rolled out across the country.\nEssentially, the new scheme means that anyone calling 112, whether for a physical or mental health issue, can receive emergency assistance. In the case of an emergency mental health issue, a call will trigger a r...",
