    {
        "title": "Excessive kissing and cutting socks: Denmark's weird and wonderful wedding traditions",
        "link": "https://www.thelocal.dk/20260727/excessive-kissing-and-cut-off-socks-denmarks-weird-and-wonderful-wedding-traditions",
        "source": "The Local Denmark",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-18T19:44:08.471342+00:00",
        "image": "https://assets.thelocal.com/cdn-cgi/rs:fit:1200/quality:75/plain/https://apiwp.thelocal.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/07/watermarks-logo-devon-divine-KKfsJRrLcWk-unsplash.jpg@webp",
        "full_content": "The ceremony\nWe’ll start off simple: the ceremony. Nothing too shocking happens here. The ceremony either takes place in a church or at a town hall, the latter being the more popular, and faster of the two ceremonies, taking at the very most 15 minutes. A church wedding will include hymns chosen by the couple.\nThe ceremony includes similar traditions as in other countries: the groom shouldn’t see the bride before she walks down the aisle, typically accompanied by her father; they don’t spend the night before the wedding in the same bedroom; rice is thrown over the newlyweds as they leave the church or town hall.\nDress code\nWhen trying to plan your outfit, there are a few colours which should be avoided at a Danish wedding. White is strictly reserved for the bride. Avoid anything white, cream-coloured, light beige, or even a white dress with flowers on. Black is the colour of mourning and should also be avoided at weddings. Red in old traditions symbolises that you have had a romantic relationship with either the bride or the groom. Red might not necessarily be off-limits, but it may be a good idea to ask the bride or groom for their opinion first. If in doubt, ask!\nKransekage\nAfter the ceremony, a larger group of guests will be invited to have champagne and kransekage, a traditional Scandinavian confection made from marzipan, sugar, and egg whites. It is shaped into stacked, concentric rings to form a tall, cone-shaped tower decorated with white icing.\nDinner\nThe number of guests usually thins slightly for the dinner, which takes place after the kransekage has been eaten.\nRemoving your jacket\nGuests can’t take their jackets off until the groom does. On a hot summer’s day, you’ll often feel a sigh of relief as guests realise the groom has taken off his jacket, after which they follow suit.\nKissing… lots of it!\nIf any celebration should contain lots of kissing, surely it’s a wedding? The Danes wholeheartedly agree, and have therefore invented some fun games for the newlyweds to take part in.\nDon’t be surprised when the wedding guests start hammering on the tables. This is the ritual performed to get the newlyweds to stand on their chairs and kiss. When guests start stamping their feet, this is the newlywed’s cue to crawl under the table and kiss.\nYou’ll often find the guests have some fun with this, getting the bride and groom to do one after the other.\nWhen the bride or groom leave the room\nKissing isn’t just reserved for the bride and groom. When either the bride or groom leave the room (as inevitably happens when they need to go to the toilet), the opposite sex line up to kiss the newlywed who was left behind on the cheek.\nSongs and speeches\nIf you’ve been to any big Danish gathering, it won’t surprise you that Danes really like to sing together.\nA fun Danish tradition is the custom of taking a well-known song (often from the Danish Højskolesangbog, a book of songs written with the purpose of being sung at folk high schools, but sometimes also popular, international songs), and replacing the lyrics, making them unique to the couple being celebrated. The new lyrics will be printed out and passed around to guests, who will then sing the song to the well-known melody.\nREAD ALSO: Why do Danes love singing group songs at family gatherings?\nDanish weddings also come with an awful lot of speeches, often making the dinner-part of the wedding drag on - especially if you don’t speak any Danish.\nHurra!\nThe Danish “Hurra!”, similar to the English “Hurray!” is used at most big celebrations. Originally used by soldiers as a battle cry, today it’s used to cheer on a newlywed couple, or a birthday boy or girl. The “hurra” is often used at the end of a speech, where the speech-giver will instruct the rest of the guests on how many “hurra”’s to do. The most common ones are:\n- Three short and one long: “Hurra! Hurra! Hurra! Hurraaaaaa!”\n- The nine-times hurra!: “Hurra! Hurra! Hurra!” times three.\n- The long live “Hurra!”: “[Name] længe leve! Hurra! Hurra! Hurra!” (“Long live [name]!” in English)\nThe wedding waltz\nWhen the dinner at long last is over, and at the very latest at 23.59, the dance floor is opened by the newlyweds with a traditional wedding waltz (brudevals in Danish). The waltz is to the melody from Danish ballet, Et Folkesagn (“a folktale” in English), composed by Niels W. Gade in 1854. Guests surround the newlyweds, clapping along to the beat of the song. As the song progresses, guests close in on the newlyweds, making them waltz in smaller and smaller circles.\nCutting the sock\nAs guests have closed in entirely on the newlyweds, making it impossible for them to continue their waltz, the groom is lifted into the air by male guests, his shoe removed, and the tip of his sock cut off (yes you read that right!)\nThis odd tradition is meant to symbolise that a bride will take care of her new husband, his clothes included. Cutting off the tip of the sock was a way for the new wife to prove her darning skills.\nRemoving the bride’s garter\nThe more daring couples sometimes also include the fun and slightly risqué tradition of removing the bride’s garter. Here the groom will crawl under the bride’s dress and remove the garter, either with his hands, but typically with his teeth. The groom will emerge to cheering guests, and then toss the garter to the group of unmarried male wedding guests - making it the male equivalent of the bouquet toss.\nDancing\nA Danish wedding is, if anything, a really great party. Expect dancing until the early morning hours, and don’t be too surprised if you wake up with a hangover.",
        "content": "The ceremony\nWe’ll start off simple: the ceremony. Nothing too shocking happens here. The ceremony either takes place in a church or at a town hall, the latter being the more popular, and faster of the two ceremonies, taking at the very most 15 minutes. A church wedding will include hymns chosen by the couple.\nThe ceremony includes similar traditions as in other countries: the groom shouldn’t see ...",
