        "full_content": "So far this month, only 17.7mm of rain has fallen in Denmark – the usual figure for August is much higher at 82.1mm, Danish public broadcaster DR reports.\nThe usual figure for a specific month is determined by the so-called klimanormal, the broadcaster reports, which looks at the average rainfall between 1991 and 2020.\nIn October last year, around 120mm of rain fell, much more than the usual 80mm seen in an average October. Since then, however, no month has even reached the normal level of rainfall, let alone surpassed it.\nThis lack of rainfall is also reflected in the country's drought index, where Denmark is currently standing at 9.4 out of a maximum 10. This puts the country in the most serious category, 'high risk for drought'.\nDR reports that there is rain forecast for this week, although despite this we're nowhere near the normal level of precipitation for this time of year.",
