        "full_content": "The pamphlets, developed by The Ministry of Green Transition, include useful information for everyone, so we’ve decided to highlight some key points.\nREAD ALSO: Wolves approaching humans in Jutland may have been 'actively fed'\n- Stay still and keep calm. It’s very rare that wolves approach people, but if you do come across a wolf who isn’t shy towards humans, and who comes closer than 30 metres, keep very still, stay calm, keep an eye on the animal, and if possible, take a photo or video as documentation.\n- Don’t follow the wolf, as this might stress or anger it. Give it time and peace to distance itself from you.\n- If you feel uneasy, make some noise. If the wolf doesn’t leave by itself, shout, clap your hands or wave your arms around. This signals that you’re a human, which in itself is usually enough to scare off a wolf.\n- Keep eye contact. Never turn your back on an approaching wolf, but continue looking directly at it. Show dominance in the same way as you might over an aggressive dog.\n- Stay seated, if you see a wolf from your car. A wolf doesn’t associate vehicles with humans and is therefore unlikely to interact with cars. Stay in your car until the wolf is gone.\n- Back away slowly if you and the wolf notice each other when you’re very close to each other. If the wolf feels threatened, it may growl and show its teeth. This isn’t necessarily a sign that it’s going to attack you, but rather that it’s afraid. Give it some space, and it will naturally flee.\n- If the wolf attacks, kick and hit it. We have yet to experience any human being attacked by a wolf in Denmark, but if it does, you need to use physical strength to ward off the wolf. Use this tactic too, if the wolf attacks your dog.\nThe Ministry of Green Transition has also published recommendations on how to avoid attracting wolves to your garden or home if you live in an affected area.\n- Stop feeding wild animals such as deer, hedgehogs and more.\n- Don’t leave out food waste. Meat attracts wolves especially much, and wolf pups are naturally curious and will learn to associate humans with food if they repeatedly find food in the same place.\n- Protect your pets. Bring your pets in at night, and don’t let your dog roam too far, as wolves can take interest in them, as they see them as equals.\nAnd lastly the Ministry of Green Transition has made a list of recommendations for how you can protect your dog against wolves.\n- Keep your dog on a lead, as these are less likely to be viewed as a threat to wolves.\n- Keep your dog close if you meet a wolf.\n- If you’re out hunting, keep your hunting dogs close, and as a minimum where you can see and hear them.\nWolves are a protected species in Denmark, but they can be shot if they are deemed to be a “problem wolf”, a term used for wolves that are not shy around people, roam urban areas, have attacked livestock behind wolf-proof fences, or are a danger to human safety.\nIf you do spot a wolf, make sure to report your sighting on ulveatlas.dk, to help the Danish Nature Agency keep track of Denmark’s wolves.",
