    {
        "title": "AFL policy is to wait while police investigate. The Swans may not",
        "link": "https://www.abc.net.au/news/2026-08-19/sydney-swans-questions-afl-policy-sexual-assault-allegations/107051552",
        "source": "Just In",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-18T20:19:42.485787+00:00",
        "image": "https://live-production.wcms.abc-cdn.net.au/61ba4da5c478a81b92b375b6e901ad07?impolicy=wcms_watermark_news&cropH=1179&cropW=2096&xPos=0&yPos=109&width=862&height=485&imformat=generic",
        "full_content": "Advocates welcome official responses to Sydney Swans allegations as police investigation underway\nIn short:\nAdvocates have welcomed a fresh statement from the Sydney Swans suggesting the club is considering sanctions against players following an alleged hotel incident.\nA police investigation is underway into a report of an alleged sexual assault at a Melbourne hotel where Swans players were staying.\nWhat's next?\nThe Sydney Swans board was scheduled to meet overnight to agree on player sanctions and penalties.\nThe Sydney Swans' declaration that some players are in \"serious breach\" of club standards has signalled a tougher approach after days of uncertainty over how the AFL would handle a report of an alleged incident at a Melbourne hotel where Swans players were staying.\nFour Swans players — Isaac Heeney, Chad Warner, James Jordon and Riley Bice — are understood to be linked to a Victoria Police investigation regarding an alleged sexual assault at an East Melbourne hotel in the early hours of Monday morning.\nWhile few details have been made public beyond the allegation itself, Victorian police are continuing to investigate.\nIt is not suggested that any of the four players are being accused of sexual assault.\nIt is not known what breaches of club conduct the club has found.\nOn Tuesday evening, the Swans issued a statement saying the club would discuss appropriate sanctions and penalties after it finalised interviews with \"all players involved\".\n\"We respect there is an ongoing investigation by Victoria Police and any actions we take are independent of this,\" the statement said.\n\"The club believes the players are in serious breach of expected club behaviour and standards, and it is appropriate to take firm action and highlight that any sanction we impose relates solely to those breaches.\n\"The Sydney Swans condemn all forms of abuse and disrespectful behaviour towards women.\"\nGrowing pressure on Swans, AFL\nUntil last night, the club and the league had said relatively little, prompting experts to question whether the AFL's policy of waiting for a criminal investigation to take place if police are involved is a strong enough position for a league that has publicly condemned violence against women.\nSome of the limited comments from AFL leaders had also drawn criticism, including Swans CEO Matthew Pavlich's mention of the club's extreme disappointment that its players had \"found themselves in this situation\".\nFormer AFL Women's star Kate McCarthy was among those who challenged that framing, noting: \"Players don't simply 'find themselves in situations.\"\nOthers, including Our Watch CEO Patty Kinnersly, observed some media commentary centred on finals season while sidelining the \"the alleged sexual assault at the centre of this story\".\n\"In the coverage to date we've seen examples of poor reporting that has centred on on-field consequences ahead of a serious, alleged crime,\" she said.\nAdvocates welcome new statements from Swans, AFL\nThe alleged sexual assault has raised questions about how the AFL deals with alleged incidents like this, or if the league is doing enough to prevent incidents in the first place.\nLast night AFL CEO Andrew Dillon offered the league's first substantial statement on the alleged sexual assault, labelling the allegations as \"serious\" and \"deeply concerning\".\n\"While the matter remains under police investigation, it raises broader issues for our game and our responsibilities as a sport,\" he said.\n\"It is important that the police process is allowed to run its course.\"\nRespect Victoria's CEO Helen Bolton, who had earlier criticised the league for not coming out strongly enough, welcomed the fresh statements from the Swans and the AFL.\n\"I think it's really encouraging that both the club and the AFL have made some really clear and accountable statements about the alleged conduct,\" she said.\nBut she said it was disappointing that those statements took so long.\nShe also stressed the need for a \"parallel process\" for the AFL to lead its own response to the allegations while the police process was underway, which she said was no different than any other workplace.\nAFL investigations pause when police involved\nAt a policy level, the AFL's default position is to let a criminal investigation run its course when police are involved.\nThe league's Respect and Responsibility policy outlines that \"an AFL investigation will wait until a police investigation has been concluded\".\nLawyer and sports integrity expert, UNSW scholar Catherine Ordway, described that policy approach as \"reactive\".\nShe said it was not in line with best practice across sporting codes, including internationally.\n\"The AFL waits for the criminal law process to be finalised before taking action against any players or officials,\" she said.\nDr Ordway said she thought the players should be stood down while a police or AFL investigation took place.\nNRL no-fault stand down rule\nSince 2019, the NRL has had a policy that if serious charges are laid against a player, the player is stood down while the process moves through the courts.\n\"There are some pros and cons of that, but I think in terms of fairness that's the right decision. There needs to be some strong messaging about how unacceptable violence is against women,\" Dr Ordway said.\nDr Ordway described it as a more \"proactive\" approach in line with Major League Baseball in the US and the English Cricket Board.\nOur Watch, which has delivered educational programs to AFL clubs on gendered violence, said it would support the league adopting a similar rule to the NRL.\n\"When we're wanting to focus on creating cultures of safety for women, we would support the no-fault stand down that the NRL has done,\" CEO Ms Kinnersly said.\nThe NRL players association opposed the no-fault stand down policy when it was introduced.\nSydney Swans scheduled to meet overnight\nAFL Players Association CEO James Gallagher was asked about the no-fault policy on Tuesday but did not say if any equivalent would be opposed by the players' union.\n\"What we've got here at the moment is allegations, and so I think part of it is understanding exactly what's happened. We should take the time with it to properly understand,\" he told Triple M.\nThe Sydney Swans said its board would meet overnight to determine appropriate penalties.\nThe ABC has contacted the AFL Players Association and the AFL for comment.",
        "content": "Advocates welcome official responses to Sydney Swans allegations as police investigation underway\nIn short:\nAdvocates have welcomed a fresh statement from the Sydney Swans suggesting the club is considering sanctions against players following an alleged hotel incident.\nA police investigation is underway into a report of an alleged sexual assault at a Melbourne hotel where Swans players were stayin...",
        "tags": [
            "General"
        ],
        "location": "Global",
        "human_time": "7m ago"
    },
    {
        "title": "Women who feared losing vital cancer treatment offered new hope",
        "link": "https://www.abc.net.au/news/2026-08-19/breast-cancer-pbs-drug-lifeline/107049936",
        "source": "Just In",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-18T20:19:42.485784+00:00",
        "image": "https://live-production.wcms.abc-cdn.net.au/50f2ec787a76be6ef1a38e006315c299?impolicy=wcms_watermark_news&cropH=432&cropW=768&xPos=0&yPos=238&width=862&height=485&imformat=generic",
        "full_content": "Breast cancer patients offered PBS repreive after removal of lifesaving drug Zoladex\nIn short:\nWomen with breast cancer who feared losing access to a critical drug Zoladex have been offered a lifeline.\nThe federal government's PBS advisory group has recommended listing an equivalent medication, triptorelin.\nWhat's next?\nThe government said it was committed to listing all medications recommended by PBAC.\nWomen with breast cancer left contemplating drastic surgery because of uncertainty over a critical drug have been offered a lifeline, with an equivalent medication on track to be subsidised.\nThe move is set to provide a long-term treatment solution to breast cancer and endometriosis patients who have grappled with uncertainty in recent months over access to the drug Zoladex.\nAs revealed by the ABC in June, AstraZeneca, the maker of the goserelin 3.6mg implant known as Zoladex, said it would pull the drug from both the PBS and private market in November.\nAn urgently convened out-of-session meeting of the Pharmaceutical Benefits Advisory Committee (PBAC) recommended the PBS listing for an equivalent medication, triptorelin, be expanded so it could be used for a number of women's health conditions.\nA health department spokesperson said the federal government was committed to listing all medications recommended by PBAC.\n\"The government lists medicines on the PBS at the earliest opportunity once recommended by PBAC, and all relevant listing requirements have been met by the pharmaceutical company,\" they said.\nIn June, AstraZeneca said the Australian price for the product, sold as Zoladex, was too low, \"making it unsustainable to supply the medicine on the PBS\".\nThe drug company initially offered a temporary access program, however later bowed to widespread pressure from advocates and patients, announcing it would provide the medication for free to eligible patients without other suitable treatment pathways.\nBreast Cancer Network Australia (BCNA) Director of Policy and Advocacy Vicki Durston said having an alternative medication like triptorelin on the PBS — rather than a separate access program run by a pharmaceutical company motivated by profits — was crucial.\n\"I think what [AstraZeneca's access program] presented is so much uncertainty,\" she said.\n\"We want to ensure… that we don't see a two-tiered model where pharmaceutical companies are running programs that sit outside those regulatory structures.\n\"And we need to ensure that [women] are protected and that there are safeguards to ensure that this doesn't happen again.\"\nCEO of advocacy group So Brave Rachelle Panitz said the last couple of months had been incredibly challenging for breast cancer patients across the country.\n\"There's a lot of anxiety and concern around, can I actually access my medicine? So I think this is a really great next step, certainly for long-term surety of access,\" she said.\n\"This will be really, really good news for a lot of patients … they will never have to worry about access to medicines and that's what we've all been hoping for.\"\nUncertainty led Katie to remove her ovaries\nZoladex, delivered monthly as a pellet injected under the skin, stops a patient's ovaries from producing oestrogen, a hormone which promotes the growth of many forms of breast cancer and can fuel endometriosis.\nThe ovarian suppression can also help preserve fertility for women with breast cancer undergoing treatment.\nThe ABC spoke to several women last month, who said they were contemplating removing their ovaries because they were scared they would die without the medication.\nKatie Thorpe, 35, has metastatic breast cancer and had been on Zoladex since 2018, before discovering it would be removed from the Australian market.\n\"I woke up to the headline in the ABC one morning and it was an absolute shock. That was the first I'd heard of that announcement,\" she said.\n\"That uncertainty and the confusion about the communication and availability really weighed on my mind.\"\nMs Thorpe, who is a consumer advocate for BCNA, had already been considering removing her ovaries, but said when AstraZeneca announced it would pull Zoladex from the Australian market, it \"cemented\" the decision.\n\"It's a key part of keeping my cancer from progressing any further in my body … it was literally part of what was keeping me alive,\" she said.\n\"At the time we did not have an alternative. It was going to be pulled from the market or have your ovaries removed.\"\nShe had the surgery, known as an oophorectomy, at the end of June.\n\"I just didn't want to take that risk, I wanted to take my health into my own hands and that was really what pushed me to have that surgery.\"\nNew medication and Zoladex 'like for like': clinician\nAssociate professor of medicine and senior oncologist Nicholas Wilcken said the recommendation that triptorelin be placed on the PBS for breast cancer patients was \"a relief.\"\nHe said the medication was equivalent to Zoladex and had also been used for a long time across the world.\n\"I would say it's like for like,\" he said.\n\"If you think about what these drugs do, their aim is to turn the ovaries off temporarily and so you can measure hormone levels and determine whether, you know, each drug is doing that appropriately and sufficiently. And the answer is that they both are.\"\nThe federal government has recently been in several protracted, high-profile stand-offs with major pharmaceutical companies, over the way the PBS operates.\nA spokesperson said the health department acknowledged AstraZeneca's move to remove Zoladex 3.6mg from the PBS had created distress for patients.\n\"We recognise that uncertainty about access to a medicine can be deeply concerning for patients and clinicians, particularly where people are considering significant treatment decisions in response.\"\nMs Thorpe said the federal government listing the alternative drug on the PBS would spare other women from the pain and uncertainty she had experienced.\n\"I know that my case is pretty extreme, but that was the reality for a lot of women who were told that their treating teams were uncertain about whether there would be treatment available and an oophorectomy or removal of ovaries would be their only option, so this is a huge relief,\" she said.\n\"I think in the future as well, we just need better communication. This caused so much frenzy and uncertainty within the community and definitely could have been handled a lot better … but having this pathway and this way forward is certainly going to offer a lot of hope.\"\nBCNA has encouraged women who are still uncertain about their treatment to reach out to them or talk to their clinician.",
        "content": "Breast cancer patients offered PBS repreive after removal of lifesaving drug Zoladex\nIn short:\nWomen with breast cancer who feared losing access to a critical drug Zoladex have been offered a lifeline.\nThe federal government's PBS advisory group has recommended listing an equivalent medication, triptorelin.\nWhat's next?\nThe government said it was committed to listing all medications recommended by...",
