        "title": "Tasmania inquiry to weigh costs, benefits of new AI data centres",
        "link": "https://www.abc.net.au/news/2026-08-19/ai-data-centre-inquiry-tasmanian-greens/107050792",
        "source": "Just In",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-18T20:19:42.485781+00:00",
        "image": "https://live-production.wcms.abc-cdn.net.au/3e03c1a0ab78b3d01febde3f6f282752?impolicy=wcms_watermark_news&cropH=2208&cropW=3925&xPos=0&yPos=81&width=862&height=485&imformat=generic",
        "full_content": "Data centre inquiry by Greens to examine 'what, if any,' benefits will come to Tasmania from rollout\nIn short:\nWith communities around the world grappling with AI data centres seeking to establish in towns and cities, the Tasmanian Greens have set up an inquiry to examine \"what, if any, economic benefit\" they will bring to the state.\nAn expert says Tasmania's \"guiding principle\" on data centres should be that they ultimately \"leave communities measurably better off\".\nWhat's next?\nSubmissions to the inquiry are open until September 21.\nA Tasmanian parliamentary committee has been set up to examine the construction and operation of AI data centres across the state in the face of rising community concern.\nThe inquiry has a broad mandate and will consider a range of factors, from how to protect public resources and the environment to making sure Tasmanians receive \"tangible benefits\" from all AI and data facilities constructed.\nData centres have been thrust into the spotlight in Tasmania in recent times with Firmus Technologies' plan to build three new factories in the state.\nThe tech company is currently constructing a $2.1 billion AI factory in the Launceston suburb of St Leonards and development applications have been lodged to build at Bell Bay in the state's north and Wesley Vale in the north-west.\nBut it faced backlash from communities with concerns ranging from noise levels to water and energy use and a lack of consultation.\nEhsan Noroozinejad, the principal researcher on Resilient Construction and Infrastructure at Western Sydney University, said it was the scale of data centres which \"creates legitimate concerns\".\nHe pointed to the \"very high electricity and water demand, pressure on household bills, diesel emissions, noise, land use and limited ongoing employment\".\n\"The key issue is ensuring private investment does not transfer costs or environmental risks to communities,\"\nDr Noroozinejad said Australia's planning and environmental laws \"were not designed for large, clustered data centres\".\n\"Australia's national expectations are positive, but many remain voluntary rather than enforceable requirements.\"\nThe Tasmanian Greens have been advocating for months to set up an inquiry into AI data centres.\nGreens technology spokesperson Tabatha Badger said it was about understanding how the laws need to change to regulate the centres through planning, construction and operation.\n\"The community have been so concerned right across Tasmania, particularly when they're seeing large AI factories pop in their backyard without adequate community consultation, consuming large amounts of energy and serious questions over water use allocation.\n\"An inquiry is what we need to ensure that we are seeing the holistic picture, that we can put forward recommendations on what guardrails might look like for Tasmania to ensure that Tasmanian people and our resources are adequately protected when we see this industry rollout and rapidly expand.\"\nMs Badger said the inquiry can consider whether there should be parliamentary oversight for the allocation of some resources, or more transparency around data centres and their use of resources and the jobs they will bring.\nAnd she said the inquiry is about ensuring data centres serve Tasmania and figuring out \"what, if any, economic benefit\" they bring to the state.\n\"What we want to ensure is that Tasmanians are getting a benefit when we're seeing AI and data factories rolled out across the state,\" she said.\n\"What scale do Tasmanians want these AI and data facilities to look like?\n\"What's actually sustainable for our island moving forward?\"\nIn the meantime, the Greens want the government to go further and pause further approvals until the laws have caught up, a call echoed by their counterparts in other states.\n\"Tasmanians are not happy at the current rate that we're seeing AI factories in particular rollout, and that's why they're calling for a moratorium.\"\nBut it is not something the Tasmanian government is interested in.\nResponding to a question in parliament from independent Peter George yesterday, Tasmania's Business, Industry and Resources Minister Felix Ellis said the government would not be considering a moratorium on new data centres.\nMr Ellis told parliament the state government would release its statement of expectations for AI data centres in the coming days.\n\"There are some significant opportunities and some challenges that come with data centre development,\" Mr Ellis said.\n\"We're already seeing data centres developed here, so that's a process that we're continuing to work through because as we scale, there are new challenges.\n\"What is different with these very large data centres … [is] very high energy use, and depending on the kinds of technology used … you can also see significant water usage.\"\nHe told parliament it was important that data centres pay their way in terms of water and energy.\nMr Ellis said some aspects of the developments could be managed under Tasmania's \"strong planning system\" and environmental regulations.\nDr Noroozinejad said Tasmania has a \"genuine opportunity to become a responsible data-centre destination\" because of its renewable-energy strengths.\n\"But renewable electricity is not unlimited\", he cautioned.\n\"The guiding principle should be simple: data centres must bring new capacity, pay their full infrastructure costs and leave communities measurably better off.\"\nSubmissions to the inquiry are open until September 21.\nThe recommendations of the inquiry will not be binding.",
        "content": "Data centre inquiry by Greens to examine 'what, if any,' benefits will come to Tasmania from rollout\nIn short:\nWith communities around the world grappling with AI data centres seeking to establish in towns and cities, the Tasmanian Greens have set up an inquiry to examine \"what, if any, economic benefit\" they will bring to the state.\nAn expert says Tasmania's \"guiding principle\" on data centres sh...",
