        "title": "Erin Patterson's bid to overturn her murder convictions begins",
        "link": "https://www.abc.net.au/news/2026-08-19/erin-patterson-mushroom-murder-appeal-explainer/107049830",
        "source": "Just In",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-18T20:19:42.485777+00:00",
        "image": "https://live-production.wcms.abc-cdn.net.au/686742dcd66405ef96ff2c2d8bf21d0f?impolicy=wcms_watermark_news&cropH=803&cropW=1428&xPos=368&yPos=284&width=862&height=485&imformat=generic",
        "full_content": "Mushroom murder appeal begins, as Erin Patterson and prosecutors mount legal challenges\nNearly 12 months after flagging an appeal, lawyers for triple-murderer Erin Patterson will today front Victoria's highest court in a bid to overturn her convictions.\nPatterson was found guilty of using poisonous mushrooms to murder relatives Don Patterson, Gail Patterson and Heather Wilkinson during one of the highest-profile trials in Australian history.\nShe was also found guilty of attempting to murder Heather's husband, Ian Wilkinson, the sole survivor of the fatal beef Wellington lunch at her house in Leongatha.\nPatterson was sentenced to life in jail, with a non-parole period of 33 years.\nIf her appeal is ultimately successful, the Court of Appeal could overturn her convictions and order a retrial.\nAs Patterson fights to clear her name, Victorian prosecutors have lodged their own legal challenge, and are calling on the Court of Appeal to strip her of the chance to ever walk free again.\nThe hearing is expected to run for two days.\nWhy is Patterson appealing?\nLast year, Patterson's legal team filed the appeal and briefly outlined seven grounds they would rely on:\n- The integrity of the verdicts was \"fatally undermined\" by a gaffe that saw jury members sequestered in the same hotel as a police witness and members of the prosecution team.\n- Phone tower evidence and evidence about death cap mushroom sightings in the nearby towns of Loch and Outtrim caused unfair prejudice and should not have been permitted in the trial.\n- The trial judge, Christopher Beale, made an error by preventing prosecutors from introducing evidence about photos and videos of mushrooms found on an SD card at Patterson's home.\n- The judge allowed evidence from Patterson's Facebook \"friends\" into the trial, causing \"unfair prejudice\" and a \"substantial miscarriage of justice\".\n- The cross-examination of Patterson by lead prosecutor Nanette Rogers SC was \"unfair and oppressive\".\n- A further miscarriage of justice occurred during her closing address to the jury.\n- Prosecutors did not outline a motive for murder at the start of the trial, but changed the legal case by later \"implying\" there was a motive.\nThose grounds will be expanded upon, both in written form and oral arguments made in court on Wednesday.\nWhy is the prosecution appealing?\nAfter Patterson was found guilty, the prosecution called on Justice Beale to impose a life sentence with no parole, a measure that would effectively see her locked up with the key thrown away.\nJustice Beale ordered the life sentence but set a non-parole period, giving Patterson a chance of leaving custody in the year 2056. By that stage, she would be aged 81.\nGiven the gravity of the crimes, the Office of Public Prosecutions argues that the setting of any parole period makes the sentence \"manifestly inadequate\".\nWhen will the decision be announced?\nIt is still unclear when a decision will be announced.\nThe first hurdle both sides will need to clear is to be granted \"leave\" for their appeals by the Court of Appeal.\nIn other words, this is permission from the judges for the appeal to be considered.\nEven though leave must be granted for an appeal to occur, the court regularly allows both the leave application and the appeal hearing itself to take place at the same time.\nIf either side is refused leave, their appeal has failed and a judgment could be delivered within weeks.\nIf leave is granted, the judges may still refuse the appeal. However, they will provide a more comprehensive judgment setting out their reasons in detail. That could take months.\nWhere is the appeal happening?\nThe appeal hearing will take place in the Supreme Court building on the corner of William and Lonsdale streets in the Melbourne CBD.\nGiven public interest in the case, the Court of Appeal has shifted the hearing from its own building to the larger Supreme Court next door.\nThe murder trial was held in the country town of Morwell, about 60 kilometres from Patterson's home in Leongatha. Her legal team had wanted the case held close to her local community.\nWho will be in the room?\nNot Erin Patterson.\nPatterson has chosen to watch proceedings from a video link in prison, instead of fronting the appeal judges in person.\nThe three judges who will rule on the appeal applications will be Justices Stephen McLeish, Lesley Taylor and Peter Kidd.\nColin Mandy SC and Sophie Stafford, Patterson's barristers from the 2025 trial, have moved on. In their place will be Richard Edney and Veronika Drago. Patterson has retained her solicitor, Bill Doogue, and senior associate Ophelia Holloway.\nThe prosecution will now be spearheaded by the director of public prosecutions, Brendan Kissane KC, and crown prosecutor Jeremy McWilliams.\nSeats in the court gallery will be made available for the general public, police, media and family members connected to the case.\nHow can I follow the appeal?\nThe Court of Appeal plans to publicly live-stream the hearing, making it available to the general public. The link will be available on the Supreme Court of Victoria website.\nA quick note: it is against the law in Victoria to take screenshots, make recordings, or republish audio or video from the stream.\nThe ABC will be live-blogging the hearing and will have news coverage on its radio, digital and television platforms.\nThe Case Of podcast, formerly known as Mushroom Case Daily, will recap both days of the hearing, with episodes available on the ABC Listen app hours after court wraps.",
        "content": "Mushroom murder appeal begins, as Erin Patterson and prosecutors mount legal challenges\nNearly 12 months after flagging an appeal, lawyers for triple-murderer Erin Patterson will today front Victoria's highest court in a bid to overturn her convictions.\nPatterson was found guilty of using poisonous mushrooms to murder relatives Don Patterson, Gail Patterson and Heather Wilkinson during one of the ...",
