        "title": "Queensland social housing waitlist drops to 44,000 with 'work to do'",
        "link": "https://www.abc.net.au/news/2026-08-19/queensland-social-housing-register-waitlist/107050360",
        "source": "Just In",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-18T20:19:42.485764+00:00",
        "image": "https://live-production.wcms.abc-cdn.net.au/d6bd323d59f3cf145675de292a57475e?impolicy=wcms_watermark_news&cropH=3069&cropW=5457&xPos=0&yPos=129&width=862&height=485&imformat=generic",
        "full_content": "Queensland social housing waitlist drops to 44,000 people as advocates call for affordable homes targets\nIn short:\nThe latest figures for July showed 44,294 people on Queensland's social housing waitlist, a drop from the 48,539 recorded in October 2024 when the LNP came to office.\nHousing Minister Sam O'Connor chalked the decrease up to a more actively managed list, and more homes coming online.\nWhat's next?\nQShelter is calling for specific targets, including for supportive and affordable housing.\nQueensland's housing minister concedes there is more work to do, with more than 44,000 people on the state's social housing waitlist.\nBut one advocate noted the register was not the only indicator of need, with a huge number of people ineligible, labelled the \"missing middle\".\nThe latest figures for July showed 44,294 people on the waitlist, a drop from the 48,539 recorded in October 2024 when the LNP came to office.\nThat number steadily rose over the following year, peaking in November 2025 at more than 59,000 people on the waitlist.\nHousing Minister Sam O'Connor said the state government had arrested the rise since then, recording a sharp drop from the start of the year.\n\"It is over 20 per cent down on that peak. So, we have been playing catch up because there hadn't been enough homes delivered,\" he said.\n\"We are going in the right direction and we're not there yet, but we are pleased with the progress that has been made.\"\nHe chalked the decrease up to a more actively managed list, and more homes coming online.\n'The missing middle'\nFiona Caniglia, chief executive of peak housing and homelessness service QShelter, noted the eligibility threshold for social housing was \"quite low\".\nThe income cap for an existing household is $80,000. For new applicants, the limit for a single person with no children is $609 weekly, and a couple $755.\n\"There are a lot of people who are struggling to either sustain a tenancy they've got, from an affordability point of view, or break into the private rental market,\" Ms Caniglia said.\n\"It's what we call the missing middle. There isn't a lot between social housing and the open private rental market for people who are relying upon renting.\n\"There's a huge amount of work to do to build not just social homes, but homes that are affordable where people have access to discounted market rents, that are well located to infrastructure and jobs.\"\nCall for set goals\nHousing was one of four central planks in the LNP's push for power, with Premier David Crisafulli making bold pledges in other key areas.\nThey included a promise to resign if victim of crime numbers did not fall by the end of the term, and to bring ambulance ramping below 30 per cent.\nBut Mr O'Connor would not commit to a hard figure he would bring the social housing waitlist down to.\n\"The target given to me by the premier was 53,500 social and community homes to be delivered by 2044,\" he said.\n\"That is the only thing that will see the register continue to reduce. That target was modelled to meet our current and future demand.\"\nMs Caniglia said while she welcomed that lofty goal, she would like to see more specificity.\n\"We need targets for supportive housing, which is for really vulnerable people where they get the support they need, and a target for affordable housing.\n\"People are simply being pushed out of better-serviced communities, into outlying areas where they're struggling even more with the impacts of poverty and disadvantage just because they're so far away from where they really need to be.\"",
        "content": "Queensland social housing waitlist drops to 44,000 people as advocates call for affordable homes targets\nIn short:\nThe latest figures for July showed 44,294 people on Queensland's social housing waitlist, a drop from the 48,539 recorded in October 2024 when the LNP came to office.\nHousing Minister Sam O'Connor chalked the decrease up to a more actively managed list, and more homes coming online.\nW...",
