        "title": "The answer to preventing falls may be as simple as exercise",
        "link": "https://www.abc.net.au/news/2026-08-19/exercise-programs-helping-to-prevent-falls-older-australians/107027424",
        "source": "Just In",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-18T20:19:42.485760+00:00",
        "image": "https://live-production.wcms.abc-cdn.net.au/5133d99f8198c89a2c50f02cd180898d?impolicy=wcms_watermark_news&cropH=1125&cropW=2000&xPos=0&yPos=104&width=862&height=485&imformat=generic",
        "full_content": "Exercise programs play a vital part in preventing falls among older Australians\nIn short:\nExperts say community intervention like exercise programs can help prevent older Australians from falling.\nFalls are the leading cause of injury requiring hospitalisation and are particularly common for older people.\nWhat's next?\nThe impact of falls on the health and wellbeing of people's lives, as well as the impact on under-pressure emergency departments, is expected to worsen as the population ages.\nFalls are the leading cause of an injury requiring hospitalisation — and with them particularly common among older people, experts warn they will only become more prevalent as the population ages.\nBut there are many things people of all ages can be doing to help prevent falls, and exercise is top of the list.\nThat message has hit home for 79-year-old grandmother Sandra Franklin, particularly since she was widowed two years ago.\nHaving to be mostly self-reliant for everything from walking the dog to doing the groceries and chores means Ms Franklin knows she has to keep her strength up, and herself mobile.\n\"You've got to keep your body fit as you get older,\"\n\"You can't go ask a shopper at the grocery to put one thing in a bag and carry that home. You need to use your muscles and keep strong.\"\nThere is also the motivation of being able to continue to pick up and hug her three-year-old granddaughter and even her seven-year-old grandson, as well as the mental health benefits of socialising and keeping connected to the community.\n'Get up and do it'\nBut it has not always been easy.\nWhen her husband Dennis passed away, Ms Franklin said she struggled.\n\"I've been on my own since then and I did find it very hard but then I sat down and had a good talk to myself and said, 'No get up and do it',\" she explained.\n\"I can do it and the only way you can do it is by keeping active mentally and physically.\"\nStrength, balance, mobility\nBased south of Brisbane, Ms Franklin is one of dozens of participants who attend a weekly free exercise class aimed at falls prevention, something she said is always in the back of her mind as she ages.\nThe class is run out of a community centre and federally funded through the Brisbane South Primary Health Network.\nCoach Julie McNaught said age and abilities levels vary, but everyone shares a common goal: increasing fitness and confidence.\nExercises focus on balance, coordination, hand-eye coordination and hand-foot coordination, as well as ankle stability, and Ms McNaught said she often receives feedback about how helpful it is with reducing stumbling and boosting everyday strength, like the ability to open a jar or peg out the laundry.\nAnd for many, the social element is just as important as the physical one.\n'The earlier the better'\nThe premise of the class is simple, but that is exactly what's needed, according to a geriatrician.\nThe University of Queensland's Professor Ruth Hubbard described exercise, particularly high-resistance and balance training, as \"the most powerful way\" to prevent falls.\n\"The earlier the better is the answer,\"\nProfessor Hubbard described hospital as an environment \"not designed for older people\" and warned there could often be long-term consequences to an admission.\nThat is because older people are likely to lose strength and conditioning during a hospital stay, she said, which can mean either an aged care admission or more care at home when they leave.\nAlready, data from the Australian Institute of Health and Welfare shows falls are the leading cause of injury requiring hospitalisation.\nIn 2024-25, fall-related injury hospitalisations were most common among people aged 75 and over. In 2023-24, fall-related injuries cost the health system about $5.4 billion.\nWith an aging population, there are concerns falls will become increasingly prevalent, leading to poorer health outcomes across the population and extra pressure on already stretched emergency departments.\nDr Terry Nash sees that every day.\n\"It's one of the most common reasons that someone, particularly an older person, will need emergency medicine care in Australia,\" he explained.\nThe emergency physician said while most falls which result in a minor injury can be dealt with in a \"straightforward\" manner, that was not the case for everyone.\n\"There's also a small cohort of older people who are frail where a fall can be the sentinel event that means that person needs more support before they can return home.\"\nDr Nash said community intervention to help prevent falls was key to reducing demands on emergency departments.\n\"Engaging in programs that mean that you're less likely to fall is a huge personal benefit, and I think a benefit to the whole healthcare system,\" he said.\n\"It's never too late to be doing anything that keeps you active and engaged.\"\nOther elements that can help prevent falls include home modifications such as removing trip factors, ensuring vision and eyesight is good (through access to hearing aids an eyewear), good nutrition and being on the correct medication.",
        "content": "Exercise programs play a vital part in preventing falls among older Australians\nIn short:\nExperts say community intervention like exercise programs can help prevent older Australians from falling.\nFalls are the leading cause of injury requiring hospitalisation and are particularly common for older people.\nWhat's next?\nThe impact of falls on the health and wellbeing of people's lives, as well as th...",
