        "image": "https://i.cbc.ca/ais/0c6c3def-5436-4bfe-8203-75fae280484e,1787066037710/full/max/0/default.jpg?im=Crop%2Crect%3D%280%2C417%2C7996%2C4497%29%3BResize%3D620",
        "full_content": "Evacuation order lifted for majority of Summerland, B.C., with about 150 structures believed damaged\nMost residents can return, but Garnet Valley, Prairie Valley and Meadow Valley remain under order\nAfter more than a week of stress and anxiety, most residents of Summerland, B.C., can return to their homes.\n“It’s the news we’ve all been waiting for for the last week,\" Mayor Doug Holmes told CBC's Daybreak South.\nThe community of more than 12,000 was forced to flee after the Bald Range wildfire broke out on Aug. 7 and grew explosively, claiming the life of an 80-year-old woman.\nHolmes said an evacuation order was lifted for 3,380 properties at 7 a.m. PT Tuesday, what he called \"the majority of Summerland.\"\nOrders for a smaller number of properties were lifted over the weekend.\nAs of Tuesday, 530 properties remain under an evacuation order in the Garnet Valley, Prairie Valley and Meadow Valley.\nAll other Summerland residents remain under evacuation alert and must still be ready to leave at a moment's notice.\nHolmes said those areas remain unsafe until geotechnical experts can test slope stability above the communities, and that Garnet Valley remains without power.\n\"I know there was a lot of frustration, people waiting, I was feeling frustrated myself,\" he said.\n\"But we had to do it properly, and orderly, and this is the big day.”\nA boil water advisory remains in place due to the community's water treatment plant going offline during the evacuation.\nThe community's landfill is also still on fire, meaning trash will need to be trucked to a facility operated by the regional district.\nOn Monday, the Regional District of Okanagan-Similkameen said an initial assessment found the fire had damaged or destroyed about 150 structures.\nAbout 40 of those structures were within the municipality's boundaries.\nHolmes said people who had lost property were contacted by social workers and counsellors, and given the chance to see their homes before the order was lifted.\n\"We just thought it was really important that people were able to go and see their properties, that had lost their homes, before we opened it up to all of their neighbours,\" he said.\nAs of Tuesday, the 214-square-kilometre fire remained classified as out of control.\nFirefighters have successfully held the fire behind control lines on the southern and eastern flanks, and have turned their attention to the fire's west side where it continues to show aggressive behaviour.",
