        "title": "Another duty to warn letter casts doubt on Carney's rapprochement with India",
        "link": "https://www.cbc.ca/news/politics/sikh-activist-threat-rcmp-india-9.7311154?cmp=rss",
        "source": "CBC | Top Stories News",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-18T19:44:08.471087+00:00",
        "image": "https://i.cbc.ca/ais/cb6ad01b-a0e9-48e5-89f9-0cc6e9b3973c,1763830948476/full/max/0/default.jpg?im=Crop%2Crect%3D%280%2C0%2C1920%2C1080%29%3BResize%3D620",
        "full_content": "Another duty to warn letter casts doubt on Carney's rapprochement with India\nCarney government says India has stopped its threats, but RCMP continues to warn India's critics of danger\nAnother Sikh Canadian activist has been issued a \"duty to warn\" letter, casting further doubt on the Carney government's claims that it has dealt with the issue of Indian government foreign interference and transnational repression.\nInderjeet Singh Gosal says he received the letter on Friday, Aug. 14, after RCMP contacted him by phone in Caledon, Ont.\nDuty to warn letters are issued by police when they possess credible intelligence that there is a threat to the life of a citizen. They typically do not provide information on the nature of the threat.\n\"They said there was a threat to my life and that they needed to see me immediately to provide me with that duty to warn in writing,\" Gosal told CBC News.\nCBC News has seen the letter, which is signed by two RCMP members and by \"Intended Victim\" Inderjeet Singh Gosal. (The targets of duty to warn letters are asked to sign to acknowledge receipt of the warning.)\nGosal said the RCMP verbally repeated an earlier offer to enrol him in a witness protection program, although he is not a witness in any criminal proceedings.\n\"They said that offer still stands and they were pushing towards that. But I respectfully declined because I would rather take India's bullet than stop campaigning for the Khalistan referendum,\" he said.\nKhalistan is the name Sikh separatists give to their proposed independent Sikh homeland in India's Punjab region. A series of non-binding referendums held by the group Sikhs for Justice in Sikh diaspora communities around Europe, North America and Australia since 2021 has angered the Indian government.\n- Carney government says India threat is over — Sikh activists say not so fast\n- AnalysisSenior RCMP officer's 'no evidence' comment about India seems to contradict past statements\nHardeep Singh Nijjar was leading the Canadian referendum campaign as deputy to international leader Gurpatwant Singh Pannun when he was killed in Surrey, B.C., in June 2023.\nPannun has since appointed Gosal to replace Nijjar as leader of the referendum campaign in Canada. Gosal told CBC News that this latest letter is his sixth official police warning.\nWarnings keep coming\nThe letter is one of a number that have been issued by Canadian police since the Carney government began its rapprochement and normalization process with the Narendra Modi government.\nRelations between Canada and India hit a low point following the assassination of Nijjar, and allegations from the Trudeau government that the hit had been ordered by New Delhi to suppress the Khalistani movement overseas.\nBoth countries saw dramatic reductions in their diplomatic representations following tit-for-tat expulsions.\nThe RCMP also held an unprecedented news conference in August 2024 to warn of an Indian government-inspired campaign of criminal violence across Canada.\nMore recently, the Carney government has shown eagerness to move on from the dispute and focus on growing commercial and investment links with India. Canadian officials have said they are satisfied by assurances from the Indian government that it won't engage in illegal activities or foreign interference in Canada.\nAnd yet duty to warn letters have continued to be issued. The letter issued to Gosal did not mention the government of India, but merely said \"the police have determined that a threat has been made against your life. At this time, we are unable to provide you any further, specific details of the threat.\"\nUN asking questions\nCanada has also faced questions from five UN rapporteurs, skeptical about its claims that it has addressed the issue of threats to Canadian citizens of Sikh origin, and focusing in particular on the case of British Columbia activist Moninder Singh who, like Inderjeet Singh Gosal, in involved in the referendum effort that Nijjar was part of when he died.\nCanada has assured the rapporteurs that it takes it duty to protect its citizens seriously. But those who feel like targets of the Indian government are not assured, and point to the duty to warn letters as evidence that the government knows the threat continues.\n\"I obviously appreciate the RCMP for performing their statutory duty and informing me about these active death threats,\" Gosal said.\nBut Gosal was critical of the Carney government.\n\"How can they justify resuming aggressive business and bilateral diplomatic outreach to India while the Modi regime continues to actively plan extrajudicial assassinations on Canadian citizens on Canadian soil?\"\nIndia has denied involvement with acts of violence in Canada, including the Nijjar killing.\n\"My decision hasn't changed. I'm going to continue. Oct. 18 is the next referendum, taking place in Edmonton, Alberta, and I'm going to continue organizing that and we'll go from there,\" Gosal said.\n\"This is the exact democratic exercise that the Indian government is trying to suppress through transnational repression. So I know I'm a target for that, but that's not going to stop me.\"",
        "content": "Another duty to warn letter casts doubt on Carney's rapprochement with India\nCarney government says India has stopped its threats, but RCMP continues to warn India's critics of danger\nAnother Sikh Canadian activist has been issued a \"duty to warn\" letter, casting further doubt on the Carney government's claims that it has dealt with the issue of Indian government foreign interference and transnati...",
