        "title": "Wildfire watch: Inside London’s fire control centre on the hottest day of the year",
        "link": "https://www.telegraph.co.uk/global-health/climate-and-people/londons-fire-control-centre-on-the-hottest-day-of-the-year/",
        "source": "www.telegraph.co.uk for the latest news from the UK and around the world.",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-18T19:44:08.470434+00:00",
        "image": "https://www.telegraph.co.uk/content/dam/global-health/2026/08/14/TELEMMGLPICT000498244672_17867112121410_trans_NvBQzQNjv4BqqVzuuqpFlyLIwiB6NTmJwSX5rhseiWKOo9p9OQ-ymek.jpeg?impolicy=OG-Standard",
        "full_content": "“It’s been relentless, an immense number of calls,” says Yvonne Woodhams, control room commander at the London Fire Brigade’s headquarters on the morning of what turned out to be the hottest day of the year so far.\nInside a nondescript building in South-west London, dozens of fire brigade dispatchers field calls from the public and direct the city’s 5,000 or so firefighters across 102 stations to where they are most needed.\nIn the middle of another heatwave and on a day when temperatures rose to just above 38 degrees, the control room was busy – the scale and frequency of wildfires in London’s parks and grasslands are now considered unprecedented.\nAbove the dispatchers there is a live map, with red dots representing each active fire, sprawling from Enfield in the north to Purley in the south.\nWildfires nearly always have a human origin like dropped cigarette butts, glass bottles that magnifies the sunlight, or the now-temporarily banned portable barbecues.\nBut it’s the tinderbox that London has become which is worrying firefighters.\nControl Commander Yvonne Woodhams speaks with a colleague taking calls\nCredit: Simon Townsley/The Telegraph\nJordan Mitsoullis, Senior Control Commander\nCredit: Simon Townsley/The Telegraph\nWhile The Telegraph visited the London control centre, this was playing out in other parts of the country. A grass fire in Stourbridge in the West Midlands set homes ablaze, hospitalising at least 19 people, including a child, the terrifying scenes becoming front-page news.\nLondon itself is no stranger to the phenomenon. In the scorching summer of 2022, when daytime temperatures hit 40 degrees for the first time, more than 40 houses combusted in a matter of minutes as more than a dozen wildfires broke out across the capital.\n“Extreme hot weather is absolutely a more frequent and regular risk for the London Fire Brigade now,” said Jordan Mitsoullis, a senior control commander at LFB.\n“July was significantly busier than normal, one of our busiest months. We’ve taken north of 27,500 calls, and that is a huge amount,” Mr Mitsoullis said, adding that the figure is roughly 40 per cent more than what they are used to.\nTemperatures are on course to hit record highs across Britain this summer.\nMore than 21,000 hectares of land have already burned this year as of late July, while England and Wales recorded their driest July in nearly two centuries.\nThe trend is being repeated across Europe. The continent is warming twice as fast as anywhere else on earth, and this summer has already seen mass evacuations from raging fires in places like Bordeaux, Cap Ferrat, and Madrid.\nFirefighters survey the remains of a house set ablaze by a grass fire in Stourbridge\nCredit: Jacob King/PA Wire\nBy 3pm, on Thursday, the London dispatch team had already taken more than 1,000 calls, a large chunk of which were related to grass fires.\nFirefighters had been dispatched to parks and gardens all over the city, including Mitcham, Bexleyheath, Rainham, Southall, and West Drayton.\nThe fire brigade pride themselves on attending an incident in no less than six minutes.\nWhile no London wildfires have yet resulted in fatalities, the health effects of wildfires – even for those living miles away – can also be significant.\nSmoke from such blazes, which are made up of a mixture of carbon monoxide, nitrogen oxides, volatile organic compounds and dangerous hydrocarbons, can travel far and penetrate deep into the human lungs and bloodstream.\nThese particles, known as PM2.5, have been linked to an increased risk of respiratory and cardiovascular disease and premature death.\nA major study tracking the global impact of the 2023 Canadian wildfires found that smoke from wildfires raised PM2.5 levels in Europe to hazardous levels, and estimated that as many as 22,400 premature deaths were caused by long-range exposure to the smoke.\nHigh temperatures have also been linked to more than 2,877 excess deaths in England in May and June alone, mostly from heat stress.\nThe LFB control room\nCredit: Simon Townsley/The Telegraph\nThe LFB is rapidly adapting to try and meet the threat.\n“We’ve had to invest in new equipment, new training specifically designed for grass fires and wildfires, including doing hot-weather training abroad in places like Spain,” said Mr Mitsoullis.\nAmong the brigade’s new kit are a fleet of 4x4 Terrain Support Vehicles (TSVs) – smaller, compact fire engines that are able to reach parks and woodlands that larger trucks cannot.\n“The terrain support vehicles are specifically designed to carry about 475 litres of water. In terms of access, they can absolutely access the grass fire a lot easier than a fire engine can. The TSVs are much smaller, more agile, so they can actually access the fire,” said Mr Mistsoullis.\nA major issue the force has to grapple with is the safety of their workers in extreme heat.\nLast week, rules were relaxed to allow firefighters to tackle smaller-scale, outdoor fires without their heavy fire suits on, working just in their standard LFB t-shirts.\nBut full fire kits, which include heavy-duty helmets, tunics, and over-trousers, must still be worn during larger-scale and indoor fires to prevent severe burns and contaminant risks.\nDispatchers take calls at the control room\nCredit: Simon Townsley/The Telegraph\nOn Thursday, the Fire Brigade Union’s (FBU) general secretary, Steven Wright, warned that UK firefighters have been put under enormous pressure as they tackle an “unprecedented summer of dangerous and volatile wildfires.\n“Recruiting 5,000 more firefighters must be an urgent starting point, alongside providing the equipment and resources that our members require to meet these growing challenges safely and effectively,” he added.\nClimate modelling suggests that hot weather – and its subsequent effects – will now become a fact of life in Britain.\nThe dispatchers at LFB feel it is their duty to soldier on.\n“Even when they’re tired, they just carry on; they come in and support us when it’s busy, they work overtime; they come back and do 12-hour shift after 12-hour shift; it’s an amazing feat, really,” said Ms Woodhams.\nAs the clock strikes 11am, we are quickly ushered out – the hottest part of the day is about to hit, and London’s parks, squares, and gardens are all at risk, and the dispatchers need to focus.",
