        "title": "Ebola doctors ‘step back’ from WHO in drug trial ethics row",
        "link": "https://www.telegraph.co.uk/global-health/science-and-disease/ebola-doctors-step-back-from-who-in-drug-trial-ethics-row/",
        "source": "www.telegraph.co.uk for the latest news from the UK and around the world.",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-18T19:44:08.470429+00:00",
        "image": "https://www.telegraph.co.uk/content/dam/global-health/2026/08/14/TELEMMGLPICT000490058618_17867047361210_trans_NvBQzQNjv4BqRo0U4xU-30oDveS4pXV-Vv4Xpit_DMGvdp2n7FDd82k.jpeg?impolicy=OG-Standard",
        "full_content": "Volunteers put on personal protective equipment to recover the body of a three-year-old child, presumed to have died of Ebola in Bunia\nCredit: BENEDICTION MURHABAZI/AFP via Getty Images\nSarah is based in Bangkok, having reported from more than 20 countries since first joining the desk in London. She covers issues such as infectious disease, insecurity and development policy, with a focus on Asia-Pacific\nPublished 14 August 2026 2:21pm BST\nTwo leading doctors have “stepped back” from long-held positions with the World Health Organization amid a row over the ethics of an Ebola drug trial underway in the Democratic Republic of Congo.\nThe randomised control trial, called Ebo-Pep, is testing whether an experimental antiviral is an effective post-exposure treatment.\nBut experts believe the study’s design puts healthcare workers on the frontline of the epidemic at risk, because it means medics exposed to Ebola might receive a placebo treatment.\n“We owe them [healthcare workers] a duty of care to do the best possible thing, and a 50 per cent chance of getting a placebo tablet is not that,” said Prof Tom Fletcher, director of the Global Health Trials Unit at the Liverpool School of Tropical Medicine. The WHO’s health emergencies clinical unit has also deployed him to Ebola ‘Red Zones’ for more than a decade.\nThis week, Prof Fletcher and eight other experts based in the UK, US, Uganda, Rwanda, Sierra Leone and Canada published their concerns in Nature Africa, calling for a “careful reconsideration” of the trial protocol.\nThey said they are not against Ebo-Pep or randomised trials, but warned there are “additional ethical obligations” when including healthcare workers in studies like this, given the case fatality rate currently stands at 40 per cent.\nProf Fletcher said: “Ebo-Pep is a really good trial, it’s the trial we’ve talked about doing for years. But we’re talking about [protecting] the ‘one-per-centers’, the people who willingly step into one of the most dangerous environments in the world.”\nProf Tom Fletcher (left) and a Ugandan colleague in PPE as they head into a treatment centre\nCredit: via Tom Fletcher\nYet the trial’s investigators said some additional risk is unavoidable and the protocol has been approved by regulators. They added that a rigorous study is the only way to get answers quickly, and that those participating are doing so voluntarily, having given informed consent.\n“I think it’s important to remain scientific, to avoid any emotional reaction for the moment,” said Placide Mbala, director of Research, Clinical Trials and Innovation at Africa Centre for Disease Control and Prevention (CDC) and a principal investigator of Ebo-Pep.\nThe row highlights the difficulties involved in designing and running trials during a medical emergency.\nSince the outbreak was declared in eastern DRC in April, more than 4,587 people have been infected and 2,130 people have died, making it the country’s largest epidemic in history. By August 1, at least 151 healthcare workers had died.\nThe Ebola species currently spreading, called Bundibugyo, is relatively uncommon, and there are currently no approved vaccines or treatments available. Scientists are racing to launch clinical trials to help contain the virus, which is currently outpacing the response.\nThe Ebo-Pep trial began in mid-July. It is testing a drug called obeldesivir – an oral version of the antiviral remdesivir – to determine whether it offers protection for people who have been exposed to Ebola, but are yet to develop symptoms.\nIf effective, it could have a “transformative” impact on outbreaks, providing a tool to halt severe disease and break transmission chains in communities where the virus is spreading.\nTom Fletcher, right, with William Fischer, centre, and a colleague on site at a previous Ebola outbreak in Uganda\nCredit: via Tom Fletcher\nBut Prof Fletcher and Prof William Fischer – another Ebola consultant who has been working in the ‘Red Zone’ with the WHO since the devastating West Africa Ebola epidemic – have stepped back from deployments with the UN agency, because they believe Ebo-Pep puts medics at too much risk.\nThey are especially concerned about one definition of a “high risk exposure” in the protocol: a needlestick injury with a syringe contaminated by the blood of a confirmed or probable Ebola patient.\nSometimes frontline health workers operating around the clock have to work through power cuts and PPE shortages. But even in well-funded treatment centres with stringent infection control measures, accidents can happen while changing IV catheters or running diagnostics.\nProf Fletcher said he is frustrated because although obeldesivir is an experimental drug, it is “not totally unproven”. It has worked well in animal studies, and experience from past outbreaks suggests antivirals have a positive impact when given in the early stages of disease.\nOver the last decade, a status quo has also emerged where healthcare workers exposed to Ebola are immediately offered an antiviral treatment or monoclonal antibody, if it is available, in the hope of giving them the best possible chance of survival.\nBut if they are included in Ebo-Pep, their chance of receiving obeldesivir drops to 50 per cent.\nMeanwhile any foreign doctor deployed to support the DRC will still be evacuated and offered an antiviral after a needlestick exposure – three already have been in this outbreak – creating an “unacceptable double standard”.\nThere is also some evidence that the mortality rate is higher among those who contract Ebola via a needlestick injury, said Prof Fischer, who is also the director of Emerging Pathogens at the University of North Carolina School of Medicine, and recently returned from three weeks in eastern DRC.\n“The argument really revolves around this appreciation of differential risk,” he said. “Direct needle stick infections have two really important things: one is that there’s a higher viral load, two is that the infection goes [directly] into the bloodstream.”\nProf Fischer added that it is unlikely the trial will be impacted if healthcare workers with needlestick injuries are excluded, as it aims to find out if obeldesivir works to reduce transmission and disease severity in communities, not treatment centres.\n“Overall this is going to represent a really small number of people, and excluding them will not impact the scientific validity of the study,” Prof Fischer said.\n“[Ebo-Pep] could fundamentally transform how we respond to outbreaks, and I want to make sure that the trial is successful. We’re just advocating for consideration of a higher risk group,” he added.\nA patient in the red zone of the Ebola treatment centre at Rwampara General Reference Hospital, Ituri Province\nCredit: JOSPIN MWISHA/AFP via Getty Images\nThe randomised control trial gives half of participants the drug, and the other half a placebo, before tracking them for 21 days.\nIt aims to enrol a total of 800 people and, in the first fortnight, 19 people signed up, said Dr Mbala, who is also the director of Clinical Research Center at the National Institute of Biomedical Research (INRB) in Kinshasa. This includes at least six healthcare workers.\n“I think it’s very important to include healthcare workers,” Dr Mbala said. “When we talk about post-exposure prophylaxis, we have to consider all of the people that are exposed – the families taking care of relatives, the people doing safe and dignified burials, the priests and traditional healers, and motorcycle drivers who carry patients to Ebola treatment centres.”\nHe added: “That’s why we think this study will help us. If we are able to show that obeldesivir helps to prevent new infections, then we are able to use them for everyone… if it doesn’t work, we don’t need to give it to people.”\nBut other researchers who have spent time in the DRC, and spoke to the Telegraph anonymously, said it is not just healthcare workers who are being let down by the Ebo-Pep trial design.\nThey said that, given the incredibly high death rate, offering only a placebo to half of trial participants is ethically dubious. They argued that another promising but expensive monoclonal antibody, an IV treatment called MPV 134, should have been tested beside obeldesivir instead, to give everyone involved in the trial the best chance of survival.\nHealth workers lower the coffin of a suspected Ebola victim into the ground at a cemetery in Bunia\nCredit: JOSPIN MWISHA/AFP via Getty Images\nAlongside INRB, the trial is being led by Africa CDC, the DRC’s Ministry of Health, the French agency ANRS Emerging Infectious Diseases, and the NGO Alima.\nDr Marie Jaspard, another of the trial’s principle investigators, said: “Ebo-Pep is a randomised control trial that evaluates a drug that [does not have a proven] efficacy… The trial has been approved by [the] ethics committee in DRC who validate the design as it is.”\nA spokesperson for the WHO, which is not an implementing partner, said: “In this setting, and as with all clinical research, the individual is free to decline participation in the trial. In that scenario whatever is available for compassionate use... should be made available for the individual. The choice is ultimately with the individual.”\nOverall, the row shines a spotlight on the ethical and logistical debates involved when developing and deploying critical clinical trials in outbreak settings, said Dr Menelas Nkeshimana, head of the Health Workforce Development at Rwanda’s Ministry of Health.\nDr Nkeshimana was the principal investigator of a study looking at obeldesivir that began in Rwanda when Marburg emerged – like Ebola, the pathogen is a filovirus. That outbreak was rapidly contained, before the trial could start enrolment to determine the drug’s efficacy.\n“Although the current Bundibugyo outbreak is a disaster, it also provides an opportunity to test these assumptions under real-world conditions,” he said. “Because this prophylaxis is oral, it is technically very possible to deploy it rapidly and at scale.\n“But the debate shows how difficult it is to conduct trials rapidly during outbreaks while balancing science, ethics, logistics, national ownership, and public trust.”",
