        "title": "How to have the perfect slow-paced holiday in Zakynthos",
        "link": "https://www.telegraph.co.uk/travel/destinations/europe/greece/zakynthos/articles/zante-travel-guide/",
        "source": "www.telegraph.co.uk for the latest news from the UK and around the world.",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-18T19:44:08.470426+00:00",
        "image": "https://www.telegraph.co.uk/content/dam/travel/2021/04/06/GettyImages-167150012.jpg?impolicy=OG-Standard",
        "full_content": "Heidi Fuller-love is a travel writer and has lived in a dozen countries, including Argentina, Cambodia and France – where she owned a boutique hotel – before settling in Greece. She has lived there for the past 15 years and speaks Greek, French and Spanish. Heidi has covered travel, food, history and lifestyle for the past 27 years. She has been shortlisted for countless travel writing awards and won double gold at the AITO Awards. Heidi is the Telegraph's Greece destination and hotels expert and travels widely. She also has a podcast, Meet The Locals. She can be contacted via her website.\nPublished 14 August 2026 4:00pm BST\nZakynthos, also known as Zante, is renowned for raucous nightlife, but there’s a far more authentic side to the Venetians “Fiore di Levante” (Flower of the East). For such a small island, scenery is extremely varied: the southern coastline – home to Laganas’ notorious nightlife strip – also encompasses the pristine beaches of Zakynthos Marine Park, where loggerhead sea turtles come to nest, while some of the island’s best-known attractions, including Navagio Beach on the cliff-lined northerly coast, can only be reached by boat.\nBirthplace of the man who penned Greece’s national anthem, Zakynthos was also a magnet for literary greats: Byron lay in state here and philhellene Edgar Allan Poe wrote a sonnet to the island’s “verdant slopes”.\nLast but not least, food is a powerful lure here: sit in a rustic sea view tavern sipping local Verdea white wine with a plate full of traditional kouneli stifado – a garlic and tomato-soaked rabbit stew – and you’ll be hooked.\nZakynthos is famous for sweet treats: start the day with an ice café frappe and a creamy, lemon-doused slice of frigania served with spectacular views at Latas Café in Bochali. Back in town follow (semi-pedestrianised) Roma Street to flower-lined Solomos Square (browsing shops en route).\nMost of the city’s original Italianate architecture was destroyed by a massive earthquake in 1953, but some buildings remain – admire the honeyed 16th-century façade of the Agios Nicholas of Molos Church and pop into the handsome, arcaded Byzantine Museum which is home to icons, frescoes and other pre-quake artefacts.\nNext visit Saint Markos Square. Maybe it’s not as grand as the original in Venice, but this paved square surrounded by pollen yellow buildings (where the Popolaroi, rebelling against the hated Cittadini, burnt the Libro D`Oro in 1797) is a great place to sip a glass of local sherry-like Verdea, before ploughing through the fascinating hotchpotch of paintings, sculptures, manuscripts at the Solomos Museum dedicated to the poet who penned Greece’s national anthem.\nHistoric Zante town is dotted with Italianate and Byzantine architecture\nCredit: starpik\nAfternoon\nThere are half a dozen wineries on Zakynthos, but I’m a big fan of icon artist Giannis Giatras’ family estate Ktima Gouma – reached via a road that ambles through citrus groves and mountain villages slumbering on pine-studded slopes – which produces some of the best Heptanese varieties, including refreshing golden Robola whites and complex spiced and flowery Avgoustiatis reds.\nGiannis’ wines slip down easily with those spicy lado tyri cheese meze, but don’t drink too much: it’s a (nail-bitingly) picturesque route to Cape Skinari, homeport for a fleet of glass-bottom boats that make the three-hour round trip to the Blue Caves (elephant-legged limestone arches reflected in cyan blue waters) and Navagio, the beach where MV Panayiotis (one of the globe’s most-photographed shipwrecks) washed up in fierce storms in the 1980s.\nBack on dry land, intrepid drivers could cut across the island for sunset views and snacks at Stavros, a rustic taverna with half a dozen tables scattered across a rocky outcrop high above the sea.\nIf you’re a nervous driver (like me), you’ll prefer Diachroniko where hearty dishes, whose the ingredients of which are sourced in the Plessas family’s farm, easily make up for the (sylvan rather than spectacular) views.\nThe spectacular limestone arches of the Blue Caves\nCredit: darios44\nLate\nSoak up a big dose of local lifestyle at family-run Popolaro in the tiny mountain village of Ampelokoipi. Order a jug of vareli krasiou (wine from the barrel) and listen to guitar and mandolin ring out their accompaniment to the island’s traditional folk song kandathes.\nDay two: morning\nZakynthos Marine Park is the Mediterranean’s largest breeding ground for massive, loggerhead turtles (they can weigh as much as 450kgs). Agios Sostis’s tiny port is the departure point for Nefis Travels eco-friendly VIP boat trips. Tours (leaving at 8.30am and returning around 2pm) are slightly more expensive than most, but worth it: boats are small (9-10 people) and qualified guides dish out plenty of fascinating anecdotes about these endangered marine creatures as you get up close (without disturbing them).\nWith the sun high in the sky, cross the wonky wooden bridge to Cameo island opposite the port at Agios Sostis. Buy a keyring and admire the stunning views, before driving through silver-leaved olive groves for a (late) lunch of brine-fresh seafood – sat at a checked cloth-covered table with views over to the green whale-hump of Marathonissi island – at family-run Votsalo tavern.\nZakynthos’ Marine Park is a breeding ground for loggerhead turtles\nCredit: MATIJA KEBER/CasarsaGuru\nAfternoon\nBurn off those calories with a five-mile (eight-kilometre) climb from Kalamaki village to Panagia Skopiotissa, the island’s oldest monastery – as its name suggests its position atop of Skopos mountain ensures blindingly beautiful views of Laganas Bay far below.\nThere’s not much left of the monastery – but be sure to ask for the key to the church in the last café on the way up to see the marvellously gilded 13th-century icon of sad-eyed Panagia Skopiotissa.\nBack in Zante Town head for Spartakos on the limani (port) where fishermen park their colourful kaiki fishing boats. This is a no-frills restaurant, so do as locals do and follow owner Spartakos into the kitchen to see what’s on the lineup – cooked fresh by his wife – before making your choice. Sit at one of the wobbly tables on the bamboo shaded terrace and enjoy garides saganaki (shrimp baked in a tomato and feta sauce) or kalamari gemisto (oven-baked squid stuffed with a succulent blend of seafood and rice) as you watch boats bob below in the harbour.\nPanagia Skopiotissa is the island’s oldest monastery – and certainly worth the trek\nCredit: ARTUR KOTOWSKI/CCat82\nLate\nFar from Laganas’ rowdy nightlife scene, but still lively, the popular resort of Argassi is where locals come to hang out in sidewalk cafés and discuss the state of the world, before hitting the clubs.\nIf you still have some energy around midnight, follow the local crowd to the palm tree shaded garden of popular local club Cebu and sip handcrafted cocktails to the sounds of local and international DJs.\nCebu is one of Zante's many lively nightlife venues\nCredit: YUSEF/YUSEF\nTemperatures are balmy all year round along Zakynthos’s extensive coastline, but direct flights from the UK are seasonal, usually running from April to early November. It will often rain or snow from January to March. Weather up in the mountains is colder: expect chilly weather from late December to early May.\nConversely, in summer when temperatures along the coast climb to 30-35C, the mountain villages – which are often five degrees cooler – are ideal for escaping the heat. In late spring and early autumn beaches and attractions are generally far less crowded. Sea temperatures start to rise in late May and you can often still swim in October.\nOverlooking a waterfall-fed infinity pool and a small shingle beach, Lesante Blu is buried in mature gardens near the low-key resort of Tragaki. The adults-only resort is home to 92 spacious open-plan suites – some with whirlpool baths or private pools – which have sheer white walls and wood parquet flooring. One Royal Grand suite on two levels also has a good-sized pool.\nAdults-only hotel Lesante Blu is a haven of peace on the Zante coast\nCredit: Christos Drazos Photography. All rights reserved.\nDesigner digs\nLesante Cape stands on the wild and wooded slopes of Akrotiri peninsula an easy four-kilometre drive from Zante town. This stylish spa resort’s highlights include a three-tiered infinity pool and spectacular views to the Peloponnese coastline opposite, while light-filled villas and suites clustered around a village-style main square (complete with traditional kafeneion) have whirlpool baths or private pools.\nLesante Cape offers light-filled villas and suites with spectacular views to the Peloponnese coastline opposite\nBudget beauty\nLogothetis Organic Farm is located within hiking distance of the island’s best beaches. Angela and Dionysos are the third generation to work this sprawling organic farm. Seven homely stone-built houses surrounded by silver-leaved olive groves have exposed beams, flagstone floors and views over the meadows where horses graze and chickens lay the eggs you’ll eat for breakfast.\nJet2, Easyjet and British Airways operate direct flights to Zakynthos’s Dionysios Solomos airport from nine UK destinations, including Manchester, Glasgow and London. The airport is only four miles (six kilometres) from Zakynthos Town and flights from London take three hours 50 minutes. Taxis and buses leave from the arrivals area. Buses also run regularly to most parts of the island and tickets cost £2-3. You’ll need a car to explore more remote regions. One day rental with local company Smart Rentals costs from £50 (€58) per day.\nCultural etiquette\nZakynthians are relaxed about sartorial style, but they appreciate modest clothing (no shorts or crop tops) when visiting churches and other religious places – and you are expected to remove headgear when entering.\nNude sunbathing near local families is considered extremely rude – and is only acceptable on designated beaches.\nGreek hospitality workers receive a pittance in wages so do tip where you can – even a few euros will be warmly welcomed",
