        "title": "Raul Jimenez’s late penalty seals share of spoils for Wolves against Blackburn",
        "link": "https://www.telegraph.co.uk/football/2026/08/14/wolves-vs-blackburn-championship-score-latest-updates-live/",
        "source": "www.telegraph.co.uk for the latest news from the UK and around the world.",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-18T19:44:08.470422+00:00",
        "image": "https://www.telegraph.co.uk/content/dam/football/2026/08/14/TELEMMGLPICT000498380934_17867411668030_trans_NvBQzQNjv4BqbZ8LU-qv1sVXVl2BVMJBc4r4OrsmXGYYl75LDT78nrM.jpeg?impolicy=OG-Live",
        "full_content": "Raul Jimenez’s late penalty seals share of spoils for Wolves against Blackburn\nAn injury-time penalty from returning hero Raul Jimenez ensured Wolves came away with a point in their Championship opener after Blackburn had produced a comeback.\nWolves led through Marshall Munetsi’s cool finish on 18 minutes only for Sean McLoughlin’s first Blackburn goal to draw the visitors level on 27 minutes with a well-timed run and header.\nThe visitors turned the game around on 48 minutes through Andri Gudjohnsen’s close-range strike only for substitute Jimenez to level the game three minutes into injury time from the spot after Ryan Alebiosu handled the ball in the box. Jimenez and his wife marked his return to Molineux by giving 5,000 fans black scarves emblazoned with the words ‘Si Senor’ - a nod to his song - and Jimenez did not disappoint as he rifled in his penalty.\nCesar Peixoto wants Wolves to play with their heads rather than their hearts after the home side rescued a late point in the first game of the Championship season following relegation. Wolves replaced Rob Edwards with Portuguese coach Peixoto during the summer and he felt his side’s loss of organisation allowed Blackburn to take control of the game.\nPeixoto said: “One point is not what we want, we want three points. We lost some organisation in the second half and played more with our hearts and not our heads and brains.\n“We have to keep our mental stability during the 90 minutes because we’re better when we’re organised. We need to play our way with our identity. It’s a lesson for us to understand when we lose organisation it’s more difficult for us and more easy for the opposition.\n“We’re working hard to bring more solutions [into the club] but we need to work hard with the players we have. It’s the first game, there’s a lot to work on and a lot to improve.”\nMowbray: It feels like a defeat\nTony Mowbray took charge of Blackburn in the league for the first time since returning to the club for a second stint in the dugout. Rovers recorded their lowest league finish since Mowbray’s first stint in charge of the club last term but the Blackburn boss is confident his side have enough to beat the drop and climb up the table this campaign.\nMowbray said: “Ultimately, it feels like a defeat, it’s a bit disappointing and a bit frustrating. I just felt a lot of decisions were starting to swing their way. It’s harsh to take, I don’t think they deserved a second goal. We asked the players to be brave and we were collectively very strong.\n“Everybody has us relegated, probably on the back of finishing 20th last season and losing a couple of our better players and not spending much money, which is a good motivation for the team. We deserved to win the game, we showed there’s enough quality to give teams problems and I hope there’s help on the way [in the transfer window].”\nThe first chance of the season came the way of Blackburn as a cross-field pass found Alebiosu down the right, who floated a ball into the box only for an unmarked Oladipo Afolayan to head wide from eight-yards out.\nHowever, Wolves scored the first goal of the new campaign when Fer Lopez played a defence-splitting through ball into the path of Munetsi, who coolly found the net with a first time effort from the edge of the penalty area.\nBlackburn drew level through an unlikely source just nine minutes later as McLoughlin headed in his first-ever goal in English football in more than 150 games when he met Ryoya Morishita’s cross from the right at the near post.\nThe home side had chances to retake the lead before the break as Lopez curled a shot narrowly over the top from 20-yards while Kieran Trippier teed up Mateus Mane for a chance as the teenager found the top of the net from distance.\nBlackburn turned the game on its head minutes after the break as Gudjohnsen turned home Afolayan’s deflected ball in from the right from close range to stun Molineux into silence.\nPeixoto introduced Jimenez from the bench on the hour mark but even the Mexican could not lift Wolves with Blackburn comfortable for long periods as frustration grew in the stands.\nHowever, Alebiosu handled Jackson Tchatchoua’s cross from the left in injury time and the referee awarded a spot kick which allowed Jimenez to mark his Wolves return with an equaliser from 12-yards as he sent Balazs Toth the wrong way.\nMatch details\nWolverhampton: Bentley, Trippier, Mosquera, Toti Gomes, Wolfe, Lopez, Andre Trindade, Munetsi, Rodrigo Gomes, Armstrong, Mane.\nSubs used: Jiménez 60 (Mateus Mané), Doyle 67 (Rodrigo Gomes), Tchatchoua 67 (Trippier), Pedro Lima 79 (André),\nChirewa 79 (Møller Wolfe)\nScorers: M. Munetsi 18, R. Jiménez 90+3pen\nYellow card: Mateus Mané 52\nBlackburn: Toth, Alebiosu, Atcheson, McLoughlin, Pickering, Montgomery, Forshaw, Morishita, Cantwell, Afolayan, Gudjohnsen.\nSubs used: Sam Morsy 55 (Forshaw), Fevrier 68 (Afolayan), Ohashi 68 (Cantwell), Garrett 79 (Gudjohnsen), Yuri Ribeiro\n79 (Morishita)\nScorers: S. McLoughlin 27, A. Gudjohnsen 48\nYellow card: Pickering 34, Cantwell 35, Forshaw 45\nAttendance: 30,984\nReferee: Farai Hallam\nPenalty or no penalty?\nJobi McAnuff:\n“I think it is a really harsh one. He [the referee] has to be sure at such a critical time of the game. And I do not think he can be sure. I think he has almost had a guess that he is in the box. It is such a big call.”\nCurtis Davies:\n“We see the ref’s position. The fact the ref makes the decision to give the penalty in my opinion, it is a bigger decision to give it then to not. We have got the luxury of the replays and we are still not sure. His whole body is outside the box.”\nBlackburn boss Tony Mowbray\n“It looks outside the box to me [the penalty incident]. The still picture suggests to me it was outside the box. We can get better and we will have to. We have to be more dominant with the ball at times and be better in attack. We have done a lot of work on that in pre-season. Having been in this league a long time, the power of the dressing room is crucial and any motivation tool you can you use is important. I think we will be fine. You have to enjoy football and love it. I think the boys enjoyed it tonight.”\nMan of the match Ryan Alebiosu, who conceded the late penalty\n“Obviously frustrated. The boys did everything to get the three points but it is gutting in the end. I thought I was outside the box when the ball came in and the decision went against us. They are saying it is in the box and it is frustrating. We dug deep and thought we had it. I cannot fault everyone as we all put in a shift, it was just the final details with the penalty decision. We deserved the three points. If we play like that every week, we will go far in the table.”\nCurtis Davies on Sky Sports\n“Before the game we were predicting Wolves would be right up there [this season] and we thought it would be a comfortable win. But that was not the case. Blackburn gave a really good account of themselves and had lots of openings. [Cesar] Peixoto will be breathing a massive sigh of relief with that handball.”\nJobi McAnuff on Sky Sports\n“Wolves got away with one if I am being really honest. In terms of their second-half performance, they did not do anywhere near enough. I thought Blackburn were excellent throughout and deservedly took the lead. It is a huge decision [to give the penalty], but in the end Raul Jimenez steps up to get his side the point. [Cesar Peixoto] will be delighted to get something in the end.”\nOpening round of fixtures in the Championship\nTonight:\nWolves 2 Blackburn 2\nSaturday:\nBolton vs Preston\nBristol City vs Millwall\nCharlton vs Derby\nMiddlesbrough vs Lincoln\nNorwich vs West Brom\nPortsmouth vs QPR\nStoke vs Swansea\nSheffield United vs Birmingham\nSunday:\nWatford vs Southampton\nBurnley vs West Ham\nMonday:\nCardiff vs Wrexham\nFull time\nBentley spills the corner but gets a free-kick. Moments later, the referee blows his whistle for the final time and the game ends level at 2-2. A dramatic opening night of the new Championship season at Molineux. If the Championship continues in this vein all season, it will be one hell of a year.\nGOAL! Jimenez equalises late on for Wolves\nHe sends Toth the wrong way and calmly slots home to bring Wolves level in added time. That penalty decision will certainly create some debate. The Championship is always full of drama and we get some on opening night.\nIt just had to be. Jimenez rescues Wolves from the penalty spot.\nDrama on the opening night of the campaign.\nRelief for Wolves, heartbreak for Blackburn.\nPENALTY WOLVES!\nLate drama at Molineux! The cross is sent in from the left and the ball strikes the arm Alebiosu on the edge of the box. Was it inside or outside the penalty area? It is definitely handball but was it outside the box? It was very, very close but there is no VAR in the Championship so the penalty will stand.\nJimenez will take...\n90 mins: Wolves 1 Blackburn 2\nFour added minutes. Are we on the verge of seeing the first shock of the new Championship season on opening night?\nFour minutes added. Not even a reaction from the home crowd.\nWolves are desperately running out of time and Blackburn still look comfortable.\nHome fans are beginning to filter out slowly.\n86 mins: Wolves 1 Blackburn 2\nFevrier’s shot from outside the Wolves penalty area is blocked and spins behind for a corner in front of the travelling Blackburn fans. The corner comes to nothing.\nWolves struggling to create anything of note even with Jimenez’s introduction.\nCentre-back Mosquera has gone up front but appears to be struggling with an injury. He’s jogged back to his natural position now.\nDesperate long balls being sent forward but Blackburn look comfortable for now. The home fans are quite audibly unhappy with this second-half showing.\nRovers fans still making the noise.\n65 mins: Wolves 1 Blackburn 2\nYou’d have thought a goal had gone in such is the reaction from the home crowd to Jimenez’s introduction. I have to say, I’m surprised Mane has made way but Jimenez will play through the middle with Armstrong moved out to the left.\nBlackburn made a change earlier in the half with Wolverhampton-born Sam Morsy on for his Rovers debut. One manager going for it as the other attempts to shore things up.\n58 mins: Wolves 1 Blackburn 2\nNo messing about from new Wolves boss Cesar Peixoto with his side behind and discontent brewing in the stands, Raul Jimenez is going to be introduced. His name has been sung throughout the game with a hero’s welcome imminent.\nCan he inspire a comeback? Wolves and Peixoto will certainly hope so.\n53 mins: Wolves 1 Blackburn 2\nWow! What a start to the second half. Blackburn have turned it around, Tony Mowbray clearly inspired his troops at half time.\nMolineux is silenced now with Blackburn fans making all the noise. Some home supporters were still taking their seats as Gudjohnsen turned the ball home.\nCan Wolves turn the game back in their favour? It’s all a bit slow now from the hosts followed by the first signs of disgruntlement from the home supporters.\nAnd to top it off, Mane is booked for simulation.\nGOAL! Gudjohnsen puts Blackburn in front\nMolineux is stunned as the underdogs take the lead inside the opening five minutes of the second half. Tony Mowbray’s side have started well since the break and are rewarded. Afolayan is found making a run into the penalty area on the right side of the box and his cross is slotted home by Gudjohnsen. His father Eidur would have been happy with a striker’s finish like that.\nVerdict at the break\nAn intriguing first half as the whistle sounds and both teams head down the tunnel.\nBoth teams scored when the other seemed on top slightly.\nWolves have probably shaded it with those efforts from Lopez and Mane which went narrowly over.\nPlenty to think about for the two managers.\nAll square at the break.\n45 mins: Wolves 1 Blackburn 1\nForshaw is the third Blackburn player into the book tonight as the referee goes back to penalise the midfielder for an earlier foul on Andre. That was not Forshaw’s first foul either.\nWith no VAR or hydration breaks, there will be just one added minute at the end of this first half.\n35 mins: Wolves 1 Blackburn 1\nWell, well, well....\nWhat a response from Blackburn.\nBoth teams off the mark for the season now, a brilliant near-post run and header from McLoughlin who scores his first-ever goal in English football to level the game up.\nMolineux briefly stunned into silence. A slight delay from the fans celebrating the header as well.\nGame on.\n32 mins: Wolves 1 Blackburn 1\nR Gomes delivers a cross from the right side of the Blackburn box across the penalty area but Armstrong does not pre-empt that and is not on hand to meet it. However, Alebiosu, with Mane not far behind him, puts it behind for a corner. Trippier delivers and the Blackburn keeper makes a meal of it, missing his punch out but then scrambles back towards his own goal to gather the loose ball.\nGOAL! McLoughlin equalises for Blackburn\nThe visitors went behind against the run of play but probably deservedly they are back on level terms. Morishita clips in the cross from the right to the near post, where McLoughlin meets it first and guides a powerful header into the far corner. Two goals in the opening half an hour of the new Championship season.\n27 mins: Wolves 1 Blackburn 0\nBlackburn are getting great joy down their right through Alebiosu and Morishita, with the latter winning them a corner as Toti is forced to head behind from a cross. Pickering sends it into a good area and the touch comes off the head of Munetsi, whose header loops into the air and goes just over his own bar for another corner on the other side.\nCantwell’s delivery to the far post is headed away.\n24 mins: Wolves 1 Blackburn 0\nWe have the first goal of the season and it’s the returning Marshall Munetsi who gets it.\nA cool finish from the midfielder after a bit of a drab start from the home side.\nThat will please the new Wolves boss. Such a simple move but the home side ripped Blackburn wide open with two passes.\nWork to do for the visitors now who had probably started the better of the two teams before the opener.\nWolves fans make their feelings about VAR known following the goal. No VAR in the Championship of course but no VAR was needed for that anyway. A perfectly good goal.\n22 mins: Wolves 1 Blackburn 0\nMane cuts inside from the left onto his preferred right foot and curls a dangerous ball into the box that is punched away by Toth in the Blackburn goal. 18-year-old Mane was one of very few bright spots for Wolves last season and in many ways it is a surprise that no Premier League teams have lured him back to the top flight quite yet. That could though still happen before the deadline.\nGOAL! Munetsi puts Wolves ahead\nThe first goal of the Championship season comes against the run of play. It is a clinical, ruthless goal that comes from an incisive pass and clinical finish. The pass is fed through by F Lopez to pick out the run of Munetsi, who is cool, calm and collected as he finishes past Toth. Blackburn had been the better side up until that point but Wolves take the lead at Molineux.\n16 mins: Wolves 0 Blackburn 0\nA shot on target for Blackburn but an easy save in the end. Alebiosu, who has looked good in these early stages, drives down Blackburn’s right before cutting it back to Morishita on the edge of the box. Morishita takes one touch before shooting but his effort is tame and straight at Bentley.\n11 mins: Wolves 0 Blackburn 0\nBlackburn may be heavy underdogs tonight but they have settled down well away at one of the favourites to get promoted. Tony Mowbray may well be seeing this as a good time to play Wolves away.\nAlebiosu shows quick feet down Blackburn’s right and clips a cross to the back post, where Afolayan meets it but his header is just off target.\n9 mins: Wolves 0 Blackburn 0\nSafe to say the doom and gloom of last season appears to have lifted at Molineux. The home fans in fine voice early on here.\nBy contrast, Kieran Trippier’s first touch of the season is greeted by loud boos by the travelling Blackburn supporters.\nIn fact, every Trippier touch is greeted by a chorus of boos. Will they keep that up all game?\nThey’ve certainly not forgotten his Burnley connection.\nNew managers and new signings galore. This should be a fascinating spectacle.\n5 mins: Wolves 0 Blackburn 0\nBlackburn boss Tony Mowbray did say ahead of the game that Wolves have not been used to winning games over the last 12 months so can the visitors take advantage of that tonight? Blackburn are underdogs tonight and not too much is expected of them this season so surely they have nothing to lose this evening?\nReminder of the teams\nWolves: Bentley; Trippier, Mosquera, T.Gomes; R.Gomes, André, Munetsi, Möller Wolfe; Lopez, Mané; Armstrong.\nSubstitutes: Sá, Bueno, Jiménez, Lima, Doyle, Chirewa, Djiga, Tchatchoua, Edozie.\nBlackburn: Toth; Alebiosu, Atcheson, McLoughlin, Pickering; Forshaw, Montgomery; Afolayan, Cantwell, Morishita; Gudjohnsen.\nSubstitutes: Pears, Ribeiro, Tavares, Morsy, Fevrier, Carter, Henriksson, Ohashi, Garrett.\nGrim numbers\nThese do not look good if you are a Blackburn fan; these are their attacking statistics and rankings in the Championship last season:\nGoals: 42, 23rd\nxG performance: -13.9, 24th\nShots on target: 146, 23rd\nShot conversion: 7.9%, 22nd\nChances created: 368, 21st\nNear the bottom of the league in every single one of those pretty much.\nThe Wolves promotion plan after toxic season\nWhen Wolverhampton Wanderers last attacked the Championship under a Portuguese manager new to English football, it proved a vintage season.\nMolineux was under the spell of Nuno Espírito Santo in that 2017-18 campaign, and the big question is whether César Peixoto can lead a repeat nearly a decade later.\nPeixoto was a surprise appointment in June, replacing Rob Edwards, and he has been entrusted with the mission of returning Wolves to the Premier League at the first time of asking.\nCesar Peixoto on adapting his style to English football\n“I have changed some things with set-pieces and some things in other moments of the game because I want the team to be organised and understand all the different moments within a match. During pre-season we have scored some goals from high pressing, but we will not be able to press high in every moment of every game. The players have to understand the game and recognise what each moment requires. It is important that we work as a team. When we can press high, we’ll press high. When we need to defend in a mid-block, we will stay organised. When the opposition pushes us deeper, which will sometimes happen, we have to be organised there as well.\n“There are a lot of crosses in the Championship, for example, so the team needs to understand what to do in those situations. We want to play our football, be offensive and react strongly when we lose the ball, but we also need to understand the different moments of the game. I think we are at a good level to attack the Championship.”\nChampionship predictions\nOur experts have been having their say ahead of the new Championship season. A number of them believe Wolves will be promoted, including John Percy:\nWest Ham, Wolves, Middlesbrough\nIt is difficult to look beyond West Ham. Nuno Espírito Santo romped to the Championship title with Wolves in 2018 and remains a shrewd operator (along with his No 2 Rui Pedro Silva), while convincing Jarrod Bowen to stay is significant. I am going to go for Wolves and Middlesbrough as the other two promoted clubs.\nOpening round of fixtures in the Championship\nTonight:\nWolves vs Blackburn\nSaturday:\nBolton vs Preston\nBristol City vs Millwall\nCharlton vs Derby\nMiddlesbrough vs Lincoln\nNorwich vs West Brom\nPortsmouth vs QPR\nStoke vs Swansea\nSheffield United vs Birmingham\nSunday:\nWatford vs Southampton\nBurnley vs West Ham\nMonday:\nCardiff vs Wrexham\nWill Wolves bounce straight back?\nThe 2026/27 Championship season kicks off at Molineux as Wolves host Blackburn, with the home side hoping to put the bad memories of last season firmly behind them. There are new playoff rules in place for this season, with six places now up for grabs in the play-offs.\nWolves are aiming to return to the Premier League at the first attempt, having endured a miserable season in the top flight last year as they finished bottom with just three wins to their name. It has been a summer of change at the club as they chose to change managers. Rob Edwards lost his job in what was a shock move and was replaced by 46-year-old Cesar Peixoto, with this being his first head coaching position outside of his home country Portugal. Back in 2017/18, Wolves won the Championship title led by Peixoto’s compatriot Nuno Espirito Santo.\n“I am ready,” Peixoto told Wolves’ club website. “We have worked very hard during pre-season, and I think we have had a great pre-season. All the players have worked very, very hard and they understand what I want from them. I am ready and confident going into the first game. Football is football, whether it is in Portugal or England, but there are different characteristics to the game here. We have watched, observed and analysed the Championship. We did that work before I came to the club, so we know what to expect.\n“It is going to be a tough season with good teams, but we are prepared to fight for the three points game after game and week after week. We know what we want from the team and we know what our big goal is. We’re going to work very hard and fight to achieve that goal.”\nThey have brought in vast experience on the player side in the shape of Kieran Trippier and Raul Jimenez, who is back at Molineux three years after leaving for Fulham. Although they have lost Brazilian midfielder Joao Gomes to Aston Villa, most of their squad has stayed intact as they pursue promotion back to the top flight. It is a special year for the club as they celebrate their 150th anniversary.\nBlackburn, led by experienced manager Tony Mowbray, did not enjoy a great season in the Championship last year, finishing down in 20th place and just five points above the relegation zone. 14 years have now passed since Blackburn last played in the top flight.\n“I think it is a tough start,” Mowbray said. “On paper, I would suggest the first couple of games, particularly, jumped out as a tough start. One of the Premier League sides from last season, away from home. They have got a very talented squad, a pretty expensive squad. They have got some very good individual talent. Yet, there is an argument to say they have not won many football matches in the last calendar year. So, what we get, we will have to wait and see.”\nThese sides last met in the FA Cup during the 2024/25 season, with Wolves winning that fourth-round tie 2-0 thanks to goals from Matheus Cunha and Gomes, both of whom are now plying their trade elsewhere. The omens would appear to be on Wolves’ side as they are unbeaten in their last eight league meetings with Blackburn. Both sides progressed to the second round of the League Cup last weekend, with Wolves beating Port Vale 3-0 and Blackburn overcoming Burton Albion 2-1.\nTeam news to follow shortly.",
