    {
        "title": "Rabies deaths are rising. What is going wrong?",
        "link": "https://www.telegraph.co.uk/global-health/science-and-disease/rabies-deaths-are-rising-what-is-going-wrong/",
        "source": "www.telegraph.co.uk for the latest news from the UK and around the world.",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-18T19:44:08.470407+00:00",
        "image": "https://www.telegraph.co.uk/content/dam/global-health/2026/08/14/TELEMMGLPICT000495100677_17867296456270_trans_NvBQzQNjv4BqqVzuuqpFlyLIwiB6NTmJwSX5rhseiWKOo9p9OQ-ymek.jpeg?impolicy=OG-Standard",
        "full_content": "Sheuli Akter with her three-year-old son Abdul, who contracted rabies when he was bitten on the face by a dog near his house\nCredit: Simon Townsley/The Telegraph\nIn a packed intensive care ward in Dhaka, three-year-old Abdul lay paralysed, his tiny body contorted by a rabies infection that had reached his brain.\nHis mother, Sheuli Akter, 38, sat at his side massaging his legs in the hope that this might loosen his muscles, but it was having no effect. Even his hands and feet were seized up by cramps.\nAbdul had been in the hospital for a month but doctors said he was unlikely to live much longer. The infection, they said, had progressed to degenerative encephalitis; the virus had got to his brain.\nAbdul was shaking, gripped by fever. His breathing was rapid and panicked, punctuated occasionally by groans of pain.\nOn his chin was the telltale scar of a dog bite, the source of the infection.\n“He was playing outside with the other children,” said Sheuli. “The dog, which was one of those always waiting around outside, suddenly attacked him. They said it went for him because he was the smallest.”\nAbdul’s rabies infection had progressed to fatal degenerative encephalitis\nCredit: Simon Townsley/The Telegraph\nRabies is a constant threat in the crowded and unsanitary slums of Bangladesh’s capital, and the Infectious Diseases Hospital has experienced a surge in deaths from the disease over the last three years.\nAfter decades of progress, stories like Abdul’s are becoming more common again as the number of rabies cases and deaths rises in hotspots like Bangladesh, India, and in parts of Africa.\nIn almost all cases, the rising cases coincide with large populations of stray dogs. In some affected countries like Morocco and Sri Lanka, these and other stray animals have even become tourist attractions.\nSo what is to be done, and why is rabies on the rise?\nA preventable death sentence\nRabies kills over 60,000 people every year, almost one person every nine minutes, according to the World Health Organization (WHO). This number, the global health body concedes, is probably a gross underestimate.\nThe burden falls heaviest on rural poor populations in Asia and Africa, and about half of all cases are in children under 15.\nYet it is preventable, and can be brought under control or eliminated entirely with the right strategy.\nSome 99 per cent of human cases are acquired from the bite of an infected dog, and global health metrics show that countries with high numbers of free-roaming, unvaccinated strays suffer the overwhelming majority of deaths.\nIndia, for example, is believed to be home to some 60 million street dogs and accounts for more than a third of all global human rabies deaths.\nIndia is believed to be home to some 60 million street dogs and accounts for more than a third of all global human rabies deaths\nCredit: SAJJAD HUSSAIN/AFP via Getty Images\nBy contrast, countries that have enforced mandatory vaccination orders and other public health measures, and taken steps to control their stray dog populations, have effectively eliminated the disease.\nTake the United States, for example.\nOne of the most famous scenes in all of 20th century literature was the fictional lawyer Atticus Finch shooting dead a rabid dog that had wandered into town.\nThe scene, from ‘To Kill a Mockingbird’, is a key moment in the hero’s character arc but it is also a reflection of the much greater threat rabies once posed to Americans.\nIn the 1930s, when the book is set, the rabies virus was still endemic in American dog populations, leading to thousands of cases in domestic animals and dozens in humans every year.\nBut by the time the book was published in 1960, rabies was largely under control, and by the end of that decade less than 500 cases of rabies in dogs and only three in humans were reported, according to data from the US Centres for Disease Control and Prevention (CDC).\nAcademics attribute this sharp fall in rabies cases largely to post-Second World War public health drives.\nIn the 40s and 50s many US states began introducing compulsory rabies vaccination for dogs. At the same time, many local authorities also began introducing rules requiring dogs to be kept on leads.\nTargeted culls and the country’s extensive network of dog shelters also played a key role in controlling the stray dog population.\nWhile culling is not officially part of the US strategy for controlling rabies today – and has become an extremely sensitive issue around the world – a huge number of stray dogs are quietly euthanised across the country to keep the dog population under control.\nThe Best Friends Animal Society, a US animal welfare organisation, obtained data showing that some 207,400 dogs were euthanised in shelters in 2023 – roughly one every two-and-a half minutes.\nThe US successfully eliminated canine rabies in 2008 (the virus can still be found in wild mammals, however, including cats, bats and raccoons) and now spends up to $510 million a year on ongoing prevention efforts.\nPeople queue for post exposure rabies treatment at the Infectious Diseases Hospital in the Bangladeshi capital Dhaka\nCredit: Simon Townsley/The Telegraph\nSo what has stopped other countries from repeating this success?\n“It’s all about mass dog vaccination,” said Felix Lankester, Director of Rabies Free Africa and a Professor at Washington State University.\n“Those countries that have put in place mass vaccination – coordinated, systematic mass dog vaccination – have been successful, and the countries that haven’t shown any progress are, in my experience, those with no coordinated programme,” he told The Telegraph.\n“If you look across much of Africa, most countries are really doing very little. And similarly in Asia, it has to be a coordinated nationwide strategy to vaccinate dogs. Otherwise, there’ll be pockets of endemicity remaining, which will then cause problems.”\nThe reason a simple cull of strays is not enough is that the virus bounces back when the stray population recovers – unless they have been vaccinated.\nThe pandemic may also have played in the current rebound as it disrupted vaccination campaigns.\n“There are a lot of questions about whether the pandemic impacted vaccination,” said Prof Lankester. “Certainly, around the time of the pandemic, vaccination where we work in East Africa was impacted because we couldn’t get our teams out.”\nCuts to global health funding have also hurt budgets for countries with big rabies problems, he added.\nEven a brief disruption to vaccination programmes can be calamitous, as the disease can rapidly re-establish itself in a canine population.\nRabies has re-emerged as a serious public health concern in Ukraine\nCredit: Sean Gallup/Getty Images\n“In a lot of these countries, the average life expectancy of dogs is very low, and so the turnover of the dog population is quick,” Prof Lankester explained.\n“So, if you vaccinate in year one and you hit your targets, but then you don’t vaccinate so much in year two and year three, very quickly you’ll lose herd immunity because unvaccinated puppies will be born and vaccinated dogs will die,” he said.\n“You can go from 70 per cent coverage to below 40 per cent coverage within a year and a half, and once it’s below 40 per cent then rabies transmission can be sustained, and it will come back.”\nAs a case in point, rabies has re-emerged as a serious public health concern in Ukraine in recent years as a result of disruption to vaccination programmes and a surge in displaced and abandoned pets.\nThe number of people bitten by rabid animals with rabies increased from 780 in 2021 to 2,507 in 2024, according to data from the Ukrainian Ministry of Health.\nWhat about culling? Are we neglecting an important tool that has helped bring rabies under control out of concern for animal rights?\nIt’s now quite hard to imagine the hero of a book written today coldly putting down a rabid dog, and you do not need to look far for evidence of how unpopular the idea of culling dogs, or even taking steps to remove them from cities, is.\nMass protests erupted in India last year after the country’s top court, acting on its own authority against what it called the public health “menace” of rabies, ordered the authorities to begin rounding up the city’s population of stray dogs and putting them into shelters.\nYet the order was welcomed by the family of Chhavi Sharma, a six-year-old girl who died from a rabies infection after being bitten by a stray dog in the Indian capital.\n“I hope there are no more Chhavis, who are hurt elsewhere. Nobody should go through the cruel fate we underwent,” her aunt told local television.\nThe Ukraine war has disrupted rabies vaccination programmes and triggered a surge in displaced and abandoned pets\nCredit: Kateryna Klochko/AP\nBroadly, though, experts say mass culls are not the answer, and several studies have shown them to be ineffective at controlling rabies.\nRemoving a population of dogs may clear the streets temporarily, but it also creates a vacuum effect, drawing in unvaccinated – and therefore potentially rabid dogs – from the surrounding area that are less well-adjusted to humans.\nProf Lankester said that, while targeted culls may still be useful for controlling large populations of community dogs in places like Indian cities, the effectiveness of culling as a tool for controlling rabies is doubtful.\n“I don’t think we’re being too emotional about it,” he said. “I’m a pragmatist, and if it was the way to go forward, I think I would be supportive of it, but there’s no evidence of it working, and it’s expensive, and it’s deeply unpopular.”\nThe WHO wants to eliminate dog-mediated human rabies deaths by 2030 and its strategy for this is built around three main pillars:\nVaccinating stray dogs (it wants to achieve vaccination rates of at least 70 per cent in endemic areas)\nExpanding access to vaccines and life-saving post-exposure jabs for humans\nImproving community awareness about the disease and promoting responsible pet ownership\nThe problem appears to be that implementing this strategy is easier said than done in many of the places where rabies is still endemic.\nOne reason is cost. In an era of rising costs and shrinking budgets, many people and governments in the developing world simply don’t see rabies as a priority.\n“I think we’re quite a long way from being able to implement the sort of privately-funded mass vaccination of dogs that we have in America and Europe,” said Prof Lankester.\nA stray dog is captured in Cairo as part of the Free Rabies Egypt inoculation campaign\nCredit: Nariman El-Mofty/AP\nAnother problem is that, while many countries have laws around dog ownership already in place, they are rarely enforced.\n“In all the countries that I’ve worked in, there are very good existing laws about dog ownership – if you’re going to own a dog, you’ve got to vaccinate it for rabies – but these laws aren’t implemented,” said Prof Lankester.\nHuman rabies deaths are trending in the wrong direction in many of the countries with the highest burden of disease, including India, Bangladesh, the Philippines, Malaysia, South Sudan and the Democratic Republic of Congo, according to WHO data.\nRabies also remains endemic in many areas frequented by Western tourists, and the shocking death last year of Yvonne Ford, a British grandmother, four months after she was scratched by a puppy while on holiday in Morocco.\n“Watching Yvonne deteriorate so rapidly was extremely traumatic,” her daughter Robyn Thomson told an inquest into her death in Sheffield.\nThe death prompted the UK Health Security Agency to remind travellers about the risks associated with stray animals.\nSome countries have got it right, however.\nIn Latin America, human and canine rabies cases have been slashed by over 99 per cent since 1983 thanks to a major campaign overseen by the Pan American Health Organization (PAHO).\nUnder the plan, countries got together to purchase vaccines at affordable prices and carry out massive, synchronised annual campaigns to vaccinate tens of millions of dogs at the same time.\nSimilarly, regional bulk purchasing helped improve access to post-exposure prophylaxis, leading to a dramatic fall in deaths.\nMore recently, Turkey has made significant progress against rabies and is charting its own path.\nOn the one hand, it is using pioneering – but expensive – oral rabies vaccines, dropping millions of vaccine-laden “baits” from the sky across vast areas to control rabies in wildlife as well as hard to reach stray dogs.\nOn the other, the government has also pressed ahead with an order requiring local authorities to round up millions of strays into shelters and euthanise them if they are sick or dangerous.\nYoung children bitten by stray dogs are the most frequent casualties of rabies in Bangladesh\nCredit: Simon Townsley/The Telegraph\nAnimal rights activists have called this order a “massacre law,” fearing that it will lead to mass culls because of a lack of space in shelters.\nThe WHO has estimated that fully eliminating dog-mediated rabies across all the countries where it is still endemic would cost roughly $6.3 billion – a significant sum but not beyond the reach of most developed countries or even many billionaires.\nEliminating the disease “wouldn’t be hugely expensive, given the amount of money that’s swishing around,” said Prof Lankester.\nOral rabies vaccines like those used by Turkey remain prohibitively expensive for many countries but could have a major impact if they become more readily available, he added.\nYet compared with other major global health targets like malaria, HIV or polio, rabies elimination work struggles to attract funding.\nBut solving the issue of rabies may also require a shift in the way the disease is thought about on a more fundamental level.\nThis is because we tend to think of vaccinating dogs against rabies as being good for the dog, rather than being beneficial for human health.\n“When people think about rabies and vaccinating dogs, they always think about it as something that’s good for the dog – which it is – but they kind of miss the point that the reason you’re vaccinating the dog is to protect humans,” said Prof Lankester.\nRabies, he said, has become the victim of a “responsibility gap” and has largely been left to the veterinary or agricultural sectors to handle, rather than treated as a public health problem.\n“It kind of rests on the veterinary sector to solve this problem, but the benefits are for the public health sector, and therein lies the rub,” he said. “In some countries, the veterinary sector is often less interested in vaccinating against rabies because they’ve got many other diseases to fund – livestock diseases that affect productivity.”\nIn the end, it may be that tourism changes this dynamic. Ford, the British grandmother who contracted rabies from a puppy in Morocco, is not the only high profile case of a tourist contracting the disease.\nIn 2019, Brigitte Kallestad became the first Norwegian to die of rabies in over 200 years when she contracted the disease from a puppy in the Philippines, prompting major safety campaigns across Scandinavia.\nFor countries that want to build and sustain valuable tourist industries, controlling not just their populations of pets, stray dogs and cats, but also their wildlife, will become increasingly important.",
