        "title": "Unemployment rate fails to fall for Burnham",
        "link": "https://www.telegraph.co.uk/business/2026/08/18/unemployment-uk-economy-jobs-burnham/",
        "source": "www.telegraph.co.uk for the latest news from the UK and around the world.",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-18T19:44:08.470388+00:00",
        "image": "https://www.telegraph.co.uk/content/dam/business/2026/08/18/TELEMMGLPICT000498676383_17870387263470_trans_NvBQzQNjv4Bq-IWLY18X4-CzgyIcjLEAj0k9u7HhRJvuo-ZLenGRumA.jpeg?impolicy=OG-Live",
        "full_content": "Unemployment remained stubbornly high at 4.9pc\nCredit: James Manning-WPA Pool/Getty Images\nAn expected drop in the unemployment rate has failed to materialise, dealing a setback to Andy Burnham’s hopes of reviving Britain’s ailing economy.\nUnemployment remained stuck at 4.9pc in the three months to June, while the number of payrolled employees fell by 13,000, according to figures from the Office for National Statistics. Economists had predicted that the unemployment rate would ease to 4.8pc.\nWhile the three-month figure was flat, monthly data showed a sharp increase at the end of the period. Unemployment jumped to 5.4pc in June, up from 4.6pc the previous month.\nThat marked the highest reading since October 2020 and the biggest monthly jump in 13 years.\nJulian Jessop, at the Institute of Economic Affairs, described the latest jobs figures as “dire”.\nHe said: “The unemployment rate remains stubbornly high, with a big jump in the single month figure for June.”\nIt came as private sector wage growth slumped to a six-year low of just 2.8pc. That stands in contrast to a 6.1pc pay increase for the average public sector worker in the same period.\nMeanwhile, vacancies dropped to 707,000 in the three months to July – the lowest level since 2021 – with a particular strain on entry-level job openings.\nBen Harrison, director of the Work Foundation at Lancaster University, warned that the UK was “trapped in a deepening jobs drought”.\nThomas Pugh, chief economist at RSM UK, said: “Stagnant, or even negative, real wage growth in the second half of this year will intensify the pressure on Burnham and Healey to come up with a package to support the cost of living.\n“However, doing anything substantial without breaching either the fiscal rules or the manifesto commitments looks increasingly difficult given the deterioration in headroom and commitments to spend big on everything from defence to social care.”\nNepotism more helpful than talent, Britons believe\nLink copied to clipboard\nSuccess depends more on nepotism than talent, according to research conducted for a landmark government review on youth worklessness.\nAnalysis for Alan Milburn, the former health secretary, showed that just 5pc of UK adults believe a person’s achievements are based on talent.\nBy contrast, the poll of more than 2,000 Britons showed 22pc said it was based on who someone knows, while 27pc said it depended on hard work.\nThe analysis also revealed that more than half of Gen Z believe they have fewer opportunities than Baby Boomers did when they were growing up.\nThe research also showed Britons young and old believe the government should support more apprenticeships to help people get on the career ladder.\nCommenting on the findings, Alan Milburn insisted the current system had to change.\nHe said: “More and more people believe hard work is not rewarded. That undermines the promise that has been at the heart of our country’s social contract – that if you put in effort, you will be able to get ahead, and that each generation should do better than the last.\n“That promise is being broken. What I hear from young people themselves time and again confirms it. There’re too few apprenticeships, too few entry-level jobs, and too few chances to get on the first rung of the career ladder.\n“It is not a shortage of effort that is holding back Britain’s young people. It is a shortage of support and opportunity.”\nWhile the jobs market is in a downward spiral, separate figures from the ONS suggest that productivity has improved.\nInitial data shows output per hour and output per worker were 0.7pc and 1.4pc higher respectively in the second quarter compared with the same time last year.\nPreliminary estimates produced using administrative data-based methods, incorporating Pay As You Earn (PAYE) RTI data and LFS data, suggest output per hour was 0.7% higher in Quarter 2 (Apr to June) 2026 than the same time last year.\nVery important to understand the continuing slowdown in private sector pay growth - a much better leading indicator of where inflationary pressure from the labour market is heading - basically nowhere.\nVery reassuring for the Bank of England as they grapple with energy shocks. https://t.co/lS7d64DQfJ\nUnemployment rate unchanged at 4.9% with a big bounce in the single month reading to its highest since October 2020 at 5.4%. The biggest month-to-month variance for thirteen years! Good luck picking the trend out of such a noisy series. Positive news for govt is the rate is… https://t.co/cpsG2wjr0kpic.twitter.com/BGTnx5HY1n\nThe number of workers on zero-hours contracts stood at 1.2m in the three months to June, up slightly from 1.18m in the same period a year earlier.\nHowever, it marks a decline from the previous quarter when 1.25m staff were on zero-hours contracts, according to the ONS.\nIt comes as Labour pledged to ban “exploitative” zero-hours contracts in its manifesto.\nHowever, officials admitted last week that the plan to ban zero-hours contracts will reduce flexibility for companies and drive up administrative costs.\nBusiness chiefs have warned ministers that the move to replace zero-hour contracts with guaranteed hours risks locking young people out of work and increasing costs.\nPublic sector pay growth ‘risks wage-price spiral’\nLink copied to clipboard\nSurging public sector pay could drive up inflation even as wage growth in the private sector remains subdued, an economist has warned.\nProfessor Costas Milas at the University of Liverpool said any relief for Bank of England policymakers about slowing private-sector pay could prove short-lived.\nHe said: “The problem is that public sector wage growth continues to outpace strongly wage developments in the private sector. Above-inflation public pay increases risk triggering a wage-price spiral therefore adding to inflation pressures.\n“The BoE should take notice not least because its models (and as far as I know) do not differentiate between public and private wages.”\nStagnant wages put Burnham under pressure, warn economists\nLink copied to clipboard\nAndy Burnham is likely to come under pressure as Britons grapple with stagnant real wages and the rising cost of living, economists have warned.\nThe Resolution Foundation, a Left-leaning think tank, said that the Prime Minister has “inherited a mixed bag labour market” as unemployment and economic inactivity stagnates\nSenior economist Louise Murphy said: “Britain’s early summer of decent economic growth hasn’t translated into more jobs and stronger pay. Unemployment continues to hover around five per cent, while wages in the private sector are barely keeping pace with price rises.”\nMs Murphy added that the UK would need a “rosier outlook for inflation” if Britons were to avoid experiencing a widespread decline in real wages by the end of the year.\nIt comes as households and businesses experience increasing price pressures from the Iran war.\nThe Bank of England has estimated that inflation will rise to 3.2pc by the end of the year as rising energy prices filter through the economy, pushing up household bills and food prices.\nThomas Pugh, chief economist at RSM UK, said: “Stagnant, or even negative, real wage growth in the second half of this year will intensify the pressure on Burnham and Healey to come up with a package to support the cost of living.\n“However, doing anything substantial without breaching either the fiscal rules or the manifesto commitments looks increasingly difficult given the deterioration in headroom and commitments to spend big on everything from defence to social care.”\nThe splintering in pay growth between Britain’s public and private sector jobs markets has become “unsustainable”, an economist has warned.\nJulian Jessop at the Institute of Economic Affairs (IEA) warned the slowdown in private sector pay growth means wages would not keep pace with inflation in the second half of the year.\n“In contrast, pay growth in the public sector is running above 6pc,” he said. “This divergence is unsustainable.”\nMr Jessop said the “dire” employment figures reflected the strain of the rising cost of employment.\nHe added: “Businesses have been hit by higher employer National Insurance, a higher minimum wage and an expanding regulatory burden. It should be no surprise that employers are responding by hiring fewer people, cutting vacancies and holding down wages.\n“The Government cannot tax and regulate its way to a stronger labour market. Ministers should focus on reducing the cost of hiring, removing barriers to work and giving businesses the confidence to invest and create jobs.”\nThe continued decline in vacancies over the last 12 months has left jobseekers with fewer opportunities as the nation’s labour market continues to cool.\nBen Harrison, director of the Work Foundation at Lancaster University, said: “The UK remains trapped in a deepening jobs drought. Vacancies have fallen to the lowest level outside of the Covid-19 pandemic since 2014.”\nThe labour market has been particularly challenging for school leavers and university graduates as the number of entry level job openings has been in decline.\nMr Harrison added: “Over the last year, the decline in starter jobs has been 1.6 times faster than for other vacancies, making it increasingly difficult for young people to get their first foothold in work.”\nThe UK jobs market is being hamstrung by a costs crisis facing businesses, the British Chambers of Commerce (BCC) has warned.\nPatrick Milnes at the BCC said: “Business confidence is at a post-pandemic low and the squeeze on firms’ finances shows no signs of easing.\n“With last week’s announcement that changes to zero hours contracts could cost businesses almost £3bn, many will be reassessing their recruitment plans.\n“While Andy Burnham has spoken about a new deal on education and changes to apprenticeships, if businesses cannot afford to take people on then progress will be slow.”\nThe UK is stuck with an “uneven” labour market, an economist has warned, as the gap between the public and private sector widens.\nJake Finney, senior economist at PwC UK, said: “Beneath the headline figures is a more uneven picture. Consumer-facing sectors such as retail and hospitality continue to see employment fall, offset largely by rising public sector employment.”\nMany businesses have warned that rising taxes and successive inflation-busting increases to the minimum wage have made it more costly to hire new employees\nThis has caused companies to cut back on staff levels or freeze recruitment altogether.\nMr Finney added: “The risk is that younger and entry-level workers in these sectors continue to bear the brunt of the weakness, while firms in more constrained parts of the economy still struggle to find people with the skills they need.”\nInterest rates likely to remain on hold, say economists\nLink copied to clipboard\nThe latest unemployment and pay growth figures are likely to encourage the Bank of England to keep interest rates on hold at 3.75pc at the next meeting in September, economists have said.\nYael Selfin, chief economist at KPMG, said: “A stabilising labour market and contained wage pressures support a cautious approach from the Bank of England.”\nShe added that there is little sign that wage growth will lead to inflationary pressures, as private sector pay growth fell to its lowest level in six years.\nSuren Thiru, chief economist at the ICAEW, said: “These figures reduce the likelihood of a September interest rate rise, raising hopes that labour market conditions will help rather than hinder the fight against inflation by restraining pay growth and dampening demand across the economy.”\nAlex Hall-Chen at the Institute of Directors has urged the Government to take action to reverse Britain’s ailing labour market.\nToday’s data reflects a stagnant labour market, with job vacancies down by 6,000 in the latest quarter and the number of payrolled employees down 13,000 on the month.\nIf the Government is to deliver on its promise of growth and prosperity in every postcode, it must take concrete steps to restore employer confidence and support job creation.\nThis will require addressing the mounting costs and risks of employment that have resulted from the implementation of the Employment Rights Act, the hike in employer’s National Insurance contributions, and successive above-inflation increases to the minimum wage.\nYouth unemployment remains near an 11-year high as under-25s struggle to find work.\nThe unemployment rate for 16 to 25-year-olds fell to 16.2pc in the three months to June, down from 16.4pc a month earlier.\nThe figure is firmly above the Covid lockdown peak of 15.3pc seen in the summer of 2020, according to the ONS.\nBosses have blamed Labour’s decisions to put up the minimum wage and National Insurance contributions for forcing them to turn away from hiring young people.\nJob postings fell as companies continue with their freeze on hiring new staff.\nFigures from the ONS showed that vacancies dropped to 707,000 in the three months to July, the lowest level since 2021.\nThe continued decline in vacancies comes as many businesses warned that successive above inflation increases in the minimum wage have caused them to hold off on\nThe stagnant labour market and decline in vacancies come as Britons grapple with increasing price pressures from the Iran war.\nInflation is forecast by the Bank of England to climb to 3.2pc in the final three months of the year, as the Middle East conflict pushes up prices of oil, gas and fertilisers used for food.\nThe unemployment rate unexpectedly remained stuck at 4.9pc in the three months to June as businesses held back on hiring.\nThe Office for National Statistics reported the unemployment rate remained unchanged, while the number of payrolled employees fell by 13,000 between May and June.\nEconomists had expected unemployment to fall to 4.8pc, with the figures a challenge to Andy Burnham in his first month as Prime Minister.\nFirms have held off on recruiting new staff or making redundancies as uncertainty from the Iran war and domestic politics cause companies to pause decision making.",
        "content": "Unemployment remained stubbornly high at 4.9pc\nCredit: James Manning-WPA Pool/Getty Images\nAn expected drop in the unemployment rate has failed to materialise, dealing a setback to Andy Burnham’s hopes of reviving Britain’s ailing economy.\nUnemployment remained stuck at 4.9pc in the three months to June, while the number of payrolled employees fell by 13,000, according to figures from the Office f...",
