        "title": "Fact check: How do I spot AI-generated images?",
        "link": "https://www.dw.com/en/fact-check-how-do-i-spot-ai-generated-images/a-65252602?maca=en-rss-en-all-1573-xml-mrss",
        "source": "Deutsche Welle",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-18T19:44:08.470111+00:00",
        "image": "https://static.dw.com/image/75179723_6.jpg",
        "full_content": "Fact check: How do I spot AI-generated images?\nAugust 18, 2026\nAnyone with an internet connection and access to an artificial intelligence (AI) tool can create photorealistic images within seconds, for free or for very little money, and then spread them on social networks at breakneck speed.\nThe tools used to create AI-generated images are improving rapidly, reducing the number of obvious AI errors – often referred to as AI hallucinations – and making it increasingly difficult for users to determine whether a viral image depicts a real event.\nGone are the days when AI tools routinely produced images of public figures with four fingers, an excessive amount of teeth or unnatural body proportions.\nAn additional challenge is that while many images are entirely AI-generated, others are real photos that have been partially altered using AI.\nHere's one example: an authentic broadcast image from the 2026 World Cup in which a fan was replaced by an Adolf Hitler lookalike.\nOf course, the image was actually created using an OpenAI-powered tool.\nSo how can you spot AI-generated images? DW Fact check offers tips to help you navigate today's information landscape.\nLook closely at the picture\nYou may remember the story of a Syrian teenager who helped stop a knife attack in the German city of Hamburg in 2025.\nAs the story spread, conflicting accounts of the incident emerged on social media, along with AI-generated images like the one above.\nThe image looks authentic at first glance, but if you zoom in and examine it carefully, you'll find obvious inconsistencies.\nThat's why it's important to take your time and inspect the details. Common AI errors might be hidden in the background, including distorted objects and gibberish-filled text.\nIf your device doesn't allow you to zoom in far enough, online tools can help.\nA tool we often use at DW Fact check is Forensically, a free online photo forensics platform that includes a magnifier and other features for examining images closely.\nThink about the context\nRemember the Hitler lookalike from above? A supporter resembling the Nazi dictator would almost certainly have attracted immediate attention from broadcasters, stadium officials and other spectators.\nYet no reputable media organizations reported such a stir, and no fans in the stadium shared posts showing the lookalike from a different perspective.\nThis example highlights a key verification technique: move beyond the pixels and ask yourself whether the event itself makes sense.\nOne way of doing that is checking whether the apparent event has been covered by trustworthy news organizations.\nIf an image appears to show a highly unusual or controversial incident, but there is no evidence of it in credible reporting, that should raise a red flag.\nYou can also look at who's posting and sharing the image. If the account often shares AI-generated material or appears to have no connection to the event itself, it's wise to treat the image with caution.\nFind the image source\nIf you're unsure whether an image is real or generated by AI, try to find its source.\nOne way to do this is by conducting a so-called reverse image search. Instead of searching with keywords, you can upload an image to tools like Google Imagesor TinEye.\nThese tools can help answer several questions:\n- When was the image first uploaded?\n- On which platforms is the image being shared?\n- What are users saying about it in comments and posts?\n- Have any reputable fact-checkers already verified the image?\nOf course, some AI-generated images may be brand new and therefore produce few or no results.\nBut manipulated images are often based on older, authentic pictures. In those cases, a reverse search can reveal the original image and provide important context.\nIf you find that an image has already been fact-checked, one way to assess the credibility of the article or video is to see whether the organization is certified by the International Fact Checking Network (IFCN).\nThe IFCN assesses fact-checkers against a set of professional standards, which include being as fair and transparent as possible, being non-partisan, and committing to open and honest corrections.\nYou can find a list of IFCN-certified outlets here, including DW Fact check.\nThe IFCN should not be confused with the Global Fact-Checking Network (GFCN), a Russian platform with close ties to the Kremlin.\nUse AI tools with caution\nAI detectors can help assess whether an image was created or modified using AI. They search for invisible watermarks and other digital traces associated with AI-generated content.\nThere are a few different types.\nTech companies such as Google and OpenAI, the creator of ChatGPT, have developed systems that identify whether their own AI models were used to create or modify an image.\nGoogle's SynthID tool can be accessed here, and hereis the link to OpenAI's verification tool.\nOther tools, such as Image Whisperer, AI or Not, and Hive Moderation, estimate the likelihood that an image was manipulated using AI.\nAnd then AI chatbots, such as X's Grok, can provide answers to user questions on whether images are real or fake.\nIn the Hitler lookalike example, OpenAI identified a digital watermark, which confirmed it was manipulated using the AI company's tools.\nOne important word of caution: AI detectors often make mistakes.\nTheir findings should not be treated as definitive proof, but rather as one piece of the verification puzzle.\nIf possible, use more than one tool and cross-check the results to reduce the risk of drawing the wrong conclusions.\nWhen in doubt, don’t share\nEven after following all these steps, you may still be unsure whether an image is real or AI generated.\nAs AI technology continues to improve, distinguishing between authentic and AI-generated images is becoming increasingly difficult.\nIf you cannot verify an image with confidence, our recommendation is simple: don't share it, especially on social media.\nInstead, consider warning friends and family members privately that the image's authenticity has not been confirmed and that it should be treated with caution.\nAnd if you're still unsure, you can always send the image to us at factchecking@dw.com. We may be able to help verify its authenticity.\nThis article was first published in 2023 and has been revised to incorporate new developments and trends. Joscha Weber and Kathrin Wesolowski contributed to that report. Edited by Josh Axelrod.\nThis article is part of a DW Fact check series on digital literacy. Other articles include:\n- How do I spot manipulated images?\n- How do I spot audio deepfakes?\n- How do I spot propaganda?\n- How do I spot fake social media accounts, bots and trolls?\nAnd here you can read more about how DW fact-checks fake news.",
        "content": "Fact check: How do I spot AI-generated images?\nAugust 18, 2026\nAnyone with an internet connection and access to an artificial intelligence (AI) tool can create photorealistic images within seconds, for free or for very little money, and then spread them on social networks at breakneck speed.\nThe tools used to create AI-generated images are improving rapidly, reducing the number of obvious AI error...",
