        "full_content": "Middle East: No talks or conversations with Iran, Trump says\nPublished August 18, 2026last updated August 18, 2026\nWhat you need to know\n- Trump says 'no talks or conversations' between the US and Iran are held or scheduled\n- Syria, US, Turkey condemn Israeli strikes in Idlib\n- HRW says UN peacekeepers must remain in Lebanon\nRead below for a roundup of the latest headlines on the Iran war and other developments in the Middle East region on Tuesday, August 18, 2026.\nTired of missing our real-time updates? Click here to add us as a Preferred Source on Google. Then tap the \"Star\" or \"Preferred\" to keep DW News at the top of your feed.\n6 killed in Israeli strike on Gaza, Palestinian officials say\nAn Israeli airstrike on a cafe in Gaza City on Tuesday killed six people and wounded a dozen more, Palestinian officials said, while the Israeli military said it carried out an attack targeting Hamas commanders.\nMahmoud Bassal, a spokesperson for Gaza's civil defence agency, told AFP that at least six were killed, \"including a child, and a large number were wounded, as a result of an Israeli raid on tents of displaced people in Gaza port.\"\nAn eyewitness told AFP Israeli war planes fired two missiles at the \"Sweet and Bitter\" cafe on the fishermen's port.\nThe Reuters news agency cited Palestinian medics as saying 14 others were wounded in the missile attack on the small cafe.\nThe Israeli army said it carried out a \"precise aerial strike\" against \"several Hamas commanders\" who \"planned to carry out terror attacks against [Israeli military] troops operating in the Gaza Strip\" and that \"steps were taken to mitigate harm to civilians.\"\nStrike comes as Trump pushes for Gaza peace\nThe strikes came just one day after US President Donald Trump's envoy Jared Kushner was in Israel and met with Prime Minister Benjamin Netanyahu.\nIsrael has insisted it will not withdraw from the areas it still controls in Gaza, some 60% of the Palestinian enclave, until Hamas has fully disarmed.\nHamas, which Israel, the US and other countries designate as a terrorist organization, has expressed its intention to disarm, but sticking points remain including over where the arms would then go.\nAfter the Tuesday attack, Hamas said it exemplified Israel's \"blatant disregard for all calls for a ceasefire issued by mediators and guarantor states.\"\nUAE accuses Iran of firing ballistic missiles\nThe United Arab Emirates said on Tuesday it has detected two ballistic missiles fired at its territory, for which it blamed Iran.\n\"UAE air defenses detected two ballistic missiles launched from Iran toward the country, with the first falling outside the country's territorial waters, while the second fell within the territorial waters,\" said a statement posted by the UAE Defense Ministry on X.\nEarlier on Tuesday, the Interior Ministry had warned residents against \"potential missile threats.\"\nIran did not immediately respond to the UAE's accusations.\nIsraeli military acknowledges marking detained Palestinians with numbers\nThe Israeli military on Tuesday acknowledged that soldiers carrying out a raid in the northern West Bank on Monday had marked detained Palestinians with numbers.\nIn a statement to The Times of Israel newspaper, the military said the seriousness of the conduct had been stressed to the troops involved and that \"lessons have been learned.\"\nFootage emerged on Monday showing a Palestinian man speaking to local media after being questioned by Israeli soldiers, with the number 44 written across his forehead.\nThe man said the troops had marked others who were questioned.\nThe measure has long been considered taboo in Israel, as it is reminiscent of the numbered tattoos which Germany's Nazi regime used to identify concentration and death camp inmates during the Holocaust.\nEarlier in April, footage showed Palestinian women with numbers written on their hands after they were briefly allowed to enter the Israeli military-controlled Jenin refugee camp to collect their belongings.\nIn response to the footage, the Israeli military acknowledged the incident and said \"lessons have been learned,\" echoing a similar statement to the one issued on Tuesday.\nHRW calls on UN to keep international force in Lebanon\nHuman Rights Watch has called on the United Nations to keep its international peacekeepers' force in Lebanon, whose mandate ends this year.\nThe UNIFIL in southern Lebanon has been in place since 1978 to confirm Israeli withdrawal from the Arab country. It has some 7,500 personnel from nearly 50 countries.\nDespite its presence, Lebanon has seen various wars between Israel and the pro-Iranian Hezbollah group, as well as Israeli ground invasions.\nIn August last year, the UN Security Council decided to end UNIFIL's mandate on December 31, 2026, amid growing US pressure.\n\"Pulling the plug on UNIFIL without a robust international force in place would effectively abandon millions of Lebanese civilians to an uncertain and potentially disastrous fate,\" said Louis Charbonneau, HRW's UN director said in a statement issued last week.\n\"The peacekeepers are a crucial deterrent against attacks on civilians and human rights abuses,\" he added.\nHRW argued that since the decision to end UNIFIL's mandate preceded this year's war and the subsequent Israeli invasion of southern Lebanon, the decision should be revised.\nHezbollah dragged Lebanon into the wider Middle East conflict on March 2 when it attacked Israel, in retaliation for US and Israeli attacks on Iran. This led to a wide Israeli bombing campaign and a ground invasion which has killed over 4,300 people in Lebanon.\nLebanese President Joseph Aoun had last month also called on UNIFIL to stay beyond their mandate.\nUAE warns residents of 'potential missile threats'\nThe United Arab Emirates on Tuesday issued a brief phone alert for \"potential missile threats,\" in the first such alarm since July.\n\"Due to the current situation, potential missile threats, immediately seek a safe place in the closest secure building,\" the Interior Ministry said in the phone alert, which was lifted shortly after.\nThe Ministry of Defense confirmed it had detected missiles \"directed toward the country\" for the first time in months.\nAt various points in the Iran war, and especially in the early days, UAE sites were the target of Iranian missile and drone strikes, in retaliation for US and Israeli strikes on Iran.\n'Few tons of nuclear material' found in Syria, IAEA says\nRafael Grossi, the head of the United Nations International Atomic Energy Agency said on Tuesday that the nuclear watchdog has found several tons of nuclear materials at a previously undeclared site in Syria which date back to ousted President Bashar Assad.\n\"After your courageous decision to inform us that there was another place, another site where nuclear material had been stored, we were able to access this place... we are talking about a few tons of nuclear material that could be put to bad use,\" Grossi told a news conference in Damascus.\nSyria's foreign minister said the material was not dangerous and would remain in Syrian custody, subject to IAEA guarantees.\nSpeaking alongside Grossi in the press conference, Foreign Minister Asaad al-Shibani said Syria had the right to use the material for civil and peaceful purposes.\nSyria's interim government has committed to working with the IAEA to address the ousted regime's legacy of nuclear activities.\nSyria, US condemn Israeli strikes on military base\nEight Israeli airstrikes hit an unused airbase in northwestern Syria's Idlib province early on Tuesday, Syrian state media reported, in attacks widely condemned by Damascus and Washington.\nThe attacks struck the runway and storage facilities at Abu al-Duhur base, Syrian state-run broadcaster Al-Ikhbariya reported, some 70 kilometers (43.5 miles) from the Turkish border.\nTurkey is a key ally of Syria's interim government, in power since ousting former President Bashar Assad in a December 2024 lightning offensive.\nRead our full report on the Israeli strikes here.\n'No talks or conversations' with Iran, Trump says\nUS President Donald Trump stressed on Tuesday that no talks or conversations between Washington and Tehran were being held or were scheduled.\n\"The Naval Blockade remains in full force and effect,\" Trump wrote on his Truth Social platform, claiming that the strategic Strait of Hormuz is \"open and operating,\" in contradiction to Iranian reports.\n\"All water mines have been removed or detonated,\" Trump said of the strait, through which a fifth of the world's oil transited before the war began in late February.\nTrump's latest statement came after a 60-day negotiating period between the US and Iran ended with no clear resolution, causing stock markets to largely fall and interest rates to spike on Tuesday.\nEarlier on Tuesday, the British military's UK Maritime Trade Operations center reported that a projectile hit a ship as it sailed out of the Strait of Hormuz.\nWelcome to our coverage\nThank you for joining us as we bring you the latest on the situation in the Middle East, where US President Donald Trump has once again dismissed any potential breakthrough in negotiations with Iran, saying no talks or conversations are currently going on.\nThis comes a day after his son-in-law and envoy Jared Kushner was in Israel, discussing with Prime Minister Benjamin Netanyahu ways to advance Trump's peace plan in Gaza.\nOn Tuesday, Israel struck a military airbase in Syria, drawing condemnation from Washington.\nElsewhere in the region, Human Rights Watch is warning of the planned withdrawal of UN peacekeeping troops from Lebanon after their mandate expires this year.",
        "content": "Middle East: No talks or conversations with Iran, Trump says\nPublished August 18, 2026last updated August 18, 2026\nWhat you need to know\n- Trump says 'no talks or conversations' between the US and Iran are held or scheduled\n- Syria, US, Turkey condemn Israeli strikes in Idlib\n- HRW says UN peacekeepers must remain in Lebanon\nRead below for a roundup of the latest headlines on the Iran war and othe...",
