        "full_content": "The Ozempic Era Has Its Own Netflix Dating Show, and It’s Called ‘Weight to Date’\nNetflix may have discovered that love isn’t blind after all.\nThe streaming service is now casting a new dating series that follows singles after major weight loss, tracking how a changed body reshapes dating, identity and the search for love. The casting call asks a single question: “Are you ready to find love after a significant body change?” It’s seeking singles over 18 who have lost a significant amount of weight.\nThe show comes from The Intellectual Property Corporation, with executive producers Eli Holzman, Aaron Saidman and Jeanne Begley. It’s described as a docuseries rather than a high-concept format, closer in tone to “Love on the Spectrum” than “Love Is Blind.” That framing suggests a slower, more observational approach — following real people through the awkwardness and vulnerability of dating rather than staging elaborate pods or twists.\nNetflix’s press release didn’t mention GLP-1 drugs, though the surge in extreme weight loss stories tracks with the spread of Ozempic, Wegovy and similar medications. As those drugs have moved from niche prescriptions to cultural shorthand, they’ve brought a wave of before-and-after narratives into the mainstream — and reality television has a long history of chasing whatever conversation the culture is already having.\nThe series joins an unscripted dating slate that already includes “Age of Attraction,” “Temptation Island” and “Too Hot to Handle.” For Netflix, another entry in the genre is a low-risk bet on a format that reliably delivers watercooler moments, even as the streamer keeps searching for the next breakout hit to rival “Love Is Blind.”\nLike what you read? Follow Thought Catalog on Facebook, and check out more on our website.",
