        "image": "https://images.ctfassets.net/lzny33ho1g45/2FNXXjXn4shEqE4Hrfdmgl/20548b28bf956df6cde5d454dcb06490/Hero__App_tips.jpg",
        "full_content": "After setting up the Conversions API for LinkedIn Ads, most marketing teams do the same thing. They monitor it for a while to ensure it’s working as expected, adjust as needed, and move on to the next priority. After all, there's no shortage of work to do.\nBut then something breaks, and it catches them by surprise.\nDon't fall into the set-it-and-forget it trap. Here's a quick framework you can use to help audit and troubleshoot your conversion tracking workflows when something breaks—and help you prevent problems from recurring in the future.\nTable of contents\n- Why measurement breaks without anyone noticing\n- The audit: Three areas, 13 questions\n- How to prioritize what you fix\n- What changes after the audit\nWhy measurement breaks without anyone noticing\nB2B marketing is dynamic. New campaigns go live, your marketing stack evolves, and your CRM fields change on a monthly, if not daily, basis. It might sound cliche, but it's true—the constant in marketing is change.\nNone of these changes announces itself and says \"Hi, I’m your new tracking problem!\" Everything still looks like it's working, until there's a noticeable gap between what's reported in LinkedIn Campaign Manager and what your CRM says. And by then, it's too late.\nBut before you freak out, know that it happens to most of us and it's relatively easy to fix. We've put together a quick audit framework you can run through to diagnose and fix any problems. You can also repeat it periodically to prevent future issues.\nIf you're still in the process of setting up LinkedIn’s CAPI with Zapier, check out our help article on how to configure a reliable integration between LinkedIn and your other apps.\nWhat a healthy setup looks like\nBefore working through the audit steps below, take a moment to revisit what a reliable tracking setup looks like:\n- Every meaningful funnel event is captured—not just form fills but other funnel milestones too.\n- Every captured event gets sent to LinkedIn with accurate data.\n- There's a documented process for maintaining both of those conditions.\nThat last one is probably why you're reading this article to begin with, but the audit below covers all three.\nThe audit: Three areas, 13 questions\nBefore you get overwhelmed, know that the goal isn't to fix everything at once. You should focus instead on establishing a clear baseline for what's working, what isn't, and what's at risk across your CRM, LinkedIn's ad platform, and your team. That way, you can prioritize the highest-impact solutions and assign ownership.\nWe suggest working through the three areas in order, as each one builds on the last.\nArea 1: Your CRM\nStart with your CRM because everything else depends on it. If an event isn't correctly recorded in your CRM, it doesn't matter how well the rest of the integration is configured because LinkedIn will never receive it.\nHere are five questions to ask when auditing the CRM part of your connected conversions workflow:\n- Are the funnel events you want to send to LinkedIn recorded in your CRM consistently, not just sometimes? If not, think about adding automation to other sales and marketing workflows so you're less reliant on humans remembering to update data.\n- Are the CRM fields that feed your automated workflow current? Have any been renamed, restructured, or removed since you originally connected your CRM to LinkedIn?\n- Is every active channel, form, and campaign accounted for in your Zapier workflow, or are some channels falling outside what your existing automations cover?\n- Are the funnel milestones that matter to your business, like qualified meetings, opportunities created, and closed-won deals, triggering your CAPI workflow? Or do some of those moments only exist in the silo of your CRM?\n- Finally: Has anyone tested the trigger step of your Zap recently, or have you noticed any errors in your Zap history? Periodically testing your CRM-CAPI connection is a great way to catch problems before they meaningfully affect your reporting.\nIf the answer to any of these questions is \"No\" or \"I'm not sure,\" that's an indicator that you're likely not sharing the right funnel events back to LinkedIn.\nArea 2: LinkedIn\nThis step is where you check whether funnel events from your CRM are actually making it back to LinkedIn in a usable format. Here are a few questions to ask:\n- Pick a funnel event. Does LinkedIn's reported volume for the past 30 days roughly match what your CRM shows for the same event in that period?\n- Are the event names appearing in LinkedIn campaign reporting consistent with how your team actually talks about your funnel, or are you struggling to connect the dots? Using accurate and consistent terms helps make sure everyone is on the same page about which conversion events you're optimizing for and what part of the funnel they're in. It also makes it easier to map fields from your CRM to LinkedIn's Campaign Manager. If you always have to remember that it's called a \"demo\" in your CRM but a \"consultation\" in LinkedIn, it's much easier to make a mistake when adjusting your workflow or analyzing performance data.\n- Are conversion events attributed to the correct campaigns?\nKeep an eye out for discrepancies between what your CRM says and what LinkedIn shows. Any meaningful gap points to an issue with your automated workflow, like a formatting problem.\nArea 3: Your team\nWho owns your LinkedIn reporting workflows, and what happens when something changes?\nMany teams don't know the answer to these questions, and it's the reason they deal with the same measurement problems over and over. It's not always a silly oversight, either: Teams change. We've all been in the situation where someone takes on a new role or responsibilities shift, and tasks like checking up on your conversion workflows get lost in the shuffle.\nHere are a few questions to help clarify accountability:\n- Who owns our CRM-to-LinkedIn connection? Someone in Demand Gen, Marketing Ops, or RevOps should be responsible for maintaining and updating it as your marketing efforts evolve.\n- Is there a defined process for comparing LinkedIn conversion volume against CRM records month over month?\n- When a CRM workflow, field, or lead source changes, is the automation explicitly included in the change management process?\n- Is there documentation of how our CRM-CAPI connection is set up?\n- Finally: If the person who originally configured the automation left the team tomorrow, would someone else be able to step in and maintain it?\nHow to prioritize what you fix\nThis audit will probably expose a few different issues, and that's okay. While every business's marketing stack, priorities, and resources are different, here's a simple method for knowing what to fix first.\nFix CRM gaps first\nIf a funnel event isn't being recorded at the source, nothing downstream can compensate for it. A broken CRM trigger or a missing lead source is the highest priority issue to fix, because it's the one with the biggest consequences.\nTackle mapping issues second\nEvents that fire but carry the wrong or empty data fields create a specific kind of problem. LinkedIn receives them, counts them, and learns from them—but what it's learning from is inaccurate. That's harder to recover from than a gap in coverage, because it means LinkedIn has been optimizing campaigns based on bad inputs, rather than no inputs.\nFix ownership last (but please don't skip it!)\nOnce you've addressed the more technical issues, take a step back and reflect on the process that caused them to happen or to go unnoticed.\nThen, work cross-functionally to assign a named owner and establish a regular review cadence. That's what will turn your one-time audit into ongoing reliability.\nStill need to prioritize within each category? Start with the fix that has the most downstream impact. For example, the mapping issue that's most likely affecting attribution for your highest-spend campaigns is the first mapping issue you should fix.\nThe one-page audit for founders\nIf you're doing your own marketing or working with a small team, the audit above is more than you need. Here's the same diagnostic designed to be done solo:\n- Am I actually recording all the funnel events that matter to my business—not just form fills, but demos booked, proposals sent, and deals closed—in my CRM?\n- Are those events shared back to LinkedIn?\n- Have I changed anything in my CRM, like new fields, since I last checked on the integration?\n- Are the events showing up in LinkedIn labeled in a way that matches how I think about my funnel? Using consistent, descriptive terms makes it easier to analyze performance and map fields across your CRM and LinkedIn.\n- If I had to explain my current tracking setup to someone I hired, could I?\nFor any question where the honest answer is \"I'm not sure,\" that's your starting point.\nWhat changes after the audit\nFor leaders, the audit gives you a shared baseline that makes cross-functional accountability possible. It's hard to have a productive conversation about measurement ownership when everyone has different assumptions about what's being tracked and how.\nKnowing how your CRM-LinkedIn connection works changes how much you trust report data, how you evaluate campaigns, and how much confidence you bring to decisions about where to put your next dollar. That last area is where you'll see the biggest difference, because you can make ad spend decisions faster.\nMost measurement problems aren't mysterious, and they're almost always fixable. You just have to ask the right questions.\nReady to close the loop? Get started with Zapier and LinkedIn’s CAPI today—it's free.",
